  "raw": {
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15698?c=usask": {
      "title": "Casual Research Assistant, College of Pharmacy and Nutrition",
      "description": "Casual Research Assistant, College of Pharmacy and Nutrition | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Casual Research Assistant, College of Pharmacy and Nutrition Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15698 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/16/2026 Back to Search Casual Research Assistant, College of Pharmacy and Nutrition In the appointment of Research Assistant, your general duties and responsibilities will be: Provide statistical and methodological expertise to the Lab’s ongoing projects to support the design, implementation, analysis, and interpretation of health and nutrition research studies. The position will develop and apply statistical methods using Python, SAS, and R; adapt and maintain existing code; contribute to study design; and prepare statistical analysis plans and research outputs. Your general location of work will be hybrid and you will report to Dr. Hassan Vatanparast. Required Number of Hours: 20 hours (casual employment, depending on Vatanparast Nutritional Lab’s priorities. Qualifications: Formal education from the University of Saskatchewan or another recognized post-secondary institution in a relevant field of study, such as Biostatistics, Epidemiology, Public Health, Health Sciences, Data Science, or a related discipline. Candidates must have relevant experience in statistical methods in health research. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Design and conduct statistical analyses for health, nutrition, and related research studies using Python, SAS, and R. Develop, adapt, document, and maintain reproducible analysis code, including code based on existing databases and codebases. Contribute statistical expertise to study design, including sampling, variable definition, power or sample-size considerations, analytic approaches, and interpretation of results. Prepare, review, and implement statistical analysis plans. Analyze complex survey data using survey weights and appropriate variance-estimation methods, including bootstrap replicate weights. Work with nutritional and population-health datasets, including data cleaning, management, linkage, and quality assessment. Collaborate with researchers and team members to identify appropriate statistical methods and address methodological challenges. Interpret and communicate statistical findings clearly in reports, manuscripts, presentations, grant applications, and other research products. Ensure that analyses are accurate, reproducible, well documented, and aligned with study protocols and research objectives. Support the development of publications, technical reports, knowledge-translation materials, and presentations. Provide statistical consultation, mentorship, and technical guidance to trainees or research staff, as appropriate. Stay current with relevant statistical methods and contribute to improving the laboratory’s analytic practices and infrastructure. To Apply : Send your application materials to: monidipa.saha@usask.ca. Inquiries regarding this position can be directed to Monidipa Saha, monidipa.saha@usask.ca Where a graduate student is appointed to this position, it will be in-scope of PSAC. Department: College of Pharmacy & Nutrition Status: Casual Employment Group: Graduate Students - PSAC Rate of Pay: Current rates of pay Posted Date: 10/16/2026 Closing Date: 10/23/2026 Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15694?c=usask": {
      "title": "Online Instructor, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy",
      "description": "Online Instructor, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Online Instructor, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15694 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/16/2026 Back to Search Online Instructor, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy The Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy (JSGS) invites applications for an Online Instructor to teach the following course during the Winter term in the 2026-27 Regular session. Course Number, Section, and Name: JSGS 882 Strategic Management Term and Course Dates: January 6, 2027 - April 8, 2027, with the exam period April 10-30, 2027. CRN: Delivery Mode: This course will be offered online asynchronous Course Schedule: Asynchronous Expected Enrollment Limit: 30-35 Qualifications: Master’s degree in public administration, public policy or a relatable field of study. The appointment period includes additional days in recognition of preparation and marking time required. Course may be cancelled due to low enrolment. Application Process Submit as a single PDF file your curriculum vitae, a cover letter, your teaching philosophy, and evidence of teaching effectiveness no later than October 30, 2026, to jsgs‑hr@usask.ca . The subject line must indicate the course name for which you are applying. If you are currently employed by the University of Saskatchewan in a full-time non-teaching position, discuss your application with your people leader to ensure the appointment is suitable relative to your current position. Department: School of Public Policy Status: Term Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 0.32 Salary: Online Facilitator follows a different compensation structure than the ASPA\nsalary ranges: Class Size               \n        Stipend 1-50 Students                $\n8,399.