    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15688?c=usask": {
      "title": "Academic Data and Accreditation Specialist, College of Engineering",
      "description": "Academic Data and Accreditation Specialist, College of Engineering | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Academic Data and Accreditation Specialist, College of Engineering Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15688 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Academic Data and Accreditation Specialist, College of Engineering Primary Purpose: Academic Data and Accreditation Specialist, reports to the Programs and Projects Officer in Engineering and provides administrative and data management support. The focus of this position is to assist in administrative tasks and document preparation to retain accreditation for the College of Engineering programs. Work on the accreditation project will include working with colleges, departments and faculty to gather necessary course information, assessment and program improvement data. The Academic Data and Accreditation Specialist will work with the Program Accreditation Leads from the eight programs and the first-year program in the College of Engineering, as well as with faculty from other colleges that offer courses for Engineering students. Engagement with relevant stakeholders including students, alumni and industry will be required. This position will work with the Programs and Projects Officer to interpret and organize the information as necessary before submission to the accrediting body. Nature of Work: A high degree of autonomy and initiative is expected and required within the reporting structure.  This position is expected to be self-motivated and work productively with very little supervision. The responsibilities of this position are diverse and complex, requiring a high capacity for problem solving, independence, sound judgment, and attention to detail. It exercises professional academic judgment and in-depth analysis in interpreting academic policy and procedures, student programs and other academic issues and in analyzing, defining problems and issues, and finding solutions. It communicates complex and specialized information to a variety of audiences. The position requires the development and maintenance of professional relationships and teamwork that support exceptional collaboration and problem solving, working closely and collaboratively with a broad range of personnel. It therefore entails significant interaction with faculty, and academic leaders, individually and in groups, serving as a resource and providing expert counsel and analysis to all. It also requires significant collaboration and persuasive powers to facilitate resolutions. Working conditions require innovation and flexibility and require the ability to work under pressure in a fast-paced environment where competing deadlines and multi-tasking is the norm. The standard for the quality of work and timely completion of each project is high. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Assist the Programs and Projects Officer in: Research of best and comparative practices of graduate attribute assessment activities across engineering schools in Canada; Distribution and follow up of graduate assessment data collection requests for all engineering undergraduate programs; Receiving of graduate attribute assessment data from faculty, staff, and students across the institution. Managing file storage in the Accreditation Repository and archiving all files into prescribed locations. Uploading program, faculty and course information into Tandem, the CEAB data management system; Review of historical graduate attribute assessment data collection strategies and reformat files into preferred formats; Scheduling and recording of accreditation-related activities, meetings, workshops, and events; Preparing and formatting draft correspondence and documentation, as required; Preparing and circulating quarterly accreditation updates to relevant stakeholders and the College of Engineering leadership team; Supporting the implementation of a project communications plan, as required; Maintaining the Accreditation Repository on a regular basis and ensuring the Accreditation Repository is ready for the Accreditation Visit in 2027; Preparing and formatting draft correspondence and documentation related to the Accreditation Visit, as required: Scheduling and recording of Accreditation Visit-related activities, presentations, meetings, tours, and stakeholder interviews; Other project-related activities, as assigned. Qualifications Education: An undergraduate degree from a recognized Canadian institution is required; a graduate degree in relevant to the job profile will be considered an asset. Experience: Two to four years of postgraduate work experience as well as experience working with university personnel from various departments and colleges. A well-developed understanding of the undergraduate programs and academic structures in Engineering is required. An equivalent combination of academic experience and preparation may be considered. Skills: Demonstrated initiative, creativity, innovative thinking and analytical capability. Demonstrated proficiency with computer systems and computer-based applications including Microsoft programs, specifically proficiency with Excel, data management software. Ability to work independently, collaboratively or as part of a team, excellent written and oral communication skills, proven, polished presentation skills, interpersonal and organizational skills, and strong leadership, motivation and team-player skills.  Proven flexibility and success in meeting constantly changing priorities and deadlines. Department: College of Engineering Status: Term 1 year with the possibility of extension Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 10/14/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15686?c=usask": {
      "title": "Sessional Lecturer, Educational Psychology and Special Education",
      "description": "Sessional Lecturer, Educational Psychology and Special Education | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Sessional Lecturer, Educational Psychology and Special Education Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15686 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Sessional Lecturer, Educational Psychology and Special Education The Department of Educational Psychology and Special Education invites applications for a Sessional Lecturer to teach the following courses during the following session(s): 2026-2027 Regular Session Course Number, Section, and Name: EPSE 202 (01) Psychological Foundations of Teaching and Learning Term and Course Dates: Term Two (January to April) CRN: 27685 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Tuesday, Thursday, 8:30am – 9:50am Expected Enrollment Limit: 114 Location: Saskatoon Qualifications: A M.Ed. degree (or equivalent graduate degree) and relevant teaching or professional experience, or significant progress on a M.Ed. degree or equivalent. Course Number, Section, and Name: EPSE 202 (G84) Psychological Foundations of Teaching and Learning Term and Course Dates: Term Two (January to April) CRN: 28259 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Tuesday 9:00 AM - 11:50 AM Expected Enrollment Limit: 30 Location: Prince Albert Qualifications: A M.Ed. degree (or equivalent graduate degree) and relevant teaching or professional experience, or significant progress on a M.Ed. degree or equivalent. Course Number, Section, and Name: EPSE 390 (02) Exceptional Learners Term and Course Dates: Term Two (January to April) CRN: 27912 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Wednesdays, 1:00pm – 3:50pm Expected Enrollment Limit: 40 Location: Saskatoon Qualifications: A M.Ed. degree (or equivalent graduate degree) and relevant teaching or professional experience, or significant progress on a M.Ed. degree or equivalent. Course Number, Section, and Name: EPSE 390 (04) Exceptional Learners Term and Course Dates: Term Two (January to April) CRN: 25603 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Mondays and Wednesdays, 11:30am – 12:50pm Expected Enrollment Limit: 40 Location: Saskatoon Qualifications: A M.Ed. degree (or equivalent graduate degree) and relevant teaching or professional experience, or significant progress on a M.Ed. degree or equivalent. Course Number, Section, and Name: EPSE 390 (86) Exceptional Learners Term and Course Dates: Term Two (January to April) CRN: 21523 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Thursdays, 1:00pm – 3:50pm Expected Enrollment Limit: 25 Location: Saskatoon Qualifications: A M.Ed. degree (or equivalent graduate degree) and relevant teaching or professional experience, or significant progress on a M.Ed. degree or equivalent. Course Number, Section, and Name: EPSE 390 (G80) Exceptional Learners Term and Course Dates: Term Two (January to April) CRN: 24886 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Tuesdays, 1:00pm – 3:50pm Expected Enrollment Limit: 25 Location: Saskatoon Qualifications: A M.Ed. degree (or equivalent graduate degree) and relevant teaching or professional experience, or significant progress on a M.Ed. degree or equivalent. A valid driver’s license is required for any off-campus courses. Appointment periods include additional days in recognition of preparation and marking time required. Courses may be cancelled due to low enrolment. Application Process : Complete the CUPE 3287 Employment Application Form , specify the position(s) for which you are applying, and send the application along with your curriculum vitae to isabel.bacalao@usask.ca If you are currently employed by the University of Saskatchewan in a full-time non-teaching position, discuss your application with your people leader to ensure the appointment is suitable relative to your current position. Department: Educational Psychology\nand Special Education Status: Term Employment Group: CUPE 3287 Academic Calendar: Course Dates and Academic Calendar Stipend: CUPE 3287 Stipends Posted Date: 10/14/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 Number of Openings: 6 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
