    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15682?c=usask": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant, Department of Pathology",
      "description": "Executive Assistant, Department of Pathology | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Executive Assistant, Department of Pathology Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15682 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Executive Assistant, Department of Pathology Summary: The Provincial Department of Pathology is an academic and clinical department within the College of Medicine, University of Saskatchewan, and the Saskatchewan Health Authority. As such, the Executive Assistant to the Provincial Head will provide support and build relationships within the context of large and complex organizations, including the University of Saskatchewan and the Saskatchewan Health Authority, and serves as a guide and resource to others who must navigate both environments. Primary Purpose: This position will provide confidential and high-level support to the Provincial Head, Pathology, within the Provincial Department of Pathology. The position will be instrumental in providing administrative support to allow the Provincial Department senior leadership to function effectively, aiding in all aspects of program management, in keeping with the Department’s strategic priorities. Nature of Work: Under the direction of the Provincial Head while reporting to the Manager, Department of Pathology, this position interacts with a diverse group of individuals including faculty, students, staff, clinical partners and other stakeholders. The incumbent will be required to work in a complex and ever-changing environment; adapt to shifting priorities and timelines, and frequent interruptions. Establishing and maintaining relationships that support collaboration and problem-solving, and the ability to exercise professional judgment to make recommendations and decisions, with confidence, regarding issues or special assignments, is essential for success in this complex position. Considerable discretion is a requirement as this position is privy to, and handles, highly sensitive and at times confidential information. The position is responsible for ensuring day-to-day work is completed accurately and information provided to others, both on and off campus, is correct. The successful candidate will contribute to a positive, diverse and inclusive work and learning environment by actively engaging in mentoring, self-awareness learning opportunities, relationship building, and communication. Accountabilities: Administrative Responsibilities Provides confidential, comprehensive, effective and efficient administrative support to the Provincial Head, Pathology including but not limited to: Strategically manage the Provincial Head’s calendar to ensure a balance between meetings and office/work time to address priority tasks Book meetings, venues, and information technology supports including WebEx, Zoom, Microsoft Teams and other platforms Prepare meeting agendas, accurately record minutes, track action items and timelines and ensure action items are completed by the assigned individuals Ensure priorities and deadlines are met, and that correspondence is handled accurately, efficiently, professionally and confidentially. Arrange travel logistics, ensuring necessary approvals and expense reports are initiated. Prepare, gather, save/file and relay confidential information and material on a regular basis. Oversee Department reports and website updates; initiates, collates, drafts, and finalizes information Initiates and keeps records of provincial Faculty appointments, renewals, evaluations, promotions and tenures Organizes and facilitates events, arranging all logistics while complying with university guidelines Assists with special projects as needed Draft letters, presentations, and analyze data in support of the Provincial Head. Works closely with both CoM and SHA to support strategic planning Initiates and oversees continuous improvement processes in support of strategic planning and efficient use of human and financial resources. Oversees Administrative Policies Oversees all central administrative responsibilities such as filing, facilities (Office assignment/furniture), staff onboarding needs, and equipment and logistics issues Human Resource Responsibilities Supervision of Clerical Assistant(s) tasked with Provincial Department Administrative support Collaborate with the Manager to facilitate hiring, on-boarding and off-boarding of all department employees Assist the Provincial Head with physician and faculty recruitment Preparation of leadership contracts, renewals and payments Financial Responsibilities Manages purchase orders, invoices, cheque requisitions, P-card procurement, journal vouchers, honorariums, etc. and required financial transactions for the provincial Department Provides effective administration of the Department’s financial resources Manages capital equipment inventory and ensures purchasing guidelines are adhered to Prepares material needed to assist the Provincial Head with budget submissions related to the operations of the Department Manages Faculty Expense of Practice funding, budgets and expenses. Collaborates with the Manager to ensure effective management of Department's operating budget and other financial accounts Qualifications Education: Completion of an undergraduate university degree is required. Successful completion of a business or administrative program or equivalent would be preferred. Completion of a Project Management course would be considered an asset. A combination of education and experience will be considered. Experience: A minimum of five years’ experience providing confidential administrative support to senior level administrators within a fast-paced and highly dynamic office environment, preferably in an academic healthcare/university setting, is required. Experience working and collaborating in diverse and inclusive settings is required. Experience supervising others would be considered an asset. Knowledge of the University of Saskatchewan and Saskatchewan Health Authority regulations and procedures would be an asset. Experience with UniFi, Banner, FAST, Concur and Crystal Reports would be an asset. Preference will be given to candidates with previous supervisor experience. Skills: Proficiency in Microsoft Office programs and virtual meeting platforms; Strong organization skills; Ability to work independently, as well as part of a team is essential; Effective use of judgment, confidentiality and discretion when handling sensitive issues; Effective verbal and written communication skills; Flexible and open to diverse views and ways of doing; Experienced ability to support strategic goals and recognize how short term tasks align with longer term goals. Preference to those with the ability to manage the department’s online social media presence. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Department: Pathology Supervisor Job Title: Manager, Department of Pathology Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 10/14/2026 Closing Date: 10/28/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15681?c=usask": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant, Electrical and Computer Engineering",
      "description": "Teaching Assistant, Electrical and Computer Engineering | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Teaching Assistant, Electrical and Computer Engineering Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15681 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Teaching Assistant, Electrical and Computer Engineering In the appointment of Teaching Assistant, your general duties and responsibilities will be assigned by the Instructor(s) and may include one or more of the following: Marking assignments; Marking laboratory logbooks and/or reports; Marking quizzes and/or exams; Holding tutorials and/or staffing a Help Desk for students; Assisting with general administration of the course such as email communications with students regarding assignments, and recording grades Demonstrating and/or assisting with the demonstration of laboratories; Monitoring safety in the laboratory in cooperation with the instructor(s); Invigilating examinations; Collecting information for continuous program improvement; Other duties related to the course deemed appropriate by the Instructor(s) Your general location of work will be the College of Engineering and you will report to the instructor(s) responsible for the course. Required Number of Hours : Varies Duration of Employment : 2026-27 Regular Course Name, Abbreviation and Number: Various Start and End Dates: January 1 to April 30 (with some variation depending on the specific assignment) Qualifications (Skills and Abilities) Graduate student in good standing enrolled in the College of Engineering. English language proficiency, both written and oral is required. Preference will be given to individuals with previous experience as a Teaching Assistant or instructor. PSAC hiring guidelines will be adhered to. These positions are associated with the following courses: EE216 Probability and Numerical Methods EE221 Analog Electronics EE368 Mobile Robotics EE322 Microwave and RF Circuits EE241 Introduction to Electric Power Systems EE271 Materials and Heat Transport in Electrical Engineering EE322 Microwave and RF Circuits EE342 Transmission of Electrical Energy EE365 Algorithms and Circuits with Finite Precision Arithmetics EE382 Control Systems EE448 Renewable Energy and Power Systems EE465 Design of a DSP System EE466 Image Processing EE 464 Design of an Autonomous Mobile Robotics System EE468 Design of Computer Vision System EE472 Optoelectronics and Photonics EE 495 Senior Design Project CME332 Real Time Computing CME334 Network Architecture and Protocols CME466 Design of an Advanced Digital System CME498 Electronic Design Automation To Apply : Interested applicants must complete the Electrical and Computer Engineering Application which can be found at: https://www.surveymonkey.ca/r/KK6PXS5 . Submit application no later than November 13th, 2026. Email a resume to ta.recruitment@usask.ca ; please include ECE Teaching Assistant Application 202701 in the subject line. Applicants must submit a resume and complete the survey by the closing date in order to be considered as a qualified candidate. Where a graduate student is appointed to this position, it will be in scope of PSAC. Department: Electrical and Computer Engineering Status: Term Employment Group: Graduate Students - PSAC Rate of Pay: Current rates of pay Posted Date: 10/14/2026 Closing Date: 11/14/2026 Number of Openings: 20 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15656?c=usask": {
      "title": "Clerical Assistant, Career Services",
      "description": "Clerical Assistant, Career Services | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Clerical Assistant, Career Services Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15656 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Clerical Assistant, Career Services Career Services empowers students to achieve career success by leading, guiding, and partnering to advance career education at the University of Saskatchewan. Working in partnership with employers, our community and our USask colleagues, we facilitate students' access to opportunities, creation of connections, and preparation for and management of their careers. We also play a key role in supporting students in developing competencies (knowledges, skills, attributes) within their academic programs, and through experiential and work integrated learning opportunities. Primary Purpose: The Clerical Assistant will provide clerical support within Career Services and act as the first point of contact for students, employers, faculty and staff, providing quality customer service. Nature of Work: Reporting to the Employer and Community Engagement Coordinator, the Clerical Assistant will assist in the operations of Career Services by working cooperatively with other staff to ensure seamless customer service and efficiency with minimal supervision. The incumbent will be expected to have strong customer service experience as well as time management, critical thinking, organization and communication skills. They must comply with all relevant policies and procedures; complete assignments and support team efforts and projects as directed; and participate as an active and supportive member of Career Services demonstrating initiative and self-motivation where appropriate. This work is fast paced, moderately complex, with a high volume, excellent problem-solving skills and the ability to exercise good judgment critical. The incumbent must understand the impact of their work on the University context and ensure the quality of their work meets the needs and standards of the office. No travel, shift or weekend hours required. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Serves as Career Services’ front reception and first point of contact, greeting visitors and providing customer service. Responds to and redirects in-person, telephone, and email inquiries and triages inquiries, issues and concerns. Follows established guidelines to process careerlink.usask.ca job postings and approve employer profiles with good judgment. Receives and processes debit and credit card payments. Performs daily opening and closing duties. Manage office Outlook Calendars, and Schedule office wide meetings. Book rooms for various on-campus events for employers and community partners. Entering data into tracking documents. Providing clerical assistance and general support for events including the coordination of room bookings for unit events. Participating in professional development opportunities and team building activities. Maintains the physical office space including liaising with building and cleaning staff, coordinating repairs, and supporting the Director in establishing and implementing plans for short-term and long-term maintenance and upkeep of physical spaces. Manages inventory for unit including supplies, resources, furnishings, and equipment. Anticipates needs of the staff and unit and plans accordingly. Advises System Administrator on required purchases. Assists with employer registrations and waitlists for events. Assisting with administrative duties for Community Engaged Learning Projects. Overseeing & leading the office bulletin board renewals and updates. Document minutes during weekly Team Meetings and manage Team Meeting OneNote. Supporting office priorities and special events. Other duties as assigned. Education: Completion of Grade 12 is required. Completion of a relevant certificate from a recognized post-secondary institution is required . Experience: A combination of customer service and administration experience is required. Experience in an educational setting will be considered an asset. Skills: Professionalism and dedication to providing excellent customer service. Proven verbal and written communication skills with a strong attention to detail, and ability to create and edit content. Strong computer skills with proficiency in Microsoft Office including Teams, Word, Excel, Outlook and OneNote. Effective organization and time management skills, and demonstrated attention to detail. Ability to assess priorities and work in a self-directed manner, managing multiple tasks with competing timelines. Must be able to deal positively and effectively with stressful situations, and engage in constructive, collaborative relationships with cultural sensitivity. Demonstrated strong critical thinking skills and ability to exercise good judgment when problem solving. Ability to show initiative and proficiency in all facets of clerical work. Department: Career Services Status: Term 12 months with the possibility of extension Employment Group: CUPE 1975 Shift: Mon-Fri, 8:30-4:30 pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $22.74 - 27.69 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Operational Services Salary Phase/Band: Phase 3 Posted Date: 10/7/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15482?c=usask": {
      "title": "Clinical Associate, Food Animal Field Service, Large Animal Clinical Sciences",
      "description": "Clinical Associate, Food Animal Field Service, Large Animal Clinical Sciences | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Clinical Associate, Food Animal Field Service, Large Animal Clinical Sciences Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15482 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Clinical Associate, Food Animal Field Service, Large Animal Clinical Sciences Summary: The Food Animal Field Service Veterinarian will work within a collaborative and supportive team, providing high-quality care to client-owned food-producing animals in their home environment. The role combines clinical excellence, teaching the next generation of veterinarians, and an outstanding quality of life based on a 37.5-hour work week. A key advantage of this position is its predictable and sustainable work schedule, including a shared on-call rotation. The current service consists of three veterinarians and is expected to grow to five. Duties and Responsibilities: Provides high-quality care to client-owned food-producing animals in their home environment, including after regular business hours. Utilizes and assimilates available clinical information to make decisions regarding patient care. Deals with emerging and unpredictable events involving large animals. Participates in the teaching and mentoring of veterinary undergraduate students. Ensures the maintenance and growth of the Food Animal Field Service and reproductive services caseload of the VMC. Assists in providing additional teaching duties as assigned. Qualifications: A Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) degree or equivalent. Licenced, or eligible for licensure, with the Saskatchewan Veterinary Medical Association. An advanced degree, appropriate specialty board certification, or both, are preferred. Experience in mixed or food animal practice is considered desirable. Possession of a valid Saskatchewan driver’s licence (or eligibility to obtain one), an acceptable driving record as determined by the University of Saskatchewan, and the willingness and ability to travel throughout the assigned region. Experience in clinical instruction and mentorship are assets. Strong communication and interpersonal abilities. Strong organizational and time-management skills. High attention to detail and accuracy. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively within a team. The successful candidate will also receive a clinical supplement of $300 per pay period ($7,200 annually) in recognition of on-call and after-hours clinical responsibilities. A comprehensive benefit package including pension, extended health and dental, life and disability insurance, flexible spending allowance, and generous vacation are provided. An overview of the ASPA benefit plan can be found at: https://careers.usask.ca/benefits.php Department: Large Animal Clinical Sciences Status: Term Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 0.6 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $87,266.00 - 145,353.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 3 Posted Date: 10/14/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The successful applicant will be required to provide the following current verification(s) where 'Yes' is indicated below. Further information is available at: https://careers.usask.ca/tips-for-applying.php Criminal Record Check: Not Applicable Driver's License and Abstract Check: Yes Education/Credential Verification: Yes Vulnerable Sector Check: Not Applicable The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15687?c=usask": {
      "title": "Advancement Coordinator, Edwards School of Business",
      "description": "Advancement Coordinator, Edwards School of Business | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Advancement Coordinator, Edwards School of Business Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15687 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Advancement Coordinator, Edwards School of Business Summary: The advancement coordinator supports donor development, alumni engagement, and advancement initiatives for the college. This role focuses on sustaining relationships with donors and alumni through stewardship activities, communications, events, and data-informed engagement strategies. This position contributes to donor reporting, awards administration, and alumni programming that highlights the impact of giving and involvement. The coordinator maintains donor and alumni information within institutional systems and supports fiscal accountability for donor-funded activities. Through coordinated outreach and engagement efforts, this position strengthens connections between alumni, donors, students, faculty, and the broader community. Duties and Responsibilities: Implements comprehensive donor stewardship and recognition strategies to enhance donor satisfaction, retention, and long-term engagement, including first-time donor process, donor acknowledgements, and student impact. Develops and coordinates alumni engagement projects, programs, and events to strengthen relationships with stakeholders. Coordinates donor recognition initiatives to increase public awareness of the gift, the donor, and the college and university, including naming opportunities, stewardship events, gift announcements, and impact communications. Plans and executes alumni-focused events, such as networking receptions, panel discussions, and mentorship engagements. Coordinates and implements donor and student impact reporting processes. Prepares and processes terms of reference and gift agreements using established templates, coordinating internal approvals, and obtaining donor and senior leader signatures. Reviews, audits, and administers donor-related components of student award processes, including terms of reference compliance, donation management, and award financials, in collaboration with relevant teams and committees. Develops donor impact reports, newsletters, and engagement materials that highlight giving, alumni involvement, and community impact. Conducts analysis of existing programs and processes to recommend improvements and efficiencies. Maintains, reviews, and updates donor and alumni records within the Customer Relationship Management (CRM) system, tracking interactions and contributions. Monitors alumni achievements through news sources and professional platforms and engages alumni through direct outreach. Updates digital platforms, such as websites and social media channels, with alumni and donor news and recognition content. Collects and analyzes alumni data through surveys, informational interviews, and direct outreach to inform engagement strategies. Represents the college at community events and through various engagement channels, fostering relationships with individuals, corporations, and foundations interested in supporting the college. Advances donor relationships and fundraising priorities in collaboration with advancement, faculty, and college leadership. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in business, marketing, communications, or related field. Minimum of three years of related experience, preferably in donor relations, event management, or customer service. High level of accuracy and attention to detail. Strong interpersonal and communication skills. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a team. Excellent organizational and time-management skills. Ability to demonstrate diplomacy and tact while maintaining enthusiastic professionalism in interactions with diverse individuals and groups. Proficient computer skills, including Microsoft Office applications; experience with donor management or customer management software is an asset. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Department: Edwards School of Business Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 10/13/2026 Closing Date: 10/27/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15685?c=usask": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, Jane and Ron Graham Centre for the Scholarship of Teaching and Learning",
      "description": "Research Assistant, Jane and Ron Graham Centre for the Scholarship of Teaching and Learning | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Assistant, Jane and Ron Graham Centre for the Scholarship of Teaching and Learning Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15685 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Research Assistant, Jane and Ron Graham Centre for the Scholarship of Teaching and Learning As a Graduate Research Assistant in the Jane and Ron Graham Centre for the Scholarship of Teaching and Learning (SoTL), you will play a vital role in supporting a SoTL Research project. Working closely with the Graham Centre for SoTL Director, Dr. Melanie Hamilton, your responsibilities will include assisting in the early stage of the development of SoTL research project. In the appointment of Research Assistant, your general duties and responsibilities will be: Support initial project development by helping refine the research problem, research questions, and scope of inquiry. Develop and implement literature search strategies using scholarly databases, relevant journals, and publicly accessible sources. Locate, screen, and organize relevant literature, maintaining accurate references and documenting search strategies and inclusion decisions. Critically review and synthesize literature to identify key concepts, theoretical perspectives, methodological approaches, findings, and gaps relevant to the project. Prepare literature summaries, evidence tables, annotated bibliographies, and written syntheses to inform the project’s rationale, conceptual framework, and research design. Review publicly accessible websites and institutional resources to identify relevant SoTL initiatives, reports, and contextual information. Contribute to research proposals and materials for disseminating findings, including reports, presentations, and scholarly publications. Maintain detailed documentation of research activities, sources, and decisions, following established research protocols and ethical guidelines. Participate in regular project meetings and collaborate with interdisciplinary team members, as appropriate. Report directly to Dr. Melanie Hamilton, providing regular updates on project progress and seeking guidance as needed. Your general location of work will be online and you will report to Dr. Melanie Hamilton. Required Number of Hours: 30 hours during the academic term. Duration of Employment: November 15, 2026-April 30, 2026 Qualifications (Skills and Abilities) Must be enrolled in the Cross Departmental PhD in Education program with a focus in the Scholarship of Teaching and Learning. Demonstrated experience in the scholarship of teaching and learning (SoTL), including awareness of SoTL methodologies and research practices. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to effectively present research findings and insights to diverse audiences. Collaborative mindset and the ability to work effectively in interdisciplinary teams. You must be registered in the Cross Departmental PhD in Education program. To Apply : Interested applicants must include the following in their application: cover letter and curriculum vitae. Send your application materials to: sotl@usask.ca no later than October 20, 2026. Where a graduate student is appointed to this position, it will be in-scope of PSAC. Department: College of Education Status: Casual Employment Group: Graduate Students - PSAC Rate of Pay: Current rates of pay Posted Date: 10/13/2026 Closing Date: 10/20/2026 Number of Openings: 2 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15684?c=usask": {
      "title": "Clinical Associate, Veterinary Anesthesiology",
      "description": "Clinical Associate, Veterinary Anesthesiology | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Clinical Associate, Veterinary Anesthesiology Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15684 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Clinical Associate, Veterinary Anesthesiology The Clinical Associate is responsible for anesthesia clinical case management of large, small, and exotic animals, as well as the clinical instruction and supervision of final year veterinary students during the anesthesia clinical rotation.  The successful candidate will participate in the laboratory instruction of veterinary students but will not be expected to participate in didactic teaching.  After hours duties will be shared with other clinicians in the anesthesia section. Duties and Responsibilities: Involved in the day-to-day organization, supervision and responsibility for large, small, and exotic patients requiring anesthesia. Plays an integral role in the clinical education and supervision of veterinary and technical students, veterinary technicians, and interns and residents in a teaching hospital. Reports to the anesthesia faculty member scheduled on-clinics, or the service head, in matters pertaining to clinical work, scheduling, and education. Promotes clear, effective communication among team members and across services in a collaborative and positive manner. Builds and maintains strong professional relationships with clinicians, technicians and others in the building. Maintains accurate, timely, and comprehensive medical records. Delivers feedback and completes formal assessments and grading for veterinary students in anesthesia rotations. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: A Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) degree or equivalent Licensed (or able to obtain licensing) with the Saskatchewan Veterinary Medical Association A Diplomate of the American College of Veterinary Anesthesia and Analgesia (ACVAA) or the European College of Veterinary Anaesthesia and Analgesia (ECVAA) or has credentials acceptable to the ACVAA or ECVAA for board examination at the time of appointment Experience in clinical instruction, teaching, or mentorship is an asset. Strong communication and interpersonal abilities. Effective organizational and time-management capabilities. High attention to detail and accuracy. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a team. The successful candidate will also receive a clinical supplement for on call/after-hours clinical work in the amount of $7,200 per year. A comprehensive benefit package is provided; overview and ASPA plan summary can be found here: https://careers.usask.ca/benefits.php#Benefits Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026; however, applications will be accepted and evaluated until the position is filled. The desired start date is January 1, 2027 but is open to negotiation. Informal enquiries can be directed to barbara.ambros@usask.ca . Due to federal immigration requirements, we also ask candidates to indicate whether they are Canadian citizens, permanent residents, or are otherwise already authorized to work at this position for the duration of the appointment, with an explanation if this last category is indicated. Interested candidates must submit a cover letter, detailed curriculum vitae, and any supporting documents.  Applicants should also provide contact information for three professional referees. Department: Veterinary Medical Centre Status: Term 1 year with the possibility of extension Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $87,266.00 - 145,353.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 3 Posted Date: 10/13/2026 Closing Date: 11/30/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/14857?c=usask": {
      "title": "Equine Ambulatory Veterinarian, Large Animal Clinical Sciences",
      "description": "Equine Ambulatory Veterinarian, Large Animal Clinical Sciences | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Equine Ambulatory Veterinarian, Large Animal Clinical Sciences Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req14857 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Equine Ambulatory Veterinarian, Large Animal Clinical Sciences Summary: The equine ambulatory veterinarian provides on‑farm primary, preventative, and emergency medical care to horses within the local community. This role focuses on delivering routine services, such as dental care, reproductive management, performance horse medicine, and minor surgical procedures. The veterinarian also plays a key role in clinical teaching by supervising and instructing final‑year veterinary students, interns, and residents during field service rotations. This position utilizes modern portable diagnostic and therapeutic equipment to support high‑quality patient care.  Additionally, this role contributes to both clinical service and teaching within the college. Duties and Responsibilities: Provides on‑farm primary, preventative, and emergency medical care to equine patients Supervises and instructs final‑year veterinary students, equine practice interns, and residents during ambulatory rotations Performs routine equine dental care, including assessments and corrective procedures Delivers reproductive management services, such as breeding evaluations and pregnancy monitoring Carries out minor surgical procedures appropriate for field-based care Provides performance horse medicine, including diagnostics and treatment plans tailored to athletic horses Utilizes portable diagnostic equipment, such as digital radiography, ultrasound, endoscopy, and therapeutic devices Coordinates referral-based care in collaboration with specialty service professionals Contributes to departmental teaching activities, such as lectures, laboratories, and demonstrations Provides feedback and completes assessments and grading for students participating in ambulatory rotations Maintains accurate, timely, and comprehensive medical records for all equine patients seen in the field Responds to after-hours and on‑call requests as part of the equine field service team Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position Qualifications: A Doctor in Veterinary Medicine (DVM) degree or equivalent Licensed with the Saskatchewan Veterinary Medical Association A valid class five driver’s license Minimum of three years’ experience in equine ambulatory practice including equine primary care, preventative medicine, and emergency response Ability to work collaboratively with a variety of stakeholders in a multi‑clinician field service and referral hospital environment Familiarity with clinical instruction and mentorship is preferred Strong communication and interpersonal skills Excellent organizational and time-management abilities Ability to travel frequently within the province Department: Large Animal Clinical Sciences Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $87,266.00 - 145,353.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 3 Posted Date: 10/13/2026 Closing Date: 10/20/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15530?c=usask": {
      "title": "Library Assistant, Indigenous Studies Portal",
      "description": "Library Assistant, Indigenous Studies Portal | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Library Assistant, Indigenous Studies Portal Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15530 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Library Assistant, Indigenous Studies Portal The University is committed to employment equity, diversity, and inclusion, and is proud to support career opportunities for Indigenous Peoples to reflect the community we serve. We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Only Indigenous candidates will be considered for this position. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship with documentation is a condition of employment and a requirement to hold this position and the successful candidate must maintain those conditions throughout their employment. The Indigenous Studies Portal (iportal.usask.ca) is a research tool for the students and faculty of the University of Saskatchewan, but also for the people of Saskatchewan and beyond. The I-Portal is a database of full-text electronic resources such as articles, e-books, theses, government reports, videos, oral histories, and digitized archival documents and photographs. The I-Portal is an index of resources relating to Indigenous Peoples, cultures, and communities, with an emphasis on open access and freely available resources. We prioritize resources focused on what is now Canada, but also include information pertaining to Indigenous Peoples in the United States and in the larger International community. Primary Purpose: The successful candidate will select, describe, and maintain content in the Indigenous Studies Portal (I-Portal), provide assistance to people using the I-Portal, and work with colleagues, both on campus and in the larger community, to enhance the I-Portal as a learning and research tool. Nature of Work: The position is part of the University Archives and Special Collections (UASC) Division, reporting to the UASC Operations Manager, and with oversight and direction from the Assistant Dean for UASC. Work is performed in a dynamic and service-oriented environment with competing tasks and priorities. Duties are of above-average complexity involving a deep knowledge and understanding of the resources and functions related to the I-Portal. The work requires a high level of attention to detail. As the successful candidate, you are expected to use judgement and work independently on a daily basis. For the resolution of more complex issues, you will work with other I-Portal staff, the Assistant Dean, and other UASC and library colleagues. The need to be flexible and meet a variety of service standards and expectations from clients and colleagues is required. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Identifies and selects suitable content for inclusion into the I-Portal database and creates database records to represent the individual content. Uses discretion when making decisions regarding content to be added to the I-Portal, keeping in mind what constitutes culturally sensitive material; reviews I-Portal records for appropriate terminology and Indigenous context. Assists in the organization of topics and improves the range of resources available in the I-Portal. Creates descriptive metadata for each item, which includes a brief, accurate, and culturally informed descriptive annotation and other standard fields such as title, author, journal citations, and categories/subject headings. Identifies the need for changes to categories/subject headings for I-Portal records. Participates in quality assurance processes. Works collaboratively with colleagues to resolve issues, address inquiries, etc. Communicates and adheres to established policies, standards, procedures, and best practices. Participates in University Archives and Special Collections service delivery, primarily through reference desk coverage; Undertakes project work and other duties, as assigned. Participates in ongoing professional development that includes cultural capacity building, decolonial and antiracist education. Education: Library Technician Diploma, or undergraduate degree with relevant knowledge and/or experience. OCAP® training is considered an asset. Experience: Relevant library experience, particularly in the areas of cataloguing, metadata creation, and public services work – preferably with Indigenous research inquiries. In-depth knowledge of online resources. Experience working with Indigenous collections and research services would be preferred. Research experience in Indigenous arts, culture, education, health, legal systems, politics, etc. would be considered an asset. Knowledge of Indigenous worldviews, histories, governance structures, spiritualities, lands and/or cultures would be considered an asset. Experience working with/in Indigenous communities would be considered an asset. Skills: Excellent attention to detail. Demonstrated ability to use judgment and apply effective investigative, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Understanding of the importance of and commitment to the use of current language to describe Indigenous Peoples, cultures, communities and related research. Demonstrated cultural competence. Demonstrated understanding of colonial histories, Indigenous resistance and resurgence. Demonstrated ability to read, comprehend and summarize written academic documents in English and to accurately proof and edit text. Ability to find and use translation tools for Indigenous languages. Strong internet and database searching skills. Experience with technology used to manage library collections, and demonstrated aptitude for learning and interest in exploring new technologies. Demonstrated ability to work independently, use initiative, organize work, set priorities, meet deadlines and effectively handle interruptions. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with others in a team environment. Reliable, dependable, and self-motivated. Effective interpersonal skills. Effective written and verbal communication skills. Possesses a tactful, responsive, and effective manner in all personal interactions. Demonstrated flexibility to adapt to change. Inquiries regarding this position can be directed to Laurie Wing at laurie.wing@usask.ca or 306-966-2338. Department: Library Status: Permanent Employment Group: CUPE 1975 Shift: Monday to Friday, 8:30 am - 4:30 pm with an EDO every third week Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $30.25 - 38.38 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Operational Services Salary Phase/Band: Phase 5 Posted Date: 10/9/2026 Closing Date: 11/20/2026 Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15650?c=usask": {
      "title": "Engineering Shipper/Receiver and Key Custodian",
      "description": "Engineering Shipper/Receiver and Key Custodian | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Engineering Shipper/Receiver and Key Custodian Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15650 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Engineering Shipper/Receiver and Key Custodian Summary: This incumbent reports to the Supervisor, Engineering Shops. The incumbent works within established customer service guidelines and expectations of the unit. This position operates in a diverse, complex, and often highly active office environment with multiple competing demands and very strict deadlines. Continuous change, shifting priorities, and frequent interruptions are the norm. The work expectations in this position varies from basic administrative tasks to shipping and receiving of small to heavy and oversized items to, or received from, external parties. This position works independently with direct support from supervisor to handle a high volume of work and at times will be under pressure to meet deadlines. The position also works collaboratively with fellow employees and with other support staff while ensuring compliance to safety procedures when receiving dangerous goods into the building. The work includes solving problems requiring judgement assessments using defined processes and knowledge. Impact of error is high as the position is responsible to ensure day-to-day work is completed with the highest degree of accuracy.  Prompt, complete, and accurate work is extremely important to mitigate risks associated with health and safety and ensuring that movement of goods is timely. The position requires a moderate to high level of physical demand, including moderate to heavy lifting, bending, pushing or pulling associated shipping/receiving functions and other duties typically found in a warehouse environment. There is an expectation of the incumbent to be able to lift up to 23 kg unassisted and over this with proper equipment and assistance as required. The incumbent is expected to adorn required personal protective equipment (PPE), including steel toe boots. Clothing such as sweaters with hoods or loose suited clothing are not permitted. Occupational hazards may also include noise, fumes, vibrations, varying weather, and related conditions typical of working in a warehouse environment. The incumbent must be aware and mindful of safety hazards and work in compliance with all applicable policies, regulations, and safety practices. Duties and Responsibilities: The incumbent is expected to provide efficient and respectful front-line customer service to all stakeholders; ensuring queries are responded to or triaged and escalated appropriately. Key Custodian: Coordinate ordering, distributing, collecting, and accounting for all keys in the college by performing activities such as: Develop, maintain, and publicize key request form based on requirements defined by Facilities. Ensure all keys are properly authorized and safety training documented prior to issuing keys to recipient. Enter and track key requests into key database. Maintain current and accurate key records and prepare personnel or space key reports upon request. Ensure all keys returned are removed from the key database and sent to facilities for appropriate disposal. File repair and maintenance requests for locks throughout the building. Maintain and update information on college website for keys. Stores: Coordinate the purchase, inventory, deployment, and stocking of all consumable supplies (office, safety, compressed gas cylinders, and shop) in the college by performing activities such as: Maintain standard supplies in stock by performing regular inventory. Purchase supplies when stock is running low. Deploying supplies to main hubs in college or storing supplies within office. Safely handling, storing, and securing all compressed gas cylinders. Support technical staff who have procured specialized supplies by purchasing and coordinating the receipt and deployment of such items. Use university preferred vendors for purchasing supplies or identify preferred vendors for unique items that require purchasing as appropriate. Prepare and submit timely and accurate P-Card expenditures and reconciliations for all expenses incurred. Shipping & Receiving: Coordinate shipment and receipt of all products, equipment, materials, and supplies in the college by performing activities such as: Receive and inspect all incoming shipments. Promptly unload deliveries with forklift, dolly, or manually as required, and sort and stock receivables. Sort and deliver daily mail to the main hubs in the college. Return error shipments or damaged goods back to vendor. Notify intended recipients of arrival of shipment, tracing ownership by accessing purchase order if recipient identity is unclear. Prepare waybills, export documents, and transportation of dangerous goods documentation. Package shipments as required in preparation of shipping. Schedule pickup of shipments with preferred couriers. Trouble-shoot on behalf of faculty, staff, and students regarding problem orders. Maintain accurate records of incoming and outgoing shipments, scanning inventory, and counting accurately. Maintain and update information on college shipping/receiving webpages. General Duties: Develop and maintain procedural manual for the position. Maintain a clean, safe, organized, and neat workspace and office. Prepare and maintain information directories in the main college entrances and outside elevators and on college building webpages (access, hours, maps, main offices, etc). Other duties within the scope of work identified for this position will be assigned from time to time. Qualifications: Education : The incumbent must have completed grade 12. Comprehensive chemical and dangerous goods safety training including WHIMS and training and/or experience in Transportation of Dangerous Goods (TDG) for shipping and receiving is required. Incumbent must complete training successfully and maintain throughout duration of employment. Additional shipper/receiver training and/or experience in the use and safe handling of compressed gases would be beneficial. Licenses: A valid Province of Saskatchewan Class 5 driver’s license with an acceptable driving record is preferred.  It would be beneficial for the incumbent to have or obtain and maintain a valid forklift license. In the absence of a valid operator’s certificate for powered industrial vehicles (forklifts), several years of experience operating a forklift and the willingness to obtain a certificate within the probationary period will be considered. Experience : 1 to 2 years’ directly relevant experience is required.  Preference will be given to candidates who can demonstrate they possess prior experience in inventory management, administrative as well as shipping and receiving coordination. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Inquiries regarding this position can be directed to Blair Cole at blair.cole@usask.ca . Department: Engineering Shops Status: Permanent Employment Group: CUPE 1975 Shift: 8:00 - 4:00 pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $20.40 - 24.87 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Operational Services Salary Phase/Band: Phase 2 Posted Date: 10/6/2026 Closing Date: 11/17/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The successful applicant will be required to provide the following current verification(s) where 'Yes' is indicated below. Further information is available at: https://careers.usask.ca/tips-for-applying.php Criminal Record Check: Not Applicable Driver's License and Abstract Check: Yes Education/Credential Verification: Not Applicable Vulnerable Sector Check: Not Applicable The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15680?c=usask": {
      "title": "Professional Research Associate, Agricultural and Resource Economics",
      "description": "Professional Research Associate, Agricultural and Resource Economics | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Professional Research Associate, Agricultural and Resource Economics Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15680 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Professional Research Associate, Agricultural and Resource Economics The Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics is home to expertise in agricultural policy, environmental economics, and farm management. Faculty work closely with governments, producer organizations, and industry partners. The Research Fellow will draw on this research community, graduate student expertise, and departmental infrastructure to produce timely economic research and policy analysis for Saskatchewan crop producer organizations. Primary Purpose: To conduct independent, rigorous, and timely applied economic research on agricultural policy questions identified by an advisory committee. The Fellow will translate this research into clear policy briefs, presentations, and other public outputs that support evidence-based advocacy and decision-making by Saskatchewan crop producer organizations. Nature of Work: The Research Fellow will report to the Head of the Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics and will receive day-to-day supervision from faculty within the department.  The Fellow will be responsible for conducting approximately four research projects each year, with each project resulting in a policy brief and a longer article.  The fellow will work on these projecrts with faculty, and graduate students.  The projects will also involve liaising with producer organizations, government representatives, and other policy stakeholders. The work requires advanced training in agricultural economics, including econometrics, policy analysis, and economic modelling. The fellow will also need a keen understanding of the western Canadian agricultural industry. Occasional travel within Saskatchewan and elsewhere may be required for meetings, workshops, and presentations. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Produce approximately four policy briefs each year on topics selected by the advisory committee, working with faculty who have relevant subject-matter expertise. Conduct applied economic analysis using appropriate methods, which may include econometric analysis, literature reviews, economic modelling, and farm-level cost-benefit analysis. Prepare research outputs for public release and contribute to presentations and publications with collaborating faculty. Plan and convene an annual policy workshop or roundtable for producer organizations, faculty, and other policy stakeholders. Maintain regular contact with participating producer organizations and attend relevant meetings so the research agenda remains responsive to current and anticipated policy issues. Provide briefings and presentations that explain research methods, findings, limitations, and implications to varied audiences. Prepare a short annual report summarizing completed work and progress against the approved research agenda. Education: A master's degree in agricultural economics, economics, public policy, or a closely related discipline is required. A doctoral degree in a relevant discipline is an asset. Experience: Experience conducting applied economic or policy research is required. Experience with agricultural policy, farm management, trade, business risk management, environmental policy, or input regulation is an asset. Experience managing research projects, working with stakeholder or advisory groups, and communicating research to policy and industry audiences is also an asset. Skills: Strong applied research skills, including the ability to frame policy questions, select appropriate methods, assess evidence, and explain the limits of an analysis. Proficiency in quantitative analysis and economic modelling, with the ability to work with relevant statistical or analytical software. Excellent written and oral communication skills, including the ability to prepare concise policy briefs and present technical findings to non-technical audiences. Ability to work independently, manage concurrent projects and deadlines, and collaborate effectively with faculty, producer organizations, government representatives, and other stakeholders. Sound judgment, discretion, initiative, attention to detail, and strong organizational skills. Department: Agricultural and Resource Economics Supervisor Job Title: Associate Professor and Canadian Canola Growers Association Chair in Agricultural Policy Status: Term Employment Group: Research Associates - PSAC Rate of Pay: Current rates of pay Posted Date: 10/8/2026 Closing Date: 11/5/2026 Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15678?c=usask": {
      "title": "Research Technician, Soil Science",
      "description": "Research Technician, Soil Science | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Technician, Soil Science Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15678 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Research Technician, Soil Science Primary Purpose: The technician will support the Chair in Soil Biogeochemical Processes in the Department of Soil Science at the University of Saskatchewan. The role combines field and laboratory research, with an emphasis on maintaining instrumented field sites and conducting analytical laboratory work. Nature of Work: Laboratory work includes operating analytical equipment such as gas chromatographs and analyzing soil samples. The technician will keep field experiments running by installing, maintaining, repairing, and calibrating instrumentation, including gas analyzers, dataloggers, and micrometeorological towers. The technician will also coordinate student field work, train and guide graduate and summer students, and keep research spaces clean and safe. The position requires travel to field sites, occasional early-morning and late-evening work, work in harsh outdoor conditions (heat, cold, insects), and the ability to lift 50 lbs. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Monitor, operate, and maintain micrometeorological field instrumentation (environmental sensors, dataloggers, solar power systems) Operate gas chromatographers Install field instrumentation; build, repair, and calibrate specialized equipment Collect soil, plant, and manual gas samples Coordinate students for field work Collect, organize, and document data Education: Post-secondary diploma or degree in environmental science, engineering, or a related field. Experience in operating electronic instrumentation and conducting research-based field and/or laboratory work. Licenses: Must possess a valid license for driving in Saskatchewan, and posses or be willing to obtain the following certificates: WHMIS Certificate General Laboratory Safety U of S Safety Training for new Employees Working on Heights training Skills: Excellent communication and interpersonal skills and ability to relate effectively with other staff and students; effective written and oral English communication skills; ability to prioritize and organize time effectively; proven capacity to work independently and in a team setting; detail-oriented and able to perform repetitive tasks accurately and consistently; strong critical-thinking and problem-solving skills for troubleshooting field and laboratory instruments. Technical and knowledge requirements are advanced computing skills (e.g., Excel, R, Python, GIS), knowledge field and laboratory procedures, and knowledge of Saskatchewan agricultural practices. Inquiries regarding this position can be directed to Shannon Brown at 306-966-2703. Department: Soil Science Status: Term  with the possibility of extension Employment Group: Research Positions - Non-union Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Phase/Band: Salary Band 6 Posted Date: 10/8/2026 Closing Date: 11/5/2026 Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15673?c=usask": {
      "title": "Manager, Student Services, Undergraduate Medical Education (Saskatoon or Regina)",
      "description": "Manager, Student Services, Undergraduate Medical Education (Saskatoon or Regina) | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Manager, Student Services, Undergraduate Medical Education (Saskatoon or Regina) Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15673 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Manager, Student Services, Undergraduate Medical Education (Saskatoon or Regina) Primary Purpose: The Manager, Student Services provides strategic leadership, direction, and oversight for student support programming within the Undergraduate Medical Education (UGME) program in the College of Medicine. Reporting to the Director, Student Services, this role establishes the vision, strategic priorities, and long-term development of programs that support medical students’ academic success, wellness, and professional development, while also leading and managing a team of student services staff. The Manager applies expertise in student affairs, career advising and mentorship, program development, and medical education to drive innovation, strengthen student support initiatives, and enhance coordinated services. This role leads a dedicated team in delivering impactful, evidence-informed support services across all sites and through all years of medical school, ensuring alignment with accreditation standards and the College’s strategic priorities. Nature of Work : This position plays a pivotal leadership role within Student Services, exercising independent judgment to develop, implement, and evaluate initiatives that advance student success and well-being and career development. The Manager cultivates collaborative relationships with internal and external stakeholders, including learners, faculty, academic departments, student services staff, residents, and provincial partners. The role requires the ability to analyze emerging trends in student support, wellness, and professional development, translating these insights into actionable program improvements. Operating in a complex environment with confidential student interactions, competing priorities, and evolving accreditation requirements, the incumbent demonstrates strategic thinking, innovation, and leadership in shaping short- and long-term program outcomes. Typical Duties or Accountabilities Office of Student Affairs (OSA) Provide strategic leadership and oversight for student affairs programming that supports learner wellness, academic success, professional development, and student engagement across all years of the UGME program. Develop and maintain a comprehensive framework for student support services that fosters learner well-being, resilience, professionalism, and successful progression through medical training. Oversee the planning, implementation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of student affairs initiatives, ensuring responsiveness to learner needs and emerging trends in medical education. Provide leadership and oversight for student support programming, ensuring effective coordination of services, activities, and resources across all UGME campuses and distributed learning sites. Oversee the development, delivery, and evaluation of student affairs curriculum and co-curricular programming, including wellness, professionalism, transition, and learner development initiatives embedded throughout the UGME curriculum. Guide the implementation of student-focused events, workshops, orientation activities, and leadership development opportunities that enhance learner engagement and success. Ensure effective processes and supports are in place for students requiring academic accommodation, leave management, wellness supports, and referrals to university and community resources. Provide consultation and confidential support to students experiencing academic, personal, wellness, or professional challenges, facilitating appropriate referrals and interventions as required. Oversee the delivery and evaluation of student support curriculum components, including wellness and professional development programming within UGME courses and learning experiences. Lead initiatives that strengthen learner engagement and community building, including Peer Learning Networks (PLNs), student leadership opportunities, and other student-centered programs. Collaborate with Distributed Medical Education sites, including SLIC communities, to enhance learner support, strengthen community connections, and ensure equitable access to student affairs programming and services regardless of training location. Foster partnerships with University of Saskatchewan student support units and external organizations to enhance available resources and services for learners. Provide coverage for student support when required. Office of Career Advising and Mentorship (OCAM) Provide strategic leadership for the development, implementation, and evaluation of a comprehensive career advising framework supporting students throughout medical school and transition to residency. Oversee required and optional career advising activities that promote informed career exploration, specialty selection, residency preparation, and professional development. Utilize learner feedback, career advising outcomes, residency match trends, and environmental scans to identify opportunities for program enhancement and innovation. Provide oversight of mentorship programming and support meaningful engagement of faculty, residents, alumni, and community physicians involved in advising and mentorship activities. Coordinate and support faculty advisors and physicians involved in residency application preparation, ensuring consistent processes for recruitment, onboarding, communication, and engagement. Monitor provincial and national trends related to physician workforce planning, residency matching, and career development to inform advising strategies and program priorities. Promote equitable career advising and mentorship opportunities for learners across all UGME campuses and distributed learning sites. General Leadership, Management, and Strategic Oversight Collaborate with the Director of Student Services, Associate Dean UGME, and other leaders to advance the strategic direction of student support services within UGME. Provide leadership, supervision, and performance management for staff within the Student Services portfolio, including recruitment, onboarding, mentorship, professional development, workload management, and leave administration Establish performance measures, program evaluation frameworks, and reporting mechanisms to assess outcomes, demonstrate impact, and support continuous quality improvement. Oversee faculty, resident, volunteer, and stakeholder engagement in student support initiatives, ensuring alignment with institutional priorities and accreditation standards. Develop and maintain collaborative relationships with learners, faculty, academic departments, health system partners, community physicians, and university stakeholders. Manage operating budgets, monitor expenditures, and provide financial reporting and recommendations to support effective resource allocation. Ensure compliance with UGME accreditation standards, university policies, and best practices related to student support programming. Monitor trends and emerging issues in medical education, learner wellness, career development, and student affairs to inform strategic planning and program development. Lead special projects and undertake additional responsibilities as assigned. Education: University master’s degree or equivalent in social work, health care administration, education, psychology, or a related field preferably at the master’s level. Equivalent combinations of education and experience may be considered.an education or equivalent degree is required; equivalent work experience will be considered. Experience: 5–10 years of progressively responsible experience in student services, program leadership, higher education administration, health professions education, or a related field. Experience leading programs, initiatives, or teams in complex organizational environments. Post-secondary or health care education experience strongly preferred. Skills: High professional integrity and ability to maintain confidentiality and manage sensitive matters with diplomacy and discretion. Exceptional interpersonal, communication (oral and written), organizational, and analytical skills. Proven ability to take initiative and be self-directed. Proven ability to manage multiple priorities and competing demands with tight timelines. Strong leadership, delegation, and collaborative skills. Creativity, problem-solving, and attention to detail. Coaching and performance management expertise. Ability to research, present, and implement complex solutions effectively. Proficiency in Microsoft Office suite, electronic calendars, and web-based tools. Inquiries regarding this position can be directed at Sherry Pederson at sherry.pederson@usask.ca . Department: College of Medicine Status: Term 2 years with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: ASPA Shift: 8:30-4:30 pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $87,266.00 - 145,353.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Managerial Salary Phase/Band: Phase 3 Posted Date: 10/8/2026 Closing Date: 10/22/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: Open to Saskatoon or Regina. May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15651?c=usask": {
      "title": "Workforce and Administrative Coordinator, Western College of Veterinary Medicine",
      "description": "Workforce and Administrative Coordinator, Western College of Veterinary Medicine | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Workforce and Administrative Coordinator, Western College of Veterinary Medicine Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15651 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Workforce and Administrative Coordinator, Western College of Veterinary Medicine Summary: The workforce and administrative coordinator provides administrative coordination and operational support for workforce-related activities within the department. This position coordinates recruitment, onboarding, employee administration, scheduling support, and a variety of workforce transactions and processes. Working within a coordinated administrative team, this role supports managers and employees by facilitating workforce activities, maintaining records, responding to inquiries, and coordinating operational workflows. The coordinator contributes to the continuity of administrative services through collaboration with scheduling and finance staff and supports workforce-related reporting, documentation, and process improvement activities. This role also helps maintain accurate workforce information and supports effective employee transitions throughout the employment lifecycle. Duties and Responsibilities: Coordinates recruitment administration, including preparing and posting opportunities, screening applications against established criteria, communicating with candidates, scheduling interviews, facilitating reference checks, preparing selection materials, and maintaining recruitment documentation. Coordinates onboarding and offboarding activities, including employee documentation, orientation arrangements, systems and facility access, keys, parking, training requirements, and other processes that support employee transitions. Processes workforce-related transactions, including appointments, extensions, employment changes, leave documentation, and departures, while liaising with university service units and following up on outstanding items. Serves as a central administrative contact for managers and employees by responding to process-related inquiries, providing status updates, and referring matters requiring specialized support to the appropriate university unit. Escalates unusual, complex, or higher-risk matters to the appropriate manager or specialized office. Coordinates shared workflows, supports workforce-related financial and operational transactions, and maintains continuity of administrative services in collaboration with finance administration and scheduling staff. Supports casual employee hour approvals and employee time reporting processes. Assists with payroll corrections and other payroll-related transactions. Supports month-end financial processes through cash deposits, statement generation, and accounts receivable activities. Compiles, tracks, and reports workforce information, process status, and service metrics to support operational planning, follow-up, and decision-making. Develops and maintains templates, checklists, process documentation, and administrative resources, and identifies opportunities to improve consistency, efficiency, and service delivery. Maintains accurate and confidential employee recruitment, workforce and personal records in accordance with university procedures, privacy requirements, and records management practices. Provides administrative support to managers, including preparing correspondence, forms, reports, meeting materials, schedules, and workforce-related information. Supports staff scheduling activities by maintaining scheduling information, facilitating shift and coverage adjustments, and assisting with staffing coordination. Builds effective working relationships with managers, employees, candidates, and university service units while providing responsive and professional support. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Qualifications: An office administration diploma; a bachelor’s degree in a related field is an asset. Minimum of three years of progressively responsible experience coordinating recruitment, onboarding, workforce administration, scheduling, or related administrative processes in a complex service environment. Experience using applicant tracking, employee information, scheduling, financial, or related administrative systems is an asset. Familiarity with university structures, processes, and systems is preferred. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to handle confidential and sensitive information with discretion, sound judgment, and professional integrity. Excellent organizational and time-management skills. Ability to coordinate a high volume of detailed work, maintain competing priorities, and meet deadlines. Effective relationship-building and client-service abilities. High attention to detail and accuracy. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a team. Proficient computer skills, including Microsoft Office applications; ability to learn and use new systems and technologies. Department: Veterinary Medical Centre Status: Permanent Employment Group: CUPE 1975 Shift: Mon-Fri, 8:00-4:30 pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $24.74 - 31.37 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Operational Services Salary Phase/Band: Phase 4 Posted Date: 9/30/2026 Closing Date: 11/3/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15649?c=usask": {
      "title": "Graduate Commons Clerical Assistant",
      "description": "Graduate Commons Clerical Assistant | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Graduate Commons Clerical Assistant Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15649 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/8/2026 Back to Search Graduate Commons Clerical Assistant Primary Purpose: To provide high level administrative support for graduate programs in the College of Engineering, promoting a positive in-program experience for all graduate students. This includes providing high quality support and assistance in planning and implementing administrative processes to ensure effective and efficient operations to enhance the student experience and the overall academic mission of the College of Engineering. Nature of Work: The position is a member of the graduate commons team and reports directly to the Manager, Clerical Student Support in the College of Engineering. The position will receive direction from multiple stakeholders in the college; working closely with graduate chairs and faculty and is the primary contact for current and prospective students. The position works with general supervision but performs all aspects of assigned duties accurately with minimal direction.  The position is expected to coordinate their workflow within the graduate commons team and independently decide which processes to use to assist in achieving objectives. This position will be responsible for working directly with the Manager, Clerical Student Support, or other key stakeholders (College Leadership Team, Engineering Student Centre, and External Relations) on special projects and college initiatives. This part of the position requires advanced technical skills and the ability to collaborate with other professional and administrative staff, and academic leaders internal and external to the college. This position is expected to maintain a productive and professional relationship with the College of Graduate and Postdoctoral Studies. This position will operate in a diverse, complex, and often highly active office environment with multiple, competing demands and very strict deadlines. Continuous change, shifting priorities, and frequent interruptions are the norm. The volume of work will fluctuate through the year. The expectation is that work volume can be completed in a normal workday and the level of stress is moderate, with higher levels of activity anticipated during higher volume periods. The incumbent will be expected to proactively plan and execute workflow around these deadlines, peak periods, and unexpected situations. Impact of error is high as the position is responsible to ensure day-to-day work is completed with the highest degree of accuracy. Prompt, complete and accurate work is extremely important to mitigate risks associated with supporting students through the life cycle of their degree, accreditation, safety, finance and overall reputation. The incumbent is expected to operate independently, use sound judgment to make decisions, and provide accurate information to all relevant stakeholders. Decisions are made based on established policies and procedures, with assistance provided by professional staff or academics when interpretation is required or when complex issues arise. The incumbent is expected to remain current and well versed in policies and procedures related to their position. This position is expected to adapt and modify known practices to solve problems and achieve goals. The incumbent is expected to make recommendations to the work or work environment and is expected to contribute to policy and process improvement. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Provide administrative support for all aspects of the graduate programs in the College, from pre-application through to convocation including activities such as: Respond and manage inquiries from prospective and current students. Receive and process applications to the graduate programs (logging material received, corresponding with applicants, rejections, etc. within the software). Flag high quality applicants to the Graduate Chairs. Process admission deferral requests for graduate students. Adjust graduate applicant information (start term, degree, referee information). Administer program transfers and Western Dean’s Agreement. Manage and facilitate the onboarding and exiting processes for graduate students (including orientation, desk space assignment, and safety training record keeping). Initiate, coordinate, and maintain records for programs of study for each individual graduate student (including personalized program of courses, advisory committee membership, committee mtg dates, minutes, reports). Process course registrations, run registration reports and follow up on students reaching inactive status. Coordinate and manage graduate program processes (i.e. pre-defense, thesis defense, qualifying exams, comprehensive exams, candidacy assessments, extensions). Meeting, seminar, course, advisory committee, defense, and/or orientation coordination and support. Manages correspondence and other documentation for the graduate programs. Provides information about the graduate programs to potential applicants and supports recruitment activities which may include independently providing representation at recruitment events. Assists with award applications such as 75th Anniversary Recruitment Scholarship, SSF, Thesis Award, etc. Manages grade entry, class permissions, instructor entry and class lists. Ensuring data/content on website (USask and college) is current and inform changes to applicable parties or make changes directly. Updates the graduate handbook to align with institutional policy and academic process. Support student experience enhancement initiatives e.g. welcome events, Snacks for Students event, holiday activities, etc.. Engaging students and faculty to receive program and student experience feedback. Support processes at the program level for submission to CGPS e.g. academic misconduct, new and special topic course submissions, etc. Mentoring and training of new Graduate Clerical Assistants. Act as a knowledge resource. Manage additional special projects and responsibilities as assigned. Serve as the primary point of contact as a member of the graduate commons team for all graduate students in the college by providing effective and professional support including activities such as: Ensures that all communication and interactions with students, faculty, staff, and the public are accurate, timely and courteous. Emphasizes customer service and the accurate exchange of technical and factual information. Remains apprised of the resources and support programs available on campus for graduate students. Remains apprised of current CGPS policies and procedures, updating the graduate chairs and other faculty as necessary. Adapts established methods and practices to solve problems; discusses difficult or anomalous issues or problems with the Manager, Clerical Student Support to determine appropriate solutions. Completes work within established deadlines, by advanced planning and ensuring a clear understanding of the required deadlines, effectively setting work priorities and collaborating with others when appropriate. Works effectively and cooperatively with others and consciously applies team skills to support the goals of the team. Documents and updates all policies, processes and procedures, and follows them according to established standards. Education: Successful completion of grade 12 and an administrative assistant diploma from a recognized post-secondary education institution. A relevant post-secondary education will be considered. Experience: A minimum of five years of relevant experience in an office with complex policies and procedures with a minimum of three years of experience in a post-secondary educational institution is required. Experience in a University setting supporting administrative processes for graduate studies would be ideal. Skills: The following soft-skills are required for this position: Possesses high standards of professional integrity and proficiency in all aspects of operations. Maintains confidentiality and handles sensitive matters diplomatically while exercising sound judgement and discretion; Shares knowledge appropriately to allow fully informed decision making by colleagues and senior members of the unit and college. Works both independently with minimal supervision, and as an effective team member. Develops and maintains collaborative, professional working relationships with key stakeholders, both within and external to the college, to exchange accurate information, initiate planning, coordinate projects, and resolve crises with tact and diplomacy. Demonstrates an ability to deal effectively with multiple priorities that originate from a variety of sources with competing deadlines while maintaining attention to detail and ensuring accuracy. Possesses strong organizational and time management skills. Maintains the ability to learn new tasks in a fast-paced environment. Exercises sound independent judgement in decision-making and problem solving to ensure priorities and deadlines are met. Takes initiative to move beyond completion of assigned tasks to plan for future requirements. Strong interpersonal and communication skills with the ability to communicate both in writing and verbally to a diverse population of faculty, staff, and students in a professional, responsive, pleasant, proactive and effective manner. Demonstrate a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive campus environment. The following technical-skills are required for this position: Advanced proficiency using the most current Microsoft Office suite, particularly Word, Outlook, Excel, Teams and Channels, SharePoint and OneNote. Advanced proficiency using the most current PDF software. Proficiency working with marketing and design program such as Canva. Advanced proficiency inputting and retrieving data and reports from databases, experience using university specific databases will be considered an asset. Proficiency in inputting data and maintaining information on web pages. Inquiries regarding this position can be directed to Candace Poon at candace.poon@usask.ca Department: College of Engineering Status: Term 1 year with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: CUPE 1975 Shift: 8:30 to 4:30 pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $30.25 - 38.38 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Operational Services Salary Phase/Band: Phase 5 Posted Date: 9/30/2026 Closing Date: 11/4/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15672?c=usask": {
      "title": "Project Coordinator, Office of the Vice-President Research",
      "description": "Project Coordinator, Office of the Vice-President Research | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Project Coordinator, Office of the Vice-President Research Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15672 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/7/2026 Back to Search Project Coordinator, Office of the Vice-President Research Summary: The project coordinator leads the successful delivery of the Story Collider project by coordinating post-award activities, leading project administration, and overseeing financial, operational, and milestone progress. This position handles project budgets, tracks deliverables, identifies risks, and facilitates the resolution of issues that may affect project outcomes. This role coordinates a range of project functions, including reporting, communications, meetings, partnerships, and records management. This position works closely with internal and external stakeholders, funding agencies, and project partners to facilitate project activities and maintain accountability. The project coordinator also develops budget justifications, reports, and scope change documentation while supporting ongoing project planning and implementation. Duties and Responsibilities: Develops, monitors, manages, maintains, and coordinates the Story Collider project budget. Identifies emerging issues and changes and collaborates with the project team to develop solutions that mitigate risks and support project outcomes. Maintains financial and milestone accountability for the project schedule, monitors progress toward objectives and supports timely and fiscally responsible project delivery. Controls and allocates project resources to the appropriate outlet and tracks usage. Coordinates administrative functions of the project, including human resources throughout the employee life cycle, communications, management meetings, conferences, partner relationships, and intellectual property activities. Develops and executes the project Story Collider business plan and execution through research, stakeholder engagement, and planning activities on a year-to-year basis. Develops budget justifications, annual reports, and scope change documentation to support project requirements and funding obligations. Coordinates governance activities, including planning and facilitating leadership and committee meetings, preparing materials, managing communications, documenting outcomes, and tracking action items. Cultivates relationships, facilitates collaboration, and communicates with project partners, collaborators, stakeholders, academics, and committees involved in the project. Handles and maintains project records using institutional systems and databases, tracks project activities, and supports audit and reporting requirements. Coordinates the preparation and submission of reports, meeting minutes, summaries, and related project documentation while monitoring key deadlines. Creates avenues for engagement and acts as a liaison with university administration, project partners, and external organizations to coordinate project activities, facilitate reporting and communications, and foster effective working relationships. Provides proactive administrative and project support to project leadership and principal investigators. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in accounting, finance, project management, or a related field. Project Management Professional (PMP) certification is an asset. Minimum of four years of related experience in project coordination. Familiarity with Background in creative design and technology is an asset. Knowledge of the Canadian research and innovation environment. Knowledge of academic and research funding programs, processes, and requirements. Ability to develop reports, budget justifications, and project documentation. Effective interpersonal and communication skills. Ability to assess risks, monitor project progress, and support issue resolution. Strong project management, budgeting, financial analysis, and post-award administration skills. High attention to detail and accuracy. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a team. Strong strategic thinking, sound judgement, and problem-solving abilities. Effective organizational skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities. Department: Office of the Vice-President Research Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $69,035.00 - 107,868.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 2 Posted Date: 10/7/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15670?c=usask": {
      "title": "Gallery Administrator, Kenderdine Gallery",
      "description": "Gallery Administrator, Kenderdine Gallery | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Gallery Administrator, Kenderdine Gallery Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15670 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/7/2026 Back to Search Gallery Administrator, Kenderdine Gallery Summary: The gallery administrator coordinates the daily operations of the university’s galleries and art collection, supporting exhibitions, collections management, public programming, communications, and administrative activities. This position leads exhibition logistics, registrar functions, collections documentation, and access to gallery and collection resources in support of teaching, research, and community engagement initiatives. Working with faculty, staff, students, artists, donors, community organizations, and external partners, this role supports exhibitions, research projects, educational activities, and public events. The administrator handles multiple concurrent projects, maintains collection records and digital assets, and supports the stewardship and accessibility of the university art collection. Additionally, this position also coordinates communications, financial administration, and operational processes while contributing to the ongoing enhancement of gallery and collections operations. Duties and Responsibilities: Leads registrar activities related to the acquisition, documentation, movement, care, loans, and stewardship of the university art collection. Coordinates exhibition logistics including artwork transportation, installation schedules, project timelines, exhibition documentation, and related operational activities. Develops and coordinates communications supporting exhibitions, collections, public programming, and special initiatives. Facilitates access to gallery spaces and the university art collection by supporting exhibitions, teaching, research, and public engagement activities. Cultivates collaborative relationships with faculty, staff, students, artists, researchers, donors, community organizations, lenders, service units, and external partners. Coordinates exhibitions, installations, and public programming for the university art galleries. Coordinates administrative and operational activities for exhibitions, research initiatives, public programming, grants, awards, and special projects. Monitors project schedules and coordinates activities among multiple stakeholders to support project delivery. Coordinates digitization initiatives through photography, image management, metadata maintenance, and digital asset organization. Produces promotional materials, newsletters, website content, social media communications, exhibition materials, and other public-facing communications. Maintains accurate collections databases, digital records, cataloguing information, and collection documentation in accordance with professional standards. Administers documentation related to acquisitions, loans, exhibitions, condition reporting, inventories, audits, and collections activities. Coordinates exhibition openings, artist talks, lectures, workshops, tours, recruitment activities, and other public engagement initiatives. Administers purchasing, invoice processing, expense reconciliations, budget tracking, and other financial administration activities. Supports committees and working groups by coordinating meetings, preparing agendas, documenting discussions, and monitoring follow-up activities. Maintains gallery websites and digital content to support accessibility, engagement, and information sharing. Prepares reports, presentations, correspondence, briefing materials, and other administrative documentation. Coordinates the daily administrative and operational activities of the university galleries and art collection. Maintains accurate electronic and physical records related to exhibitions, collections, projects, and administrative operations. Contributes to the development and continuous improvement of operational procedures, collections documentation, administrative processes, and program resources. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in museum studies, collections management, fine arts, art history, archival studies, cultural heritage management, arts administration, or information management. Minimum of three years of experience supporting gallery, museum, collections, archival, cultural heritage, or arts administration operations. Experience coordinating collections management, registrar activities, exhibitions, public programming, cultural events, and community engagement initiatives, including collections documentation, loans, acquisitions, and records management. Experience working within a post-secondary institution, museum, gallery, or public sector environment is an asset. Knowledge of museum, gallery, registrar, collections management, or archival practices and professional standards. Strong organizational, planning, and project coordination skills with the ability to maintain multiple priorities and competing deadlines. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills. Ability to exercise sound judgment, discretion, diplomacy, and confidentiality. High attention to detail and accuracy. Ability to work independently while contributing effectively within a collaborative team environment. Proficient computer skills, including Microsoft Office applications; experience with photography, image management, graphic design, website maintenance, or digital communications is an asset. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Department: Kenderdine Gallery Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 10/7/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15666?c=usask": {
      "title": "Dispatcher, Protective Services",
      "description": "Dispatcher, Protective Services | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Dispatcher, Protective Services Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15666 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/7/2026 Back to Search Dispatcher, Protective Services Summary: The dispatcher serves as the first point of contact for incoming calls for service. Following guidelines for various types of responses, the dispatcher determines the appropriate response, resolves issues as able, and dispatches patrol officers as required. Primary Purpose: Key responsibilities include monitoring field units to support the safety and availability of officers in the field, generating incident reports, and responding to emergency  requests. Additionally, this position handles intrusion alarms, performs clerical duties, and implements lost and found procedures. Nature of Work: Reporting to the Staff Sergeant, you will demonstrate a high level of good judgement and the ability to think quickly to apply departmental policies and procedures.  Due to the nature of the work, discretion is critical.  The ability to multitask and remain calm in stressful situations will be expected. This position will operate in a diverse, complex, and often highly active office environment with multiple, and competing demands. Continuous change, shifting priorities, and frequent interruptions are the norm. Prompt, complete, and accurate work is extremely important to mitigate risks associated with supporting Officers and the community. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Monitors major incidents and creates a record for presentation at debriefing. Monitors field units for well-being and checks in on officers regarding safety and availability, revising and updated statuses as appropriate. Generates calls for service, initiates, and completes incident reports as applicable. Receives and immediately dispatches field units to intrusion alarms. Monitors intrusion alarm systems, contacts technicians, and troubleshoots to keep all systems operational. Provides reception and client service support to other departments as required. Responds to multiple telephone calls, computer systems, and radio equipment simultaneously. Determines the type of service required and location of incident, and ensures emergency requests receive immediate action. Provides appropriate information for public disclosure or refers callers to appropriate department or agency. Disseminates pertinent information to field personnel. Reviews unit policies and procedures, emergency management plans, and dispatcher responsibilities for major emergencies on a regular basis. Performs Canadian Police Information Centre (CPIC), Saskatchewan Government Insurance (SGI), and record management system queries and relays information as required. Responds to and completes warrant callback requests from the provincial courthouse. Performs occasional administrative duties including but not limited to drafting correspondence, mail sorting, and file creation. Sends and removes alerts and messages in alignment with USAFE and emergency communications protocols. Implements procedures for collection, storage, and disposal of all lost and found items. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Education: Grade 12 diploma. A post-secondary diploma or certificate in a related field is preferred. Experience: Minimum of one year administrative experience. Previous dispatch or field experience in law enforcement, security, or policing is preferred. Skills: Excellent communication skills, both verbal and written. Demonstrated ability to deliver prompt, complete, and accurate work. Excellent critical thinking, sound judgement, discretion, and timely decision-making skills. High degree of professionalism and integrity. Ability to work independently, take initiative, and multi-task efficiently and effectively. Ability to remain professional, composed, and organized under pressure or in high-stress situations. Knowledge of university buildings, locations, and street names is preferred. Ability to take initiative and multi-task efficiently and effectively. Strong computer and typing skills and proficiency with technology. Successful applicants will be required to provide a satisfactory criminal record check. Department: Protective Services Status: Term 6 months with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: CUPE 1975 Shift: Hours will vary and will include days, evenings, weekends as well as Statutory holidays. Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $24.74 - 31.37 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Security Services Salary Phase/Band: Phase 4 Posted Date: 10/7/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The successful applicant will be required to provide the following current verification(s) where 'Yes' is indicated below. Further information is available at: https://careers.usask.ca/tips-for-applying.php Criminal Record Check: Yes Driver's License and Abstract Check: Not Applicable Education/Credential Verification: Yes Vulnerable Sector Check: Not Applicable The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15664?c=usask": {
      "title": "Sessional Lecturer, Curriculum Studies, ECUR 316 (Cumberland House Cree Nation)",
      "description": "Sessional Lecturer, Curriculum Studies, ECUR 316 (Cumberland House Cree Nation) | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Sessional Lecturer, Curriculum Studies, ECUR 316 (Cumberland House Cree Nation) Cumberland House Cree Nation, SK, Canada | req15664 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/7/2026 Back to Search Sessional Lecturer, Curriculum Studies, ECUR 316 (Cumberland House Cree Nation) The Department of Curriculum Studies and the Indian Teacher Education Program (ITEP) in the College of Education, and the Gwenna Moss Centre for Teaching and Learning invite applications for an Out-of-Scope Sessional Lecturer to teach the following community-based courses during the following session(s): Winter 2027 Sessions Course Number, Section, and Name: ECUR 316.3 (E12) Methods in K to 9 Mathematics II Term and Course Dates: Term 2 (January 1, 2027 to April 30, 2027) CRN: 33290 Delivery Mode: This course will be offered as a Lecture (Live in-person on Cumberland House Cree Nation). Course Schedule: Wednesdays. Actual class times to be determined in consultation with the successful applicant at the discretion of program; 9:00 AM to 5:00 PM. No evenings or weekends. Expected Enrollment Limit: 35 Location: Cumberland House Cree Nation (approx. 4.5hrs NE of Saskatoon, SK) Qualifications: Minimum of a Master’s degree or equivalent in Education or a related discipline and/or possession of relevant teaching or professional experience. Applicants must demonstrate a commitment to engaging with Indigenous ways of knowing and to fostering respectful, reciprocal learning environments. They must also possess knowledge and understanding of First Nations, Métis, and Inuit culture as well as indigenization and the decolonization of pedagogy to uplift and support Indigenous students. A valid driver’s license is required for any off campus courses. Appointment periods include additional days in recognition of preparation and marking time required. Courses may be cancelled due to low enrolment. Application Process : Submit your cover letter and curriculum vitae to ecur_flt_posting@usask.ca . Note that this position is not in-scope and, as such, the CUPE 3287 Application Form is neither relevant nor required. If you are currently employed by the University of Saskatchewan in a full-time non-teaching position, discuss your application with your people leader to ensure the appointment is suitable relative to your current position. Department: Curriculum Studies Status: Term Employment Group: Out of Scope Sessional Lecturer Academic Calendar: Course Dates and Academic Calendar Stipend: CUPE 3287 Stipends Posted Date: 10/7/2026 Closing Date: 11/18/2026 Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15663?c=usask": {
      "title": "Sessional Lecturer, Management & Marketing, COMM 401",
      "description": "Sessional Lecturer, Management & Marketing, COMM 401 | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Sessional Lecturer, Management & Marketing, COMM 401 Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15663 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/7/2026 Back to Search Sessional Lecturer, Management & Marketing, COMM 401 The Department of Management and Marketing in the Edwards School of Business invites applications for Sessional Lecturers to teach the following courses during the following session(s): 2026-2027 Regular Session – Term 2 (Winter Session) Course Number, Section, and Name: COMM 401.3 (02) and (06) – Business Strategy Course Description: An integrative course which focuses on the functions and responsibilities of senior management. Deals with the concept of organizational strategy and how it is formulated, developed and implemented in real-life situations. Term and Course Dates: Term 2 (January - April) CRN: 21057, 21063 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Section 02: Tuesdays and Thursdays; 11:30 am – 12:50 pm Section 06: Mondays and Wednesdays; 1:00 pm – 2:20 pm Expected Enrollment Limit: 50 in each section Location: Saskatoon Qualifications: Consistent with its goal of maintaining business school accreditation, Edwards School of Business will recruit individuals with the following qualifications: A graduate or professional degree, or professional certification, in a field relevant to the course. Relevant professional experience and ongoing professional engagement clearly linked to the field in which the candidate is expected to teach. Professional engagement activities must be substantive and sustained at levels that support currency and relevance for Edwards' mission. Previous teaching experience is an asset. A valid driver’s license is required for any off-campus courses. Appointment periods include additional days in recognition of preparation and marking time required. Courses may be cancelled due to low enrolment. Application Process : Complete the CUPE 3287 Employment Application Form , specify the position(s) for which you are applying, and send the application along with your curriculum vitae (if applicable) to marketing.sessional@edwards.usask.ca . If you are currently employed by the University of Saskatchewan in a full-time non-teaching position, discuss your application with your people leader to ensure the appointment is suitable relative to your current position. Department: Management & Marketing Status: Term Employment Group: CUPE 3287 Academic Calendar: Course Dates and Academic Calendar Stipend: CUPE 3287 Stipends Posted Date: 10/7/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 Number of Openings: 2 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15662?c=usask": {
      "title": "Sessional Lecturer, Sociology, SOC 112 (North Battleford)",
      "description": "Sessional Lecturer, Sociology, SOC 112 (North Battleford) | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Sessional Lecturer, Sociology, SOC 112 (North Battleford) North Battleford, SK, Canada | req15662 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/7/2026 Back to Search Sessional Lecturer, Sociology, SOC 112 (North Battleford) The Department of Sociology in the College of Arts and Science and the Gwenna Moss Centre for Teaching and Learning invite applications for a Sessional Lecturer to teach the following off-campus courses during the following session(s): Winter 2027 Session Course Number, Section, and Name: SOC 112.3 (C50) Foundations in Sociology Social Construction of Everyday Life Term and Course Dates: Term 2 (January 1, 2027 to April 30, 2027) CRN: 22654 Delivery Mode: This course will be offered as an In Person Lecture. Course Schedule: Actual course days/times Monday 1:00 PM to 3:50 PM. Expected Enrollment Limit: 30 Location: North West College; North Battleford, SK Qualifications: Minimum of a Master’s degree or equivalent in Sociology or related discipline and and/or possession of relevant teaching or professional experience. A valid driver’s license is required for any off-campus courses. Appointment periods include additional days in recognition of preparation and marking time required. Courses may be cancelled due to low enrolment. Application Process : Complete the CUPE 3287 Employment Application Form , specify the position(s) for which you are applying, and send the application along with your curriculum vitae (if applicable) to soc_flt_posting@usask.ca . If you are currently employed by the University of Saskatchewan in a full-time non-teaching position, discuss your application with your people leader to ensure the appointment is suitable relative to your current position. Department: Sociology Status: Term Employment Group: CUPE 3287 Academic Calendar: Course Dates and Academic Calendar Stipend: CUPE 3287 Stipends Posted Date: 10/7/2026 Closing Date: 10/28/2026 Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15675?c=usask": {
      "title": "Sessional Lecturers (St. Thomas More College)",
      "description": "Sessional Lecturers (St. Thomas More College) | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Sessional Lecturers (St. Thomas More College) Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15675 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/6/2026 Back to Search Sessional Lecturers (St. Thomas More College) St Thomas More College (STM), the Catholic liberal arts college federated with the University of Saskatchewan, invites applications for the following sessional lecturer positions for the 2027 Winter session: DEPARTMENT OF PHILOSOPHY Applications should be addressed and sent to: Dr. Daniel Regnier, STM Department Head, Department of Philosophy, dregnier@stmcollege.ca SUB NUM SEC CAP TITLE TYPE DAY START END TERM PHIL 140.3 62 90 Critical Thinking LEC T TH 13:00 14:20 T2 Course Dates: Term 2: January 1, 2027 – April 30, 2027 The Irene and Doug Schmeiser Centre for Faith, Reason, Peace, and Justice Applications should be addressed and sent to: Dr. Cynthia Wallace, STM Director, The Schmeiser Centre, cwallace@stmcollege.ca SUB NUM SEC CAP TITLE TYPE DAY START END TERM CPSJ 112.3 62 35 Introduction to Peace Studies LEC M W F 10:30 11:20 T2 CPSJ 112.3 64 35 Introduction to Peace Studies LEC T TH 13:00 14:20 T2 Course Dates: Term 2: January 1, 2027 – April 30, 2027 DUTIES: The duties of a sessional lecturer include teaching as described in Article 14.2.3, and includes marking assignments and examinations, supervising the work of marking assistants (if applicable), and holding regular office hours. APPLICATION PROCESS: New Candidates (those who have never taught for STM and those who have had a break in service longer than twenty-four months) must submit an application which includes the following: a cover letter specifying the courses for which they are applying, describing teaching and research strengths. Candidates submitting an application for more than one course are required to state a ranked order of teaching preference. This information will be considered in the recommendation and appointment process, a current curriculum vitae; all stated contact information on the CV (including the email address, phone number and home address at which applicants can be reached) must be current to enable contacting applicants, a written response to the STM Mission Statement (see template ); copies of transcripts of qualifying degree, teaching dossier , including copies of most recent teaching evaluations (both by students and peers); if the applicant has previously taught the course for which they are applying, teaching evaluations from that course should be made available, a response to comments from student evaluations (see template ) , two letters of reference, specifying teaching-related experience (please have them sent directly to the Department Head as soon as possible ; applications without reference letters will not be considered). Returning Candidates (those who have taught at STM within the last twenty-four months) must submit an application which includes the following: a cover letter specifying the courses for which they are applying, describing teaching and research strengths. Candidates submitting an application for more than one course are required to state a ranked order of teaching preference. This information will be considered in the recommendation and appointment process, an updated curriculum vitae; all stated contact information on the CV (including the email address, phone number and home address at which applicants can be reached) must be current to enable contacting applicants ( do not use your STM email address or the STM mailing address as your primary contact) , Copies of most recent teaching evaluations (both by students and peers, including below threshold SLEQs); if the applicant has previously taught the course for which they are applying, teaching evaluations specific to that course should be made available, a response to comments from student evaluations (see template ) . STIPENDS: We offer competitive stipends and a compensation package that is comparable with the University of Saskatchewan. QUALIFICATIONS: Preference will be given to candidates with a Ph.D. in the discipline.  Demonstrated experience in the area of the course is expected.  Candidates with a Master’s Degree and appropriate experience will also be considered.  St. Thomas More College recognizes seniority of sessional lecturers in accordance with Article 5.1.10.8 of its Collective Agreement. In accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, this advertisement is directed in the first instance to Canadian citizens and permanent residents. St. Thomas More College (STM) is committed to diversity within its faculty. Women, Indigenous peoples, people with disabilities, visible minorities and members of other designated groups are encouraged to self-identify on their application. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.  Additional information on STM College and the University of Saskatchewan is available on the STM website ( http://www.stmcollege.ca ) While we appreciate all interest in these positions, only short-listed candidates will be contacted. Department: Saint Thomas More Status: Term Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Posted Date: 10/6/2026 Closing Date: 10/20/2026 Number of Openings: 3 #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15661?c=usask": {
      "title": "Procurement Officer, Enterprise Procurement",
      "description": "Procurement Officer, Enterprise Procurement | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Procurement Officer, Enterprise Procurement Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15661 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/5/2026 Back to Search Procurement Officer, Enterprise Procurement The University of Saskatchewan has some of the most innovative people doing groundbreaking research and pedagogy. The Procurement Officer is a key member of the Enterprise Procurement team that provides strategic analysis and support to colleges and units in order to procure the goods and services they need to achieve their goals, while balancing value, timeliness, risk, and cost. The university offers flexible work arrangements; access to the university’s recreation services; ongoing professional development and mentorship and a comprehensive benefits and pension program. Primary Purpose: The purpose of this position is to support faculty, staff, and researchers across the university in meeting their procurement and logistical needs through the provision of advice, guidance, execution, and exceptional service. Nature of Work: Working in a cross-functional team environment, the Procurement Officer is accountable for collaborating with colleges/units to facilitate the procurement process by assessing needs, gathering requirements, guiding vendor selection, and supporting the effective execution of the contracting processes within established thresholds in accordance with university policy and guidelines and relevant legislation. Also, this position offers feedback and potential solutions for process improvements that support continuous enhancements to the service performance of Enterprise Procurement. Accountabilities: Ensure requests for the procurement of goods, services, and construction that are received are supported through to completion of the source to pay cycle, including verifying and obtaining the appropriate authorizations. Build and maintain relationships with internal and external stakeholders. Execute and record purchase orders based on approved terms and conditions or agreements while ensuring due diligence in the procurement process. Negotiate with vendors and partners to achieve best value for the university. Proactively seek opportunities to add value to the university, through cost savings, revenue generation, enhanced value for money, higher quality services, or any similar opportunity. Liaise with colleagues and stakeholders to ensure seamless, timely and efficient resolution and/or routing of outstanding procurement enquiries and associated administrative processing. Respond to and resolve procurement enquiries and associated administrative processes through use of multiple procurement and supply management resources and tracking systems, software applications and technical support tools (e.g., knowledge base, case management system). Support the implementation of category management and category strategies developed by Category Specialists. Evaluate processes and identify opportunities to continually improve Enterprise Procurement services and operations. Work effectively with cross-functional teams to ensure a seamless procure to pay process and experience. Reduce institutional and contract risk by remaining current with and adhering to university policies, guidelines and processes, collective agreements and relevant legislation including trends that may have an impact on procurement. Qualifications Education: Completion of a relevant post-secondary program. An SCMP designation, or work towards one, would be considered an asset. An equivalent combination of education and directly related experience may be considered. Experience: Experience in a procurement, administrative, or customer service role is ideal. Demonstrated use of problem solving, and negotiation skills with successful outcomes. Experience working in a diverse and inclusive environment is required. Familiarity with e-procurement processes and systems is considered an asset. Experience with public procurement considered an asset, but not required. Skills: Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, both written and oral; strong customer service skills; demonstrated proficiency in analytical and negotiation skills; demonstrated ability to problem solve under deadline pressures and competing priorities; demonstrated ability in handling procurement within an ERP system; proven Microsoft Office application skills, including SharePoint and expert MS Excel skills; case management system experience would be considered an asset; and demonstrated collaboration capabilities within a team environment. Department: Enterprise Procurement Status: Term 12 months with the possibility of extension Employment Group: ASPA Shift: Mon-Fri, 8:00am - 4:30pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 10/5/2026 Closing Date: 10/19/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
