    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15626?c=usask": {
      "title": "Peace Officer, Protective Services",
      "description": "Peace Officer, Protective Services | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Peace Officer, Protective Services Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15626 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/30/2026 Back to Search Peace Officer, Protective Services Summary: The peace officer performs proactive and reactive general patrol duties as part of a platoon providing 24 hour/365 days per year service to the campus community. Through engagement with the campus community, the peace officer protects life and property, prevents crime, and engages in problem-solving initiatives. This role enforces and investigates violations of federal and provincial laws, and university bylaws in accordance with authorities. Additionally, the peace officer provides information to victims, colleges, and units regarding non-criminal incidents which require a timely response. The peace officer frequently works with other first responder agencies and community stakeholders and may be required to operate in the role of incident commander in the event of an emergency. Duties and Responsibilities: Responds to events in progress, health incidents, traffic collisions, alarms, campus events, disasters, emergencies, or other situations which require intervention. Initiates investigations resulting from complaints, assigned files, or observation of activity. Applies investigative procedures such as gathering information, interviewing witnesses and suspects, taking statements, seizing evidence, requesting specialized services, and concluding investigations. Enforces federal and provincial laws, and university bylaws, processes arrests, prepares cases, and presents evidence in court. Addresses and problem-solves immediate conditions that give rise to public safety issues such as crime, social disorder, and fear of crime. Gathers and processes intelligence through field interviews and observation. Performs patrol functions such as attending complaints, executing warrants, investigating suspicious persons and activities, obtaining and recording information relevant to assigned duties, and performing traffic enforcement. Provides qualitative advice and support to victims. Builds relationships and partnerships with community members to understand their needs. Completes and submits required reports promptly and accurately. Conducts assigned duties and patrols using bicycles, on foot, or in marked vehicles. Participates in special projects to enhance the efficiency and effectiveness of the unit. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. In the absence of a Corporal or Senior Peace Officer, Peace Officers will assume responsibility for ensuring training continues as scheduled. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: Education/Experience: A certificate or diploma in policing, sociology, psychology or a related field; a bachelor’s degree is preferred. Minimum of two years’ experience in law enforcement, security, or related field. Successful completion of the Police Officer Physical Abilities Test (POPAT) or equivalent is mandatory. Licenses: A valid class 5 driver’s license. Certification in CPR First-Aid Level C. Certification in Verbal Judo and Defensive Tactics is preferred. Skills: Strong interpersonal and communications skills including clear verbal articulation, writing, and listening skills. Ability to remain professional, composed, and organized under pressure or in high-stress situations. High degree of professionalism and integrity. Ability to work independently, take initiative, and multi-task efficiently and effectively. Capacity to coach, guide, and instruct new peace officers in departmental operations. Ability to build relationships and work effectively and collaboratively with a team. Proficiency with technology and can easily adapt to technological changes. Hiring Process: Qualified applicants must complete a pre-employment mental aptitude examination. Extensive background checks are completed by the Hiring Manager. References are contacted to determine if an applicant's personal history, traits, and characteristics are suitable for a career in law enforcement. People contacted for these interviews include present and past employers and colleagues, family members, long-time friends, neighbours, and landlords. Department: Protective Services Status: Term 1 year with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: CUPE 1975 Shift: Working hours will be a 5 5 4 scheduled rotation (following completion of initial training and orientation). Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $30.25 - 38.38 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Security Services Salary Phase/Band: Phase 5 Posted Date: 9/30/2026 Closing Date: 11/4/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 3 Work Location: On Campus The successful applicant will be required to provide the following current verification(s) where 'Yes' is indicated below. Further information is available at: https://careers.usask.ca/tips-for-applying.php Criminal Record Check: Yes Driver's License and Abstract Check: Not Applicable Education/Credential Verification: Not Applicable Vulnerable Sector Check: Yes The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15657?c=usask": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant, Administrative Network",
      "description": "Executive Assistant, Administrative Network | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Executive Assistant, Administrative Network Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15657 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/29/2026 Back to Search Executive Assistant, Administrative Network Summary: The executive assistant provides a wide range of confidential support to one or more senior leaders. Key responsibilities include coordinating complex calendars, providing comprehensive meeting support, and handling confidential information with discretion. Acting as the primary contact for the senior leader and various stakeholders, this position drafts, reviews, and sends communications on behalf of the leader. The executive assistant also plays a crucial role in initiative tracking, preparing presentations and briefing documents, and conducting research to aid decision-making. This position provides strategic monitoring of the senior leader’s initiatives and priorities and plays a crucial role in ensuring the efficiency and effectiveness of the office. Duties and Responsibilities: Provides strategic calendar management by coordinating complex schedules, establishing standards that balance meeting and office time, reflect priorities, and allow for flexibility. Provides advanced email management by monitoring, triaging, organizing, prioritizing and responding to emails on the leader’s behalf or as a delegate, ensuring timely and appropriate communication. Provides comprehensive meeting preparation with briefings, updates, materials, and information, while tracking deadlines, commitments, and emerging issues. Tracks initiatives and priorities, ensuring they go through the right channels, are included in relevant meetings, and are added to the appropriate agendas. Gathers information, creates, drafts, edits, and files a variety of documents, presentations, reports, and correspondence, ensuring confidential and sensitive information is handled appropriately. Provides meeting and committee support by coordinating meeting logistics, preparing the committee chair, creating and distributing agendas and documentation, taking minutes, tracking and following up on action items, setting up video conferencing tools, and troubleshooting technical issues for smooth communication during meetings. Coordinates assigned projects and tasks, developing collective knowledge and delegating work, and engaging colleagues. Contributes to continuous improvement initiatives by identifying issues and participating in process evaluation and re-design. Serves as the primary point of contact for the office and leaders, utilizing clear and effective communication, addressing inquiries, and providing direction in alignment with organizational values and priorities. Organizes complex travel arrangements, itineraries, submits, and approves travel and other expense requests. Coordinates and oversees all event logistics, including budget management, catering, and invitations. Builds and maintains positive relationships with key internal and external stakeholders, working collaboratively with administrative network colleagues. Processes financial transactions and reconciliations. Assists with human resource processes such as submitting job postings, scheduling interviews, submitting letters of offer, assisting with onboarding and offboarding processes, and processing changes to employment records. Provides general office administrative support for efficient and effective operations. Greets and assists visitors, providing a welcoming and professional environment. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all community members, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion, and responds to different perspectives with curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: An office administration diploma; a bachelor’s degree is preferred. Minimum of five years of senior-level administrative experience, preferably supporting executive members in a large, complex organization. Proven ability to manage executive level calendars, communications, and confidential information with a high degree of discretion and professionalism. Exceptional communication skills, including the ability to draft and edit high level correspondence and reports. Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to build and foster effective relationships with diverse internal and external stakeholder groups. Ability to act with a high degree of professionalism, discretion, and navigate sensitive matters with tact and diplomacy. Advanced organizational and time management skills with the ability to anticipate needs, prioritize tasks and manage multiple demands independently. Demonstrates resilience and adaptability in high-pressure environments through a proactive, self-directed approach to learning and problem-solving; committed to continuous improvement and aligning actions with evolving best practices and strategic priorities. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and the ability to quickly learn and adapt to new technologies and systems. Experience with university systems is preferred. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Department: Administrative Network Status: Permanent Employment Group: Exempt Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $54,520.00 - 95,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Phase/Band: Salary Band 2 Posted Date: 9/29/2026 Closing Date: 10/20/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15647?c=usask": {
      "title": "Health Sciences Librarian",
      "description": "Health Sciences Librarian | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Health Sciences Librarian Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15647 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/28/2026 Back to Search Health Sciences Librarian Applications are invited from qualified individuals for a 0.5 FTE five-year term librarian position with the University Library. We are seeking a dynamic, innovative, and flexible individual with the appropriate skills and qualifications to work collaboratively as part of our team. The appointee will serve as part of the health sciences librarian team with liaison responsibilities for the College of Pharmacy and Nutrition and will contribute to library-wide projects and priorities. The focus of the position will be on instruction and research support to students and faculty in the assigned areas. Reporting to the Dean, the successful candidate will be responsible for: Providing high quality services that support teaching, learning, and research in their liaison areas including: designing and delivering information literacy instruction and programming. providing information services, including one-on-one consultations, on a wide variety of topics including literature searching, knowledge syntheses, scholarly communication, research data management. developing high quality collections of digital and print resources in assigned disciplinary areas. Building collaborative relationships with campus community to advance learning and research. Working as part of teams across the Library to advance strategic areas such as knowledge synthesis, open scholarship, student experience, and decolonization of services, collections and spaces. The Library is currently developing a new strategic plan to guide its work in the coming years. Each librarian at the University Library will contribute to the implementation of this new plan by working as a member of one of the library’s four divisions (Learning and Curriculum Support Division, Research Support Services Division, Collections and Discovery Division, University Archives and Special Collections Division). The assignment to a division is based on a combination of the candidate’s experience, interest, and the needs of the library. Qualifications An ALA-accredited Master of Library and Information Science degree or international equivalent is required for this appointment. Professional librarian experience is preferred. Experience or education related to health sciences disciplines and/or health sciences librarianship is an asset. The Search Committee welcomes applications from candidates who demonstrate knowledge and experience in some, but not all, of the areas listed below. Candidates who are excited about this role are encouraged to apply. We are seeking candidates who can demonstrate: Experience and/or knowledge in providing high quality research support, such as literature searching, knowledge synthesis, scholarly communication, and research data management; Experience teaching and/or knowledge of effective teaching practices; Building and sustaining effective and collaborative professional working relationships and partnerships; The ability to take initiative and be a self-starter; Flexibility and creativity; and An awareness of current trends and challenges in health sciences librarianship in academic libraries. Salary, Benefits, and Application Information Salary bands, based on 1.0 FTE, for the 2026-2027 academic year are as follows: Assistant Librarian: $95,187 to $113,527 Associate Librarian: $113,527 to $135,535 Librarian: $135,525 to $164,879 It is anticipated that this position will be filled at the Assistant Librarian level. Starting salary will be prorated to 0.5 FTE and is calculated from the base salary with consideration given for previous librarian experience. This position includes a comprehensive benefits package: dental, health and extended vision care plan; pension plan, life insurance (compulsory and voluntary), academic long-term disability, sick leave, travel insurance, death benefits, an employee assistance program, a professional expense allowance, and a flexible health and wellness spending program. Interested candidates must submit, via email and preferably as a single PDF, a cover letter highlighting qualifications and accomplishments, and a detailed curriculum vitae to: Lori Birrell University Librarian and Dean University Library, University of Saskatchewan Email: library.jobs@usask.ca Due to federal immigration requirements, we also ask candidates to indicate whether they are Canadian citizens, permanent residents, or are otherwise already authorized to work at this position for the duration of the appointment, with an explanation if this last category is indicated. The review of applications will begin on October 20, 2026. Applications received after this date will not be considered. The anticipated start date is January 16, 2027. About Saskatoon Saskatoon is situated along the banks of the South Saskatchewan River, on Treaty 6 Territory and the Homeland of the Métis. Saskatoon is a vibrant, livable city known for its natural beauty, friendly community, and diverse cultural and leisure offerings. Although Saskatoon is the largest urban centre in the province with a population of over 300,000 its residents enjoy short commute times and some of the lowest apartment rental and housing prices in the country. About the University of Saskatchewan The University of Saskatchewan is one of the top research-intensive, medical doctoral universities in Canada, and is home to world-leading research in areas of global importance, such as water and food security and infectious diseases. Guided by our University Plan: The University the World Needs , we work together across disciplines and with our communities to find creative solutions to the most pressing global challenges. USask has a reputation for excellence in teaching, research, and scholarly activities and offers a full range of undergraduate, graduate, and professional programs to a student population of over 25,000. About the University Library As one of Canada’s leading research libraries, the University Library serves as the intellectual hub of USask, providing resources, services, and spaces that facilitate inquiry, discovery, and knowledge creation. Our Strategic Framework focuses on building scholars and decolonizing the library. The University Library is committed to developing welcoming, responsive, and accessible services and resources to support our diverse clientele, with a strategic focus on Indigenous peoples. The University Library has a robust digital library and is physically distributed across six locations on campus to support the learning, teaching, and research communities. We are structured in four functional divisions: Collections and Discovery, Learning and Curriculum Support, Research Support Services, University Archives and Special Collections. For more information, visit the University Library website . Status: Term Employment Group: Out of Scope Faculty Member Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 0.5 Posted Date: 9/28/2026 Closing Date: 10/20/2026 The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15640?c=usask": {
      "title": "Building Automation Specialist, Campus Operations",
      "description": "Building Automation Specialist, Campus Operations | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Building Automation Specialist, Campus Operations Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15640 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/28/2026 Back to Search Building Automation Specialist, Campus Operations Summary: The building automation specialist designs, programs, commissions, and supports building automation systems that monitor and control mechanical building systems across the university campus. This position contributes specialized technical expertise in controls design, system integration, programming, commissioning, and lifecycle optimization of building automation infrastructure. Working collaboratively with project managers, consultants, contractors, and internal stakeholders, the specialist develops reliable and sustainable controls solutions that support occupant comfort, operational effectiveness, energy management, and campus sustainability objectives. This role influences projects with university-wide impact and provides advanced technical guidance related to automation systems, networks, and controls strategies. Duties and Responsibilities: Designs building automation and control systems for new construction, renovation, and infrastructure renewal projects, developing control strategies, system architecture, and technical solutions that support operational performance, occupant comfort, and energy management objectives. Develops control sequences, programs automation systems, configures databases, and creates graphical user interfaces that facilitate effective monitoring, operation, and optimization of building systems. Provides technical leadership throughout project planning, implementation, startup, and commissioning activities. Estimates project costs related to controls equipment, internal labour, and contractor services, monitors expenditures, and recommends actions to address cost variances and emerging issues. Addresses complex technical requirements and challenges in collaboration with project managers, consultants, contractors, and operational stakeholders. Reviews consultant designs, specifications, and sequences of operation, recommending modifications that align with university standards, operational requirements, and long-term sustainability objectives. Coordinates and participates in commissioning activities, validating system performance, optimizing building operations, and resolving deficiencies identified during project implementation and occupancy. Produces AutoCAD drawings, technical documentation, and related materials that communicate design intent and support project execution, operations, and maintenance activities. Investigates and resolves complex building automation, controls, networking, and system integration issues, providing expert technical guidance to maintenance personnel and other stakeholders. Evaluates automation technologies, controls equipment, and system enhancements using operational, lifecycle, reliability, and cost considerations, recommending solutions that maximize long-term value and system performance. Delivers technical training, knowledge transfer, and consultation to colleagues and stakeholders to strengthen the operation and maintenance of building automation systems. Maintains current knowledge of building automation technologies, industry standards, codes, and regulatory requirements, incorporating leading practices into system design, programming, and operational support activities. Advances campus sustainability initiatives through automation system design, operation, and continuous improvement activities. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: A technologist diploma in a related field. Minimum of five years of related experience. Advanced knowledge of building automation systems, controls architecture, and control strategies. Advanced knowledge of heating, ventilation, and air conditioning (HVAC), electrical, mechanical, and refrigeration systems. Knowledge of university policies, planning processes, and sustainability principles is an asset. Strong understanding of computer systems, networking principles, and communication protocols. Ability to analyze complex technical issues and develop practical solutions. Effective communication skills with the ability to translate technical concepts for varied audiences. Ability to manage multiple projects and competing priorities. Strong relationship building and stakeholder engagement skills. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a team. Proficiency in automation programming, database configuration, graphics development, and systems integration. Proficient computer skills, including Microsoft Office applications and project management software; experience with AutoCAD and technical drawing development. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Department: Campus Operations Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Shift: Mon-Fri, 8:00-4:30pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $69,035.00 - 107,868.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 2 Posted Date: 9/28/2026 Closing Date: 10/26/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15618?c=usask": {
      "title": "Marketing and Communications Coordinator, Crop Development Centre",
      "description": "Marketing and Communications Coordinator, Crop Development Centre | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Marketing and Communications Coordinator, Crop Development Centre Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15618 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/28/2026 Back to Search Marketing and Communications Coordinator, Crop Development Centre Summary About the Crop Development Centre The Crop Development Centre (CDC) exists to turn momentum into impact through the continuous advancement of Canadian seed. Since 1971, for more than 55 years, the CDC has carried discovery forward from foundational research through breeding, testing, training, and commercialization in one connected system. That integration is what sets the CDC apart. The CDC’s work spans pulses, cereals, oilseeds, and specialty crops. The centre develops varieties that perform under real conditions, licenses them through industry partnerships, and returns that value to farmers, the province, and the broader agricultural system. Embedded within the Department of Plant Sciences in the College of Agriculture and Bioresources, the CDC runs large breeding programs across more than 40 crops, and its teams grow to more than 300 people through the summer field season. This role sits on the small central team that supports communications, brand, and administration across the whole centre. The CDC holds strong brand equity rooted in trust, independence, and practical outcomes. The centre has a recently updated brand foundation and a growing set of assets, and is building out the templates, content, and materials that bring that brand to life across every channel. Nature of Work The Communications Coordinator supports how the CDC tells its story to seed growers and licensees, industry partners and commodity groups, funders and government, and university leadership. Reporting to the Manager, Crop Development Centre, the coordinator assists in the development, coordination, and delivery of internal and external communications, producing and distributing a wide range of content and designed assets that keep the CDC visible, consistent, and current across platforms. The coordinator works collaboratively with the CDC Manager, the CDC Director, breeders, program staff, and the College of Agriculture and Bioresources communications team to promote programs, events, and achievements through storytelling and campaign support, while keeping every piece aligned to the CDC and University of Saskatchewan brand guidelines. The Manager sets communications strategy and priorities, and the coordinator carries them out, turning direction into finished content, assets, and events. This is a hands-on, execution-focused role where roles are broad, priorities shift, and follow-through matters. Flexibility for occasional work outside normal hours is required, and travel within Saskatchewan to field sites and industry events is required at times. This is primarily a CDC role, however the coordinator also provides some communications support to the Department of Plant Sciences, including the department newsletter, social media management, and website updates. Key Responsibilities Contributes to the development and execution of CDC's annual marketing and communications plans in support of the priorities set by the CDC Manager. Drafts, edits, and distributes content across the CDC's channels, including website updates, social media posts, newsletter articles, research impact stories, variety release announcements, and program updates, and monitors engagement metrics. Produces the CDC semi-annual newsletter, including writing, layout, and distribution, under the direction of the CDC Manager, as well as support for the Plant Sciences quarterly internal newsletter. Designs and produces print and digital assets within the CDC brand system, such as social graphics, website banners, event display banners, pull-up flags, tradeshow materials, brochures, and branded templates. Applies the CDC and University of Saskatchewan brand and style guidelines consistently across all materials. Captures and coordinates photography and videography that reflects the CDC's work in real field conditions, including liaising with external photographers and travel to trial sites as required. Assists in managing the CDC's photo and video assets, including organizing archives and coordinating shoots. Schedules and monitors the CDC and Plant Sciences social media content, currently LinkedIn and X (Twitter), and helps grow engagement. Builds, maintains, and expands the CDC's website within the university’s content management system (Cascade), working within its constraints, alongside the CDC Manager and updates the Plant Sciences website occasionally. Supports the CDC's presence at the annual Crop Production Show, Ag in Motion, field days, and industry events, including materials, logistics, and on-site coordination. Provides logistical and administrative support for communications projects and events. Works closely with the CDC Manager, CDC Director, breeders, researchers, and program staff whose work the role helps communicate, and collaborates with the College of Agriculture and Bioresources communications team on shared initiatives and brand alignment. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications A bachelor's degree in communications, marketing, graphic design, journalism, public relations, or related field. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths, and an equivalent combination of education and experience may be considered. Two or more years of related experience in communications or marketing. Strong writing, editing, and proofreading skills, with the ability to make technical work clear and credible for non-specialist audiences. Design skills and a strong visual eye, with experience producing print and digital assets within an established brand system using Canva and the Adobe Creative Suite (InDesign, Illustrator, Photoshop). Familiarity with social media platforms and content management systems, including content scheduling and basic analytics. Strong organizational skills and the ability to manage multiple projects and meet deadlines. Attention to detail, sound judgment, critical thinking, and professionalism in all communications. Ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a small, collaborative team where roles are broad and priorities shift. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications. Comfortable using AI tools in a communications and marketing workflow, and eager to help shape how the CDC uses them (Microsoft Co-Pilot, Claude, Gemini etc.) Willing and able to attend field events, tradeshows, and industry days, with occasional work outside regular hours (minimal) and travel within Saskatchewan as required. Preferred Qualifications Interest in or curiosity about agriculture, agri-food or crop science. Experience with email marketing platforms and newsletter production, such as Mailchimp. Photography skills would be an asset. How to Apply Submit your application through the University of Saskatchewan careers portal. Please include: A cover letter telling us why this work interests you and what you would bring to it. A current resume or CV. Two or three samples that demonstrate both your writing and your design work (links to published work or a portfolio are welcome). Department: Crop Development Centre Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 9/28/2026 Closing Date: 10/16/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15236?c=usask": {
      "title": "Emergency and Urgent Care Veterinarian, WCVM",
      "description": "Emergency and Urgent Care Veterinarian, WCVM | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Emergency and Urgent Care Veterinarian, WCVM Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15236 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/25/2026 Back to Search Emergency and Urgent Care Veterinarian, WCVM Summary: The emergency and urgent care veterinarian has primary care responsibility; providing high-quality emergency and urgent care to small and exotic animals presenting to a veterinary teaching hospital. This role focuses on timely case management, effective collaboration with specialists, and clear communication with clients and referring veterinarians. This position involves active supervision, instruction of senior veterinary students during clinical rotations, working with interns and contributing to hands-on learning in an emergency care setting. The veterinarian supports hospitalized patient care while maintaining accurate medical documentation and fostering a positive, team-oriented clinical environment. Duties and Responsibilities: Provides high-quality care to client-owned animals presenting for emergency or urgent treatment. Oversees and delivers high-quality care to hospitalized patients in an acute care setting within a referral teaching hospital. Promotes clear, effective communication among team members and across services in a collaborative and positive manner. Builds and maintains strong professional relationships with clients, referring veterinarians, technicians, and other clinicians. Maintains accurate, timely, and comprehensive medical records. Provides educational opportunities and mentors veterinary technicians, veterinary students, and postgraduate veterinarians, including rotating interns. Delivers feedback and completes formal assessments and grading for veterinary students in emergency and urgent care rotations. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: A Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) degree or equivalent. Licensed (or able to obtain licensing) with the Saskatchewan Veterinary Medical Association. Advanced training in emergency medicine is preferred. Minimum of two years of clinical practice experience or an internship. Experience in clinical instruction, teaching, or mentorship is an asset. Strong communication and interpersonal abilities. Effective organizational and time-management capabilities. High attention to detail and accuracy. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a team. Successful candidate will also receive a clinical supplement for on call/after-hours clinical work in the amount of $300 per pay period or $7,200 per year. Comprehensive benefit package provided; overview and plan summary can be found here . Department: Small Animal Clinical Sciences Status: Term 6 months with the possibility of extension Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $87,266.00 - 145,353.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 3 Posted Date: 9/25/2026 Closing Date: 11/6/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15635?c=usask": {
      "title": "Pre-Clerkship Coordinator, College of Medicine (Regina)",
      "description": "Pre-Clerkship Coordinator, College of Medicine (Regina) | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Pre-Clerkship Coordinator, College of Medicine (Regina) Regina, SK, Canada | req15635 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/24/2026 Back to Search Pre-Clerkship Coordinator, College of Medicine (Regina) Primary Purpose: Provide central oversight and manage the administration of Pre-clerkship in the Undergraduate Medical Education program on the Regina Campus.  Ensure seamless and consistent program delivery across at all sites and compliance with accreditation standards. Nature of Work: Reporting to the Associate Dean, Regina Campus and Associate Dean - Undergraduate Medical Education (UGME) through the Finance and Administration Manager for the Regina Campus.  The Pre-clerkship Coordinator will build and maintain professional relationships with various stakeholders including students, faculty, departmental colleagues, and outside agencies.  This position will work as part of a team as well as independently.  This position requires the ability to work under pressure in a fast paced environment while balancing competing deadlines and multiple tasks.  Some travel within the province may be required. Working as an integral team member of the College of Medicine (CoM) undergraduate medical education (UGME), the Coordinator will liaise between the Saskatchewan Health Authority, Academic Health Sciences (AHS) in Regina and the College of Medicine to ensure the successful implementation of the pre-clerkship program in Regina. This will include hands on operational involvement in all aspects related to the pre-clerkship delivery in Regina. The Coordinator is also responsible for and accountable to ensure that all legal health and safety requirements are followed within the pre-clerkship program, Regina campus. Accountabilities: Contributing actively to problem-solving and decision making, including participating in team meetings. Managing any extraordinary issues or challenges that arise. Managing matters pertaining to the Regina pre-clerkship operations, including but not limited to: curriculum, curriculum delivery, and faculty involvement. Tracking, analyzing, reporting pertinent information: Recruitment needs. Responding to/clarifying standardized and volunteer patient statistics. Student movement through the undergraduate program. Physician involvement in pre-clerkship delivery. Lecture hours originating in Regina. Facilitating the delivery of the pre-clerkship program to ensure successful implementation in Regina in relation to: Collaborating with the provincial and local UGME team on planning curriculum delivery and implementing it at the Regina campus. Participating in relevant UGME program meetings and committees, both provincially and at the Regina campus. Coordinating delivery of some curriculum modules. Ensuring resources are in place for the module delivery; identifying resources needs to leadership. Contributing to the recruitment of Regina based faculty. Being aware of recruitment challenges and opportunities. Problem solving with the team to meet recruitment needs. Providing guidance related to medical education and processes. Assisting with student meetings and student documentation (relates to both professionalism and academic matters). In collaboration with the faculty Year Chair or Year Coordinator, monitoring student academic progress and challenges, including developing methods and work standards for monitoring academic progress. Facilitating remediation planning for Regina students, working with the Academic Support team and Office of Student Affairs. Faculty (physician and non-physician): Attending meeting with new faculty to promote teaching opportunities. Orienting and supporting Regina-based faculty to teaching expectations, tools, access to software, etc. Receiving concerns about faculty, conducting analysis of the concern, and presenting the concern to Regina-based CoM leadership for action. Identifying and sharing resource needs related to faculty to leadership. Developing and maintaining an excellent working relationship with student cohorts: Contributing to the provision of supportive and inclusive learning environments for students. Supporting the Regina-based student orientations. Supporting the student success at the Regina campus: Meeting with the Regina student representatives regularly to address concerns, to share information, and to discuss how to maintain a positive learning environment in Regina. Leading the Regina Pre-clerkship Feedback sessions. Participating in accommodation planning, remediation planning, professionalism reviewing, and other student meetings. Working with the OSA to identify and address student issues. Working with UGME program designates to review exams, identify knowledge gaps, and design learning or remediation plans to address student needs. Addressing individual student needs as they arise, collaborating with CoM and AHS to meet student needs. Obtaining constructive feedback from students as to their educational experience. Monitoring program feedback tool, attendance tracking, low (academic) performance flags and forwarding to the appropriate individual for action. Knowledge and expertise in all of the different student information systems currently being used. One45, Canvas, CONCUR, JIRA, MIDAS, etc. Education: A degree in a related field is required.  (primarily Health Care, Administration, Education, Adult Education, Human Resources, or Psychology). A combination of education and experience may be considered. Experience: At least 2-3 years of related experience. Experience working in an institutional environment would be considered an asset. Experience working and collaborating in diverse and inclusive settings is required. Skills: Strong evidence of relationship building, demonstrated professional maturity, judgment and decisiveness, exceptionally strong written, verbal, research, organizational and communication skills. Ability to locate needed resources, connect “supply and demand” and facilitate communication and development of sustained connections. Ability to take initiative and be self-directed, ability to work collaboratively, with polished presentation, written and interpersonal communication skills, proven strategic planning skills. Ability to foster a cooperative work environment within a complex partnership/organizational structure. Ability to provide technical guidance and leadership to professional staff and clientele in the field. Energetic, showing leadership in a highly collaborative manner. Demonstrated ability to think and act strategically and exercise diplomacy and creativity in solving issues. Proven ability and creativity in negotiating mutually beneficial solutions. Ability to make data-driven decisions and demonstrated commitment to quality. Demonstrated experience in project management and being highly organized. Proficiency with contemporary computer applications including web maintenance program. This position is in scope of the Administrative and Supervisory Personnel Association. Inquiries regarding this position can be directed to Annie Ethier (306) 766-4890. Department: College of Medicine Status: Term 15 months with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: ASPA Shift: Mon-Fri, 8:00-4:00 pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 9/23/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15628?c=usask": {
      "title": "Manager, Finance and Administration, Office of the Vice Dean Indigenous Health",
      "description": "Manager, Finance and Administration, Office of the Vice Dean Indigenous Health | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Manager, Finance and Administration, Office of the Vice Dean Indigenous Health Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15628 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/24/2026 Back to Search Manager, Finance and Administration, Office of the Vice Dean Indigenous Health The University is committed to employment equity, diversity, and inclusion, and is proud to support career opportunities for Indigenous Peoples to reflect the community we serve. We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Only Indigenous candidates will be considered for this position. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship with documentation is a condition of employment and a requirement to hold this position and the successful candidate must maintain those conditions throughout their employment. The Office of Vice Dean Indigenous Health (OVDIH) is a unit within the Dean’s Office at the College of Medicine. The office supports a coordinated approach to community engagement for priority projects of the college and works collaboratively across the college and provincial health system to ensure the high-level aspirations of the college and departments related to Indigenous Health are met. The office works closely with the Department of Indigenous Health and Wellness (DIHW) to help set up and grow the department in a sustainable and good way.  Indigenous-led establishment of partnerships through respectful Indigenous community engagement protocols, building access to networks of community partners, and determining respectful sustainable resource strategies, will be the distinctive work of the office and department. The OVDIH works with the DIHW internally and externally to uplift Indigenous concepts, methodologies, and pedagogies. Primary Purpose: The Manager, Finance and Administration, Office of the Vice Dean Indigenous Health, is accountable for providing management of financial and administrative functions of the office with major responsibilities for strategic planning, budgeting, financial reporting, and human resource management. The Manager will ensure operations are efficient, effective, and align with the University’s policy and procedures. This position is a key resource in supporting the Department of Indigenous Health and Wellness (DIHW) in strategic planning and implementation of major goals and objectives. The Manager will have a responsibility to work in collaboration with the College of Medicine and any of the partner organization’s financial teams. The Manager will play a lead role in the recruitment of staff, faculty and contracted resources within the OVDIH and DIHW including developing contracts, job profiles, participating in the interview process and managing the onboarding process. Nature of Work: Reporting to the Vice Dean of Indigenous Health and under the direction of the Provincial Department Head, the Manager is responsible for providing strategic and administrative leadership for the OVDIH, and the DIHW. This dual reporting role is responsible for all financial and administrative operations of the units. The Manager provides supervision and directs the work of staff in the financial, educational, administrative, and clinical areas. The Manager develops, monitors, and updates financial and administrative policies, procedures and processes, and recommends efficiencies to meet the group’s goals and objectives, while ensuring that all activities are compliant with university and college policies and procedures. The Manager is required to provide timely and innovative responses to continuous shifts in policy and direction. In providing various financial and administrative services, the Manager exercises discretion and professional judgment by maintaining confidential information, and applies sound practices with a consistent, proactive, collaborative approach. A high level of concentration is required for long-term planning and for dealing with multiple projects with competing priorities. Emerging and unpredictable events are to be dealt with in a timely manner. The Manager demonstrates resiliency while working in an environment where there is ambiguity, continuous change, and shifting priorities. Accountabilities and Responsibilities: Ensure that exposure to risk and liability is mitigated by ensuring compliance with relevant legislation, accounting standards, agreements, and university policies and procedures. Participates in strategic planning ensuring that developed goals align with the university and college goals, and contribute towards maximizing academic performance, clinical service, and research productivity. Oversee all administrative staff (ASPA & CUPE) including direction and assignment of duties, performance reviews, feedback, mentoring, orientation, training, disciplinary action and dismissals. Lead and administer recruitment and remuneration of OVDIH staff and faculty including developing job profiles, interacting with human resources, interviewing and the hiring process. Provide financial insight and timely financial reconciliation of accounts, processing of payments, invoices, and accurate reporting for all areas of responsibility. Create and implement long and short-term strategies to address the needs and responsibilities of the Office Vice Dean Indigenous Health. Provide accurate and timely budget preparation, manage revenues and expenditures. Prepare quarterly and annual reporting for the College of Medicine financial operations. Present and convey accurate information and data to ensure financially viable and sustainable decision-making and problem-solving. Ensure awareness and adherence to collective bargaining agreements. Collaborate with the staff of Office Vice Dean Indigenous Health and of Partner administrative and academic units to identify and implement the program management initiatives required to strategically meet the objectives of the College of Medicine. Builds a management culture based on teamwork, open communication, prudent risk taking, innovation, mentoring and accountability. Represent the Office Vice Dean Indigenous Health on committees and strategy teams for high-level special programs and projects. Advise the Office Vice Dean Indigenous Health staff on policy, new initiatives, and emerging challenges. Qualifications: A relevant university degree, preferably in Commerce, Business, or related field, along with a minimum of five (5) years of leadership experience with demonstrated excellence in leading people and finance management of a complex and diverse fund source organization such as a health care or post-secondary education environment. Proven ability to build relationships and work collaboratively with team members from different areas of expertise, and with senior administrators and leaders across a variety of organizations and partners. Established capacity to think strategically with expertise in complex problem solving, decision making and critical thinking skills, and display good judgement. Demonstrated ability to act with autonomy and discretion on matters within scope of authority, exercising a high degree of initiative and judgment on a wide range of issues. Must possess knowledge and understanding of issues (including cultural, socio-economic and other factors) affecting Indigenous peoples; and an awareness that Indigenous engagement is a strategic priority at the University of Saskatchewan. Must have experience working in and promoting a diverse and inclusive environment. Commitment to building a high-trust professional community. An equivalent combination of education and related experience may be considered. Skills: Demonstrated leadership, engagement and support of others. Demonstrated quality interpersonal communication skills (verbal and written). Ability to work independently as well as collaboratively within a team environment. Ability to lead, coach and mentor staff to attain goals. Demonstrated sound judgment and confident decision-making. Possess and display initiative, tact, diplomacy, discretion and confidentiality. Maintain a high degree of accuracy; demonstrated proficient skills in finance and budgeting. Strong analytical skills. Proficient knowledge of computer applications including Office 365, SharePoint, and other databases. Strong facilitation and organizational skills. Demonstrated ability to make evidence-based decisions with a commitment to quality. Department: College of Medicine Status: Term 1 year with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: Exempt Shift: Mon-Fri, 8:00-4:30 pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $72,511.00 - 119,943.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Phase/Band: Salary Band 3 Posted Date: 9/24/2026 Closing Date: 10/22/2026 Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15621?c=usask": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Equine Theriogenology, Large Animal Clinical Sciences, WCVM",
      "description": "Lecturer, Equine Theriogenology, Large Animal Clinical Sciences, WCVM | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Lecturer, Equine Theriogenology, Large Animal Clinical Sciences, WCVM Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15621 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/23/2026 Back to Search Lecturer, Equine Theriogenology, Large Animal Clinical Sciences, WCVM Applications are invited from qualified individuals for a full-time position as a Lecturer in Equine Theriogenology in the Department of Large Animal Clinical Sciences ( https://wcvm.usask.ca/departments/large-clinical.php ) at the Western College of Veterinary Medicine (WCVM). The appointment will be for a three-year term, with the possibility of renewal, and will focus on delivering high-quality teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels while providing clinical expertise in support of the College’s service mandate. This position entails a 75% commitment to the equine theriogenology clinical service of the Veterinary Medical Centre ( https://vmc.usask.ca/ ) while providing supervision and instruction to undergraduate students – primarily those in the fourth or final year of the Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) program – as well as interns and residents. The successful candidate will also contribute to the department’s teaching responsibilities, including lectures, laboratories, and demonstrations in Years 1 to 3 of the DVM curriculum, and graduate courses. The remaining 25% of the appointment will be devoted to research, administrative responsibilities, and related activities. Applicants must possess a DVM degree or equivalent, have completed an approved residency in theriogenology, be either certified, i.e., a Diplomate, of the American College of Theriogenologists or be eligible for certification at the time of appointment, and have earned an advanced degree in a related discipline. They must also be eligible to work in Canada and must possess an appropriate Saskatchewan Veterinary Medical Association license ( https://svma.sk.ca/become-a-member/dvm/ ) by the start of the appointment. The Equine Theriogenology Service currently operates with one faculty member who will be on a one-year sabbatical leave beginning on July 1, 2027, as well as two Residents and a Specialty Intern. This position presents an exciting opportunity to contribute to the expansion of clinical services in assisted reproductive technologies. The WCVM ( https://wcvm.usask.ca/ ) is Western Canada’s regional college of veterinary medicine and currently enrolls about 360 undergraduate students from three western provinces and northern jurisdictions, as well as approximately 200 graduate students. The University of Saskatchewan is a member of U15 Canada – an association of the country’s leading research-intensive universities – with a reputation for excellence in teaching and other scholarly activities. The university offers a full range of undergraduate, graduate, and professional programs to more than 26,000, including over 21,000 undergrads and 4,500 graduate students. Located within the WCVM, the Veterinary Medical Centre is a state-of-the-art AVMA- and AAHA-accredited hospital and referral centre. It offers specialty services and training in anesthesia; behavior; dentistry; dermatology; exotic, wild, and zoo animal medicine; internal medicine; medical imaging; nutrition; oncology; ophthalmology; radiation oncology; rehabilitation; surgery; and theriogenology. The salary band for this position for the 2026-2027 academic year is $95,187 to $139,203. Salary is negotiable based on qualifications and experience. This position is within the scope of the University of Saskatchewan Faculty Association ( https://usaskfaculty.ca/ ). The university offers an attractive and comprehensive benefits package that includes six weeks vacation; dental, health, and extended vision coverage; a pension plan; life insurance (both compulsory and voluntary); sick leave, long-term disability, and death benefits; an employee assistance program; a flexible health and wellness spending plan; travel insurance; and a professional expense allowance. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, detailed curriculum vitae, and any supporting documents via email to: Dr. Andy Allen, Head Department of Large Animal Clinical Sciences Western College of Veterinary Medicine University of Saskatchewan Email: andrew.allen@usask.ca Telephone: 306-966-7294 In accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, priority will be given to Canadian citizens and permanent residents. Candidates are asked to indicate whether they are Canadian citizens, permanent residents, or otherwise authorized to work in Canada for the duration of the appointment. Review of applications will begin upon receipt and continue until November 6, 2026. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027, or on another date mutually agreed upon with the successful candidate. Status: Term Employment Group: USFA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Posted Date: 9/23/2026 Closing Date: 11/6/2026 The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15620?c=usask": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty Member, Beef Research & Extension",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty Member, Beef Research & Extension | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Tenure-Track Faculty Member, Beef Research & Extension Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15620 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/23/2026 Back to Search Tenure-Track Faculty Member, Beef Research & Extension Applications are invited from qualified individuals for a full-time tenure-track faculty position in Beef Cattle Research and Extension in the Department of Large Animal Clinical Sciences ( https://wcvm.usask.ca/departments/large-clinical.php ) at the Western College of Veterinary Medicine (WCVM). This position includes a 50% commitment to research and scholarly activity; 25% to teaching; 20% to extension; and 5% to administration. The successful candidate will be expected to develop and maintain an externally funded research program focused on beef cattle health, production, and disease investigation. Research outcomes should contribute to the scientific literature through publication in peer-reviewed journals and be translated into practical benefits for veterinarians and producers through extension activities and continuing education programs. This position will play a key role in sustaining and strengthening the department’s long-term capacity in beef research, clinical epidemiology, and industry engagement, while enhancing the department’s leadership in beef health and production research. Teaching responsibilities will focus on clinical epidemiology and beef cattle health and production within both the undergraduate Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) curriculum and graduate programs. The successful candidate will, initially, be expected to contribute to the routine administrative activities of the department and college. Over time, the faculty member will be expected to become increasingly involved in college and university governance, as well as service to academic, professional, or scientific organizations. The successful candidate will join a collaborative academic environment and will be supported by the Beef Cattle Research Council Chair in Beef Cattle Health, the Alberta Chair in Beef Cattle Health and Production Medicine, the Lead of the Disease Investigation Unit, two Professors of Teaching, and faculty with expertise in food animal theriogenology, large animal internal medicine, animal welfare and behavior research, and food animal field service. The department is also being considered for a Canada Excellence Research Chair ( https://www.cerc.gc.ca/home-accueil-eng.aspx ) in environmental sustainability in beef cattle production and is also currently recruiting a Professor of Teaching in Food Animal Health and Production. The ideal candidate will possess: A DVM degree (or equivalent); A PhD degree (or equivalent); A strong understanding of, and experience with, the North American beef cattle industry; Experience and familiarity with beef cattle practice in North America; and A demonstrated commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate education. Candidates with an MSc degree and actively completing a PhD may also be considered. Eligibility for licensure to practice veterinary medicine in Saskatchewan ( https://svma.sk.ca/become-a-member/dvm/ ); certification, or eligibility for certification by the American Board of Veterinary Practitioners in Beef Cattle Practice or Food Animal Practice; certification, or eligibility for certification by the American College of Veterinary Preventive Medicine; and university level teaching experience will each be considered valuable assets. The WCVM ( https://wcvm.usask.ca/ ) is Western Canada’s regional college of veterinary medicine and currently enrolls about 360 undergraduate students from three western provinces and northern jurisdictions, as well as approximately 200 graduate students. The University of Saskatchewan is a member of U15 Canada – an association of the country’s leading research-intensive universities – with a reputation for excellence in teaching and other scholarly activities. The university offers a full range of undergraduate, graduate, and professional programs to more than 26,000 students, including over 21,000 undergraduate and 4,500 graduate students. The main campus is in Saskatoon, on Treaty 6 Territory and the Homeland of the Métis, a city with a diverse and thriving economic base, a vibrant arts community and a full range of leisure opportunities. Salary information for this position for the 2026-2027 academic year is as follows: $113,527 to $135,535. Salary is negotiable based on qualifications and experience. This position is within the scope of the University of Saskatchewan Faculty Association ( https://usaskfaculty.ca/ ). This position includes a comprehensive benefits package: dental, health and extended vision care plan; pension plan, life insurance (compulsory and voluntary), academic long-term disability, sick leave, travel insurance, death benefits, an employee assistance program, a professional expense allowance, and a flexible health and wellness spending program. Interested candidates must submit a complete application that includes: Cover letter; Detailed curriculum vitae; and Supporting documents Due to federal immigration requirements, applicants are asked to indicate whether they are Canadian citizens, permanent residents, or are otherwise authorized to work in Canada for the duration of the appointment. Applicants selecting the latter category should provide a brief explanation of their status. Completed applications must be emailed to: Dr. Andy Allen, Head Department of Large Animal Clinical Sciences Western College of Veterinary Medicine University of Saskatchewan Email: andrew.allen@usask.ca Telephone: 306-966-7294 The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027, or another date mutually agreed upon with the successful candidate. Review of applications will begin upon receipt and continue until November 6, 2026. Applications received after this date will not be considered. Status: Permanent Employment Group: USFA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Posted Date: 9/23/2026 Closing Date: 11/6/2026 The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15617?c=usask": {
      "title": "Academic Integrity Specialist, Office of the Provost and Vice-President Academic",
      "description": "Academic Integrity Specialist, Office of the Provost and Vice-President Academic | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Academic Integrity Specialist, Office of the Provost and Vice-President Academic Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15617 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/23/2026 Back to Search Academic Integrity Specialist, Office of the Provost and Vice-President Academic Summary: The academic integrity specialist provides institutional leadership and serves as the central resource for advancing academic integrity across the university. This position leads academic integrity operations and supports the implementation, coordination, and continuous improvement of academic integrity initiatives, policies, regulations, and practices. Working collaboratively with academic and administrative partners, this role develops institutional plans, educational resources, and support programs that promote integrity in teaching, learning, assessment, and student success. The specialist monitors emerging trends, issues, risks, and opportunities, including the impact of artificial intelligence and other technologies on higher education. This position informs policy and practice, promotes procedural consistency, and advances coordinated approaches that foster a culture of academic integrity across the institution. Duties and Responsibilities: Identifies, prioritizes, and addresses complex academic integrity issues, risks, and emerging challenges. Leads the planning, implementation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of institution-wide academic integrity programs, initiatives, and strategic priorities. Leads complex projects and change initiatives across colleges and administrative units, influencing stakeholders and promotes consistent academic integrity practices throughout the university. Provides leadership across the university to advance and implement institutional strategies regarding academic integrity in assessment of student learning. Supervises department staff by coaching and mentoring employees, supporting professional development, assigning workflow, and supporting all stages of the employee lifecycle. Fosters a culture of academic integrity as it relates to the role of emerging technologies across the university. Advises academic leaders, governance bodies, and university partners on academic integrity matters, emerging issues, and evidence-informed practices. Provides expert leadership on academic integrity policy, governance, compliance, and institutional risk management, developing recommendations for senior leaders and governance bodies. Monitors, analyzes, and interprets institutional data, sector trends, emerging risks, and opportunities to inform decision-making, policy development, and continuous improvement. Supports the implementation, interpretation, and continuous improvement of academic integrity policies, regulations, procedures, and governance frameworks. Develops and communicates recommendations, reports, and strategic resources that support institutional awareness, accountability, and evidence-informed academic integrity practices. Monitors and provides guidance on the implications of emerging technologies, including generative artificial intelligence, for academic integrity and academic misconduct. Advances coordinated approaches to student education, support, and success related to academic integrity in collaboration with academic and administrative units. Develops educational resources and professional learning opportunities that promote academic integrity in assessment, particularly in technology-enhanced learning environments. Promotes evidence-informed and ethically responsible approaches to the use of artificial intelligence and other emerging technologies within the university learning environment. Builds and sustains productive relationships with internal and external stakeholders and represents the university in professional, sector, community, and academic integrity networks. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: A master's degree in education, administration, public administration, student affairs, policy studies, or a related field; a doctoral degree is an asset. Minimum of five years of progressively responsible experience in a post-secondary environment, including experience leading strategic initiatives, programs, projects, or committees involving multiple stakeholders and competing priorities. Experience providing advice and guidance related to policy, governance, regulatory implementation, or organizational practice, preferably as it relates to academic integrity. Experience developing and delivering educational programs, professional learning opportunities, communications, and institutional resources, as well as analyzing information, identifying trends, and preparing reports and recommendations. Knowledge of academic integrity principles, governance frameworks, policy implementation, and emerging issues affecting higher education. Understanding of teaching, learning, assessment, student success, and the evolving impacts of artificial intelligence and other educational technologies. Ability to build collaborative relationships and work effectively across diverse stakeholder groups. Excellent project management skills and the ability to maintain multiple priorities and initiatives. Demonstrated capacity for strategic thinking, systems analysis, and problem-solving. Strong consultation, facilitation, and relationship management skills. Ability to influence, build consensus, and lead through collaboration in a complex organizational environment. Excellent communication, presentation, and interpersonal skills. Ability to analyze complex information and translate findings into practical recommendations and resources. Ability to exercise sound judgment, discretion, and professionalism when dealing with sensitive matters. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Department: Office of the Provost and Vice-President Academic Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $87,266.00 - 145,353.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 3 Posted Date: 9/23/2026 Closing Date: 10/21/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15610?c=usask": {
      "title": "Your Voice Matters Program Presenter, Diefenbaker Canada Centre (Prince Albert, SK)",
      "description": "Your Voice Matters Program Presenter, Diefenbaker Canada Centre (Prince Albert, SK) | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Your Voice Matters Program Presenter, Diefenbaker Canada Centre (Prince Albert, SK) Prince Albert, SK, Canada | req15610 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/21/2026 Back to Search Your Voice Matters Program Presenter, Diefenbaker Canada Centre (Prince Albert, SK) Primary Purpose: Sponsored by Elections Saskatchewan, Your Voice Matters is an education program offered throughout the province for students in grades 4-12. YVM is currently delivered to students in Saskatoon, Regina and surrounding communities. This position will help establish a new Prince Albert-based programming hub, facilitating the continued growth and expansion of YVM to communities throughout Saskatchewan Nature of Work: Reporting to the Programs and Outreach Coordinator, Diefenbaker Canada Centre, the YVM Program Presenter will deliver the Your Voice Matters: Building a Better Saskatchewan (YVM) program to students in Prince Albert and surrounding areas. The YVM program comprises multiple topics, including federal, provincial, and Indigenous governance; political involvement; and Canadian citizenship, democracy, and voting. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Learn the YVM program and its current modules using interpretive guides, PowerPoints, and activity materials. Make updates and revisions to program materials as required Deliver full- and half-day YVM programs to students in grades 4-12 based in Prince Albert and other areas in northern Saskatchewan. This includes organizing all program materials, planning logistics, presenting PowerPoints and other media in an active and engaging manner, running themed activities for an entire class, answering pertinent questions, conducting surveys, and engaging with educators Gather reporting information by recording the number of students, school name, school district, and other relevant information from each program booking Education: Completion of an undergraduate degree, ideally in education, political studies, public policy, history, or another field focused on Canadian governance. Current students pursuing an undergraduate or graduate degree may also be considered. Completion of Masters in a related field is considered an asset. Experience: Experience in educational programming delivery and/or classroom work is required Experience working with youth and the general public is required Experience presenting information and leading activities is preferred Knowledge of the Saskatchewan school curriculum is preferred A combination of experience and education may be considered Skills: Desire to work with people, particularly youth (grades 4-12) Demonstrated strong interpersonal and communication skills, featuring a professional, responsive, pleasant, and effective manner Knowledge of and/or passion in the areas of Canadian and provincial governance, elections, and history Must possess a valid class 5 driver’s license Regular access to a personal vehicle is required and the incumbent must be able to travel within Prince Albert (to schools) and surrounding communities as requested Organizational skills and the ability to set and adhere to work priorities and meet deadlines while working on multiple projects Ability to solve problems effectively and independently Reputation for establishing and building mutually supportive relationships with diverse internal and external stakeholders while demonstrating tact, diplomacy, and intercultural sensitivity Demonstrated ability to be self-motivated combined with the ability to take direction, work effectively, and show initiative Demonstrated effective punctual, organizational, and time-management skills Must be able to provide a Criminal Record and Vulnerable Sector Check upon request Ability to communicate in French (written and oral) would be considered an asset Inquiries regarding this position can be directed to the Diefenbaker Canada Centre at dief.centre@usask.ca Department: Diefenbaker Centre Status: Casual Employment Group: Undergraduate Students - Non-union Full Time Equivalent (FTE): casual Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $23.53 - 32.94 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Phase/Band: Salary Band 5 Posted Date: 9/21/2026 Closing Date: 10/18/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15701?c=usask": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant, College of Engineering",
      "description": "Teaching Assistant, College of Engineering | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Teaching Assistant, College of Engineering Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15701 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/16/2026 Back to Search Teaching Assistant, College of Engineering In the appointment of Teaching Assistant, your general duties and responsibilities will be: Example: Preparing, delivering and/or attending lectures; demonstrating, marking, student consulting, invigilating, holding office hours, setting up experiments, supervising field trips and conferring with the supervisor in charge as required by the assignment. Your general location of work will be within the College of Engineering Required Number of Hours : Number of positions and Expected hours: Civil Engineering – 2 positions, 15 hours each Mechanical Engineering – 1 position, 17 hours Engineering Physics – 2 positions, 12 hours each Environmental Engineering – 2 positions, 16 hours each Duration of Employment : 2026 Fall Term Course Name, Abbreviation and Number: GE 112 – Engineering Discipline Experience Start and End Dates: December 14 to December 18 Qualifications (Skills and Abilities) Good interpersonal skills, knowledge, and experience with discipline. You must be registered in a graduate program. To Apply : Interested applicants must apply with this link https://www.surveymonkey.ca/r/KJBFJ7B Where a graduate student is appointed to this position, it will be in-scope of PSAC. Department: College of Engineering Status: Term Employment Group: Graduate Students - PSAC Rate of Pay: Current rates of pay Posted Date: 10/16/2026 Closing Date: 10/31/2026 Number of Openings: 7 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15756?c=usask": {
      "title": "Finance Strategic Business Advisor",
      "description": "Finance Strategic Business Advisor | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Finance Strategic Business Advisor Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15756 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Finance Strategic Business Advisor Primary Purpose As a Finance Strategic Business Advisor (SBA), we partner with colleges and units across campus to support strategic finance initiatives focused on the University Plan 2025 in being the university the world needs (nīkānītān manācihitowinihk; ni manachīhitoonaan). Nature of Work Our Finance SBA team is part of the Administration portfolio, which includes shared services, human resources, finance, services, and ICT. Finance SBAs are assigned to support specific colleges and units and implement broader Administration initiatives. Reporting to the Manager, Finance Advisory Services, the SBA is a strategic finance partner, providing support to leadership in colleges or units assigned within their portfolio.  The SBA provides financial support to and works with leaders to develop and implement solutions that achieve priorities and outcomes. Relationships are at the heart of what we do, and it is critical that we are comfortable and effective in working across multiple, diverse and at times, challenging stakeholder groups. From academics to administration, research to operations, we work with all groups in identifying, planning, and implementing financial best practices to advance the mission of the university. Primary responsibilities of the Finance SBA role include financial and strategic planning, supporting strategic initiatives, financial analysis, financial management, reporting, budgeting and controls, and cost management.  The SBA assists leadership teams with various financial activities while maintaining a strong connection to Central Financial Services and other SBAs on campus.  The SBA demonstrates a combination of strong university acumen and financial functional knowledge to participate in the development of financial plans and strategies.  The SBA supports the delivery of unit activities and processes while contributing to the strategic and operational planning and development of financial services across the University. The SBA addresses complex financial matters, applying comprehensive financial management skills and offering exceptional client service, often during periods of change, high work volumes, and stringent deadlines.  The SBA facilitates and assists with auditing, reporting, and accounting requirements for the portfolio, and ensures compliance with ethical accounting standards and university policies and procedures. In providing various financial services, the SBA exercises discretion and judgment in maintaining confidential information, and applies sound practices with a consistent, proactive and collaborative approach. Accountabilities College/Unit Support We participate in strategic thinking and planning about how to best use resources at the unit level. We provide strategic advice and support for all budgeting, financial planning and analysis to support the unique financial circumstances of our assigned portfolios. We provide regular financial reporting for units against budgets and plans. We make recommendations for the alignment between revenue sources and unit priorities and ensure initiatives are monitored and addressed. We monitor funding and revenue sources, determine how financial resources are utilized and identify opportunities that achieve greater effectiveness of operations. Functional Expertise & Collaboration We establish and maintain strong working relationships with a variety of constituents across campus, including college and support centre staff, central office colleagues, and service centre partners to ensure success in meeting the college’s, unit’s, and university’s strategic and operational goals. We contribute to the development of the Finance portfolio through leading and participating in projects to advance the tools and supports delivered by Finance to colleges and units across campus. We ensure that exposure to risk and liability is mitigated by ensuring compliance with relevant legislation, accounting standards, collective agreements, and university and unit policies and procedures. We provide valuable financial insight and timely and accurate reporting for all areas of responsibility. We provide and implement solutions that will improve financial procedures and processes within the portfolios we support. We monitor financial progress on approved projects and initiatives, ensuring funds are expended and reported in an appropriate and timely manner to relevant stakeholders. We produce reports for the units we support and prepare financial statements to review with appropriate stakeholders. We present and convey accurate information and data to ensure financially viable and sustainable decision-making and problem-solving. We support when necessary, procurement of significant goods or services identified within the unit, and works with central office colleagues and service centre partners to coordinate acquisition. We ensure effective internal controls are in place for financial transactions. We help to ensure that services provided by service centre partners (i.e. ConnectionPoint) are timely, accurate and effective. Qualifications A relevant university degree, preferably in Commerce, Business, Accounting or related field, along with a minimum of five (5) years’ experience in general accounting, financial management and budgeting, and financial planning and analysis. A professional accounting designation is considered a strong asset. Experience working in a diverse and inclusive environment is preferred. Demonstrated knowledge of accepted accounting rules, practices, tax laws, and reporting requirements is required. An equivalent combination of education and related experience may be considered. Skills Knowledge and understanding of issues (including cultural, socio-economic and other factors) affecting Indigenous peoples and an awareness that Indigenous engagement is a strategic priority at the university. The ability to influence outcomes and provide guidance to operational decisions unique to a college or unit. Excellent facilitation, conflict resolution, analytical, problem solving, and communication skills to provide strategic supports and guidance across portfolios and functional areas of finance. The ability to work collaboratively, build relationships with internal and external partners, and manage multiple demands to meet deadlines. Experience working within a multi-reporting organizational structure is an asset. Proficient computer skills, including Microsoft Office applications and a high level of attention to detail are required. Department: SBA Finance Status: Term 1 year with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: Exempt Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $72,511.00 - 119,943.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Salary Phase/Band: Salary Band 3 Posted Date: 10/28/2026 Closing Date: 11/11/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15752?c=usask": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant, Plant Sciences",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant, Plant Sciences | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Administrative Assistant, Plant Sciences Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15752 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Administrative Assistant, Plant Sciences Primary Purpose: This position provides a broad range of high-level and confidential administrative organizational, communication, planning, and problem-solving assistance and support to the senior department leadership. The role requires high level functioning in balancing complex, confidential, and competing priorities. In this position, you would be responsible for the management of associated logistics, including receiving, triaging, and scheduling multiple requests and opportunities as appropriate to meet the demands of a challenging, diverse and high-volume departmental office. As the first point of contact for the department, this position requires the ability to engage in professional and meaningful communications at all levels. Nature of Work: Reporting to the Department Head, the Administrative Assistant to the Department Head is part of a collaborative and agile administrative team that provides support to the Department of Plant Sciences. This is a high volume, fast paced position involving tight timelines and multiple competing priorities. Working directly with the senior leadership, the incumbent must use their discretion and sound judgment to prioritize meeting deadlines in an environment where there is continuous change, shifting priorities and frequent interruptions. This position works closely with the Department Head in a highly confidential manner to make recommendations and coordinate responses on behalf of the Department Head, communicate to the department on a variety of issues and initiatives, coordinate and manage projects and collaborate with a wide range of stakeholder offices, including but not limited to the to the Dean’s Office, College of Agriculture and Bioresources, and other administrative units. This position requires timely and effective follow-up on decisions with minimal direction, as well as effective and accurate exchange of specialized and complex information to others.  Attention to detail is important as the incumbent is responsible for ensuring that department’s responsibilities and project work, under the direction of the Department Head, are completed accurately and in a timely manner. It is also essential to ensure the Department Head and has access to necessary, accurate and timely information. Confidentiality and considerable discretion are required as this position is privy to and works with sensitive, highly confidential information.  The ability to trust and maintain positive relationships within and external to the department are critical for this role. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Provide timely, accurate and pro-active administrative support for all assigned duties such as: reception; scheduling calendars; arranging meetings; distribution of agendas and minutes and ensuring all appropriate parties have required material prior to meetings; responding to telephone, electronic, and in-person inquiries; data entry, managing office filing systems; preparing briefing notes. Provide effective and confidential support to the Department Head, including coordinating faculty recruitment (correspondence to applicants and referees, arrange interviews and accommodations). Strategically coordinate, and support, and assist in the analysis and improvement of collegial processes in the department. Effectively analyze, create, and prepare a variety of documents, reports, presentations and packages for the Department Head as required in an accurate and timely manner. Strategically manage assigned workload of the Department Head, establishing standards that provide a balance between meeting and office/work time, reflect realistic priorities in terms of who and when to schedule, and allow sufficient flexibility for shifts in response to the fast paced and fast changing agendas. Represent the Department Head in all contacts, ensuring the right tone and interest are projected, dealing with issues and concerns, and appropriately referring problems related to departmental functioning as they arise. Assist Department Head with collegial processes, including CV preparation, case files, annual reviews, and teaching reviews. Assist with the creation, coordination and development of reports, projects and actions within the department’s strategic plan. Work closely with the Department Head to prepare speaking notes, presentations and other such materials. Provide effective, accurate and confidential administrative support to the Department Head including drafting documents, reports, proposals, correspondence, and other operational and strategic documentation. Involvement with and coordination of department strategic planning sessions/workshops/town halls, meetings, and retreats. Effectively manages and prioritizes projects as assigned to ensure completion within an appropriate timeframe. Ensures the timely and judicious articulation of those issues about which the Department Head needs to be aware, and/or which the leadership can alone address or refer. Provide support to direct requests and queries appropriately and track and follow-up requests to ensure timely responses are provided and issues are resolved. Works with other administrative staff to effectively streamline operational practices and procedures within the unit. Provide administrative support to committees as assigned and arrange travel and track expenses as required. Models a positive, professional, productive and collaborative work environment in the unit. Exercises sound judgment, confidentiality and discretion. Other duties as assigned. Education: An undergraduate degree in business, office administration, or a related field is preferred. However, a post-secondary certificate in office administration or a related field may be considered, as may a combination of education and experience. Licenses: N/A Experience: A minimum of 5 years of progressively responsible experience providing administrative, communication and other support to administrative leadership. Experience in in a university environment would be an asset. Skills: Demonstrated ability to work effectively and discreetly with confidential and sensitive information is essential. Exercises sound judgement in decision-making and problem solving to ensure priorities and deadlines are met. High attention to detail, proactive, proficient organizational skills with demonstrated ability to deal effectively with multiple, possibly conflicting, priorities that originate from a variety of sources, in order to meet deadlines; Models a positive, productive, and collaborative work environment in the team. Shares knowledge appropriately to allow fully informed decision making by colleagues and leadership of the department Projects high standard of professional integrity and proficiency in all aspects of administrative activity. Effective and exemplary interpersonal and communication skills (both oral and written) resulting in the ability to develop and maintain positive and professional working relationships with a wide variety of individuals. Analytical and problem-solving abilities, particularly where there are no pre-existing processes or precedents. Works both independently and as an effective team member. Maintains the ability to learn new tasks in a fast-paced environment. Demonstrated proficiency with Microsoft Office applications, electronic calendaring systems. Department: Plant Sciences Status: Term 9 months Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 10/28/2026 Closing Date: 11/11/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15751?c=usask": {
      "title": "Academic Programs Assistant, College of Agriculture and Bioresources",
      "description": "Academic Programs Assistant, College of Agriculture and Bioresources | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Academic Programs Assistant, College of Agriculture and Bioresources Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15751 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Academic Programs Assistant, College of Agriculture and Bioresources Please note that a strong internal candidate has been identified . Summary: The Academic Programs Assistant provides administrative coordination and operational support for undergraduate academic programs and student services within the College of Agriculture and Bioresources. The position administers a broad range of academic processes related to curriculum management, class scheduling, student records, scholarships and bursaries, academic evaluations, student progression, and graduation. The role serves as a resource to faculty, staff, and students by providing information and guidance regarding academic procedures, regulations, and university processes. Working in a dynamic environment with cyclical deadlines, the position requires strong organizational skills, sound judgment, attention to detail, and the ability to manage multiple priorities while maintaining accurate records and effective working relationships. Duties and Responsibilities: Administers the undergraduate class build process, including course scheduling, data maintenance, instructor assignments, space considerations, and updates within established timelines. Attends Undergraduate Affairs Committee meetings and supports the Director, Academic and Student Affairs as needed with undergraduate curriculum revisions in order to implement changes through the class build process. Reviews and processes student requests related to class overrides, program changes, and other academic administrative transactions. Responds to student inquiries and correspondence, providing guidance on administrative processes and referring academic advising matters to advisors. Administers processes related to undergraduate convocation, including graduation audits, application processing, eligibility verification, statistical reporting, and preparation of governance and ceremony materials. Provides logistical support and administration for processes that support student enrollment, retention, and graduation, including reviewing and processing class overrides and requests for program changes; maintaining and updating student records in DegreeWorks and Banner Document Management and supporting student registration activities. Administers undergraduate awards including scholarships, bursaries, convocation awards, and travel awards by maintaining award records, preparing preliminary eligibility information, supporting award adjudication , communicating with recipients, and facilitating collaboration with key stakeholders in the college and Student Finance and Awards. Compiles, analyzes, and maintains undergraduate enrollment data, statistics, and reports to support operational planning and decision-making. Coordinates the administration of student course evaluations and related academic review processes. Researches, identifies, and recommends improvements to academic administrative processes and procedures to enhance service delivery and operational effectiveness. Builds and maintains effective relationships with departments, faculty, staff, and central support units. Understands and accurately communicates university policies and College Regulations to staff, faculty, and students. Maintains accurate records, files, and documentation in accordance with university procedures and records management requirements. Supports student events specifically Bean Feed, and Pinning Ceremony. Supports other student events as needed. Prioritizes competing responsibilities and manages multiple projects and deadlines within the academic cycle. Protects confidential information and exercises discretion when handling student, faculty, and institutional records. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position, and supports the effective functioning of the student services office by assisting with additional operational and administrative responsibilities as needed. Occasional travel may be required. Qualifications: Minimum of a post-secondary diploma in office administration, business administration, or a related field; a bachelor's degree is preferred. Project management training is an asset. Minimum of three years of experience supporting academic, administrative, student service, or related operational functions in a complex organization. Experience administering records, coordinating processes with multiple stakeholders, and managing time-sensitive deadlines. Experience working with student information systems, enterprise business systems, or comparable technology platforms is an asset. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply policies, procedures, and regulations. Demonstrated analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills with exceptional attention to detail and accuracy. Demonstrated ability to manage competing priorities and work independently in a deadline-driven environment. Demonstrated ability to build collaborative working relationships and provide effective client service to diverse stakeholder groups. Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications, including Excel, Word, and Outlook. Department: College of Agriculture and Bioresources Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 10/28/2026 Closing Date: 11/4/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15750?c=usask": {
      "title": "Academic Advising and Program Assistant, College of Agriculture and Bioresources",
      "description": "Academic Advising and Program Assistant, College of Agriculture and Bioresources | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Academic Advising and Program Assistant, College of Agriculture and Bioresources Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15750 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Academic Advising and Program Assistant, College of Agriculture and Bioresources Please note that a strong internal candidate has been identified . Purpose : This position provides academic advising and administrative support to undergraduate students and develops initiatives that enable student success. This position will support students across all of our degree, diploma, and certificate programs, including specialized support for Indigenous students and international students. Nature of the Work: Reporting to the Director, Academic and Student Affairs, this position is part of the college team that provides academic services to students in degree, diploma, and certificate programs. Using a developmental advising model, this position will work with students to choose a major and to promote their effective use of Degree Works and other university resources and tools available to them, such as the Course and Program Catalogue, Career Services, the International Student and Study Abroad Centre, Student Learning Services, and other support units, so that students can gather the information they need to make their own decisions. This position will provide guidance and direction to students and others within the college regarding academic policies and procedures. This position provides support to incoming and outgoing Study Abroad exchange students, including reviewing applications, providing academic advice on course selection, facilitating transfer credit evaluation of courses offered at other post-secondary institutions, and providing support for registration in collaboration with the International Study and Study Abroad Centre. This position performs administrative functions related to undergraduate Student Services including, but not limited to: processing course overrides, program changes, and major/minor declarations; maintaining and updating student records on DegreeWorks; maintaining and updating student files; sending communications to students via PAWS; supporting Faculty Advisors; supporting student events as required; assisting with class build and convocation as required. Occasional travel to recruitment and other events may be required. This position builds effective working relationships with students, parents, colleagues, faculty, and First Nation Band members and their communities. This position also must work effectively with the Gordon Oakes Red Bear Student Centre, the International Student and Study Abroad Centre, and other units on campus to develop strategies to support international and Indigenous students. Working in a complex environment, this position requires discretion, tact, professionalism and a commitment to continuous improvement. The work requires attention to quality of work, the ability to understand and accurately communicate college academic programs, and the need to prioritize in order to meet deadlines. Typically, the incumbent will apply established policies and procedures, exercising effective decision making within the scope of job duties. Accountabilities Accurate communication of university and College of Agriculture and Bioresources program regulations and requirements and academic policies, including admission requirements, course selection, major selection, academic counselling, prerequisite checking, schedules, funding and scholarships. Timely and effective communication with students seeking registration and program assistance. Support students as they resolve student issues, making necessary referrals to link students with existing campus services, and working with students experiencing difficulty academically to develop student action (success) plans. Encourage and empower students to assume responsibility for their academic career. Effectively identify, participate in, adopt, implement and monitor best practice student life, advising services and supports. Participate in the development of strategies and initiatives to enhance student experience and share these initiatives with members of the AgBio Student Services team and faculty advisors. Model a high standard of performance and outcomes; ensure positive, diverse and respectful work environments. Build and maintain strong working relationships with faculty and staff. Collaborates with others as required to complete assignments and support team efforts. Deal with sensitive and confidential issues using discretion, judgment and compassion; all confidential information and materials are treated in an appropriate and professional manner. Understand and appreciate diversity; sensitivity to and knowledge of cultural differences. Maintain a commitment to professional participation and development. Complies with all relevant legislation, regulations and procedures. Processes and procedures are documented and updated and are followed according to established standards. Education and Experience :  A Bachelor’s degree in a relevant discipline. A minimum of two years of experience working with undergraduate students and the educational process in service-related roles. Strong assessment skills with the ability to differentiate academic issues from those requiring personal counselling; knowledge of basic counselling techniques. Understanding of the issues faced by Indigenous students and adult learners will be considered an asset. Proficient working knowledge of MS Office. Working knowledge of Banner student applications will be considered an asset. Skills : Excellent interpersonal communication and listening  skills; must possess the aptitude to plan strategically, in consultation with others; effective planning and organizational skills; must be flexible and responsive to an evolving and dynamic work place; proactive, versatile and self-directed; ability to build collaborative relationships and to work effectively as part of a team; ability to relate to people of diverse cultural and organizational backgrounds. The work requires creativity, collaboration, issues management, strong initiative and follow-through. Department: College of Agriculture & Bioresources Status: Permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 10/28/2026 Closing Date: 11/4/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15748?c=usask": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, School of Rehabilitation Science",
      "description": "Research Assistant, School of Rehabilitation Science | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Assistant, School of Rehabilitation Science Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15748 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Research Assistant, School of Rehabilitation Science About the Unit/Department: The School of Rehabilitation Science at the University of Saskatchewan fosters a healthy environment that promotes a positive work experience, life-long learning, and professional pursuits of Rehabilitation Science for students, faculty, and staff. As part of the University of Saskatchewan, we are situated in the vibrant city of Saskatoon on Treaty Six territory and the traditional homeland of the Métis. Recognized for a growing and diverse student body, we have gained a reputation nationally and internationally for preparing high-quality, collaborative clinicians and rehabilitation researchers, focused on improving function while promoting optimal ability, mobility, activity, and participation. We respond to changing environments, contribute to high quality clinical care in local and global communities, and are committed to improving health and the healthcare system through innovative research and evidence-based practice. Collaboration is a hallmark of our School and is embedded in all aspects of our mission. A dynamic forward-looking faculty and staff demonstrate resilience, compassion, humility, professionalism and a passion for advancing rehabilitation science and health-related learning, teaching, research, and mentorship. Primary Purpose: To provide administrative support for qualitative research projects focused on addressing ableism and understanding the lived experiences, needs, and perspectives of people with intellectual and developmental disabilities. Nature of Work: The Research Assistant reports to Dr. Kirsten Smith and collaborates closely with other researchers, faculty, students, community partners, and self‑advocates. The work is primarily conducted in an office environment with flexibility for remote work, alongside field‑based activities such as interviews, focus groups, or community consultations. This role requires strong ethical judgment, cultural humility, and the ability to work respectfully with sensitive topics and diverse participants. The position requires a high level of discretion in managing confidential data, coordinating accessible research processes, and supporting participatory research activities. The pace of work varies depending on project milestones, with increased activity during periods of recruitment, data collection, and analysis. Occasional travel to community organizations or research sites may be required; some activities may occur outside standard working hours to accommodate participants’ schedules. Typical Duties or Accountabilities: Conduct literature reviews related to ableism, disability justice, inclusive practices, and intellectual and developmental disabilities. Support the drafting and revision of research ethics board applications for a variety of projects. Support qualitative data collection, including conducting or assisting with interviews, focus groups, and observational research using inclusive and accessible approaches. Assist with qualitative data analysis (e.g., thematic analysis) using tools such as NVivo, Quirkos or manual coding strategies. Contribute to the development of accessible research materials, including easy‑read consent forms, interview guides, recruitment materials, and knowledge‑mobilization products. Maintain meticulous documentation, data organization, and ethical records in compliance with institutional and research ethics requirements. Assist with preparing reports, conference presentations, manuscripts, policy briefs, and community‑focused dissemination tools. Support relationship‑building and communication with self‑advocates, families, community agencies, and interdisciplinary collaborators. Coordinate meetings, schedule research activities, and assist with administrative tasks that support the successful implementation of research projects. Education: A bachelor’s degree or diploma in a relevant field such as Disability Studies, Psychology, Social Work, Public Health, Health Sciences, Sociology, or a related discipline. Equivalent combinations of education and experience may be considered. TCPS-2 and Talking Mats Communication Protocol training are required and will be provided as needed. Licenses: None required. Experience: Experience conducting or assisting with qualitative research, including interviewing, coding, or analyzing narrative data. Experience or familiarity working with people with intellectual and developmental disabilities, disability communities, or equity‑deserving groups. Knowledge of anti‑ableist, disability justice, or human‑rights‑based frameworks is an asset. Experience preparing accessible or plain‑language documents is an advantage. Previous involvement in academic, community‑based, or participatory research environments is preferred. Skills: Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to build trust, communicate respectfully, and engage with diverse individuals, including people with varying communication needs. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to produce clear summaries, reports, and research documents. Competence with qualitative research software (e.g., NVivo, Quirkos) or willingness to learn. Strong organizational skills, attention to detail, and ability to manage multiple tasks and timelines. Demonstrated commitment to equity, accessibility, anti‑ableism, and inclusive research practices. Ability to work independently, exercise sound judgment, and collaborate effectively in a team‑oriented, community‑engaged research environment. Inquiries regarding this position can be directed to Kirsten Smith at kirsten.smith@usask.ca . Department: School of Rehabilitation Science Status: Casual Employment Group: Research Positions - Non-union Full Time Equivalent (FTE): Casual Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $23.53 - 32.94 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Phase/Band: Phase 5 Posted Date: 10/28/2026 Closing Date: 11/4/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15747?c=usask": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Coordinator",
      "description": "Clinical Research Coordinator | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Clinical Research Coordinator Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15747 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/28/2026 Back to Search Clinical Research Coordinator Primary Purpose: The Clinical Research Coordinator's primary purpose is to proficiently assist the Principal Investigator (PI) and associated research team through providing research coordination and support. Success in the role will require some judgment and decision making, following procedures and guidelines set by the PI and collaborating with the research team. Nature of Work: The CRC/CRN reports to the Director of Research and is accountable to the PI. The position is responsible for coordinating clinical studies within the PIs research program. The position involves some research assistant functions within the research program. The incumbent must be capable of working under general supervision subject to occasional review by the supervisor. The ability to effectively work through complex issues and concerns with low to moderate stress resulting from work pressure is necessary. Strong communication, organizational, analytical, and problem-solving skills are required. The ability to work well as a team member is critical to the role. The position may involve field work and irregular hours, such as weekend and evening work. Accountabilities Acts as the primary contact and provides administrative and coordination support for research Establishes and maintains strong working relationships with participants, researchers, and other study personnel; Liaises between study sponsors, staff, investigators and the CTSU Ensures protocol tasks are completed as per the schedule of assessments Maintains organization and proper documentation of research materials Ensures a safe environment for research participants and staff Demonstrate a commitment to teamwork and positivity, along with an ability to create and nurture positive working relationships. Resolve queries – Answer electronic data capture (eCRF/EDC) queries Maintain and complete accountability of sponsor-provided study supplies and devices Participate in regulatory and ethics continuation efforts (communication with CRPs to renew approvals) Change 5th point to “Participates in Sponsor monitoring visits, audits and Health Canada inspections” Duties Study Set-up (20%) Participates in site pre-qualification and qualification efforts Ensures all required approvals are in place prior to enrollment Assists in planning, implementation, and coordination of data collection Recruits research participants and conducts eligibility assessment Study Execution (50%) Coordinates and conducts study visits and procedures per protocol Obtains informed consent and conducts baseline testing Maintains informed consent and oversees participant retention and compliance Creates and maintains accurate patient source documents ensuring GCP Participates in Health Canada inspections and sponsor audits Prepares periodic and special reports, manuals, and correspondence Study Close-out (5%) Prepares and submits case report forms in compliance with SOPs and regulations Verify data cleanliness for database lock Return, destroy or retain remaining supplies according to protocol Ship back sponsor-provided devices Organize the Investigator Site File/Regulator binder for proper archiving Research Administration 25% Assists with monitoring and ordering of study specific supplies Attends meetings including investigator, site initiation/close out, and monitoring visits Provides general office duties, including distribution of memos and announcements, photocopying, mailing, and faxing Qualifications Education and Experience: A Bachelor’s Degree in a health-related field with 2 years of experience in clinical research or a Master’s Degree in a health-related field. Required Skills: Proficiency with Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, Outlook) Excellent verbal and written communication skills Excellent organizational, time, and project management skills Experience conducting patient-oriented research Excellent interpersonal and ability to collaborate with team members Detail oriented and able to exercise initiative and good judgement Training: The following certifications are required prior to commencing any study related procedures TCPS2 Core Course Safety Orientation for Employees (USask) Off-Campus Activity Safety Plans (USask) Canada GCP (CITI) Clinical Research Coordinator (CITI) Health Canada Division 5 (CITI) Protocol Specific SOPs (N2) McMaster HiREB Chart Review Tutorial Department: Clinical Trial Support Unit Status: Term 24 months with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: Research Positions - Non-union Shift: Mon-Fri, 8:00-4:30pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $27.18 - 38.06 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Phase/Band: Phase 6 Posted Date: 10/28/2026 Closing Date: 11/18/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
