    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15717?c=usask": {
      "title": "Simulation Technician, Academic Health Science",
      "description": "Simulation Technician, Academic Health Science | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Simulation Technician, Academic Health Science Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15717 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/27/2026 Back to Search Simulation Technician, Academic Health Science Primary Purpose: To serve as a Lead Simulation Technician (ST) while working as an independent and collaborative interdisciplinary team member. The Lead ST coordinates the communication, planning, setup, and implementation of simulated learning experiences within the Clinical Learning Resource Center (CLRC). Acting as the lead for assigned courses, projects, and operational activities, this position coordinates workflow, facilitates communication among team members, and delegates project-related tasks as required to support successful delivery of clinical learning experiences. This position is integral to maintaining the clinical learning areas as part of a high-caliber service unit for health science colleges and programs. The role helps foster an environment of collaboration, excellent customer service, and efficient day-to-day operations to meet the educational and research needs of various health science programs and external partners. Nature of Work: Reporting to the CLRC Manager, the Lead Simulation Technician (ST) works collaboratively with CLRC staff, faculty, students, standardized patients, and visitors to support the delivery of clinical learning experiences within a simulated healthcare environment. The incumbent is responsible for planning and implementing clinical labs and other learning activities for various health science colleges and schools, while ensuring operational requirements are met efficiently and effectively. As the lead for assigned courses, projects, and operational initiatives, the incumbent provides functional guidance related to workflow, prioritization, and task allocation, supporting the successful delivery of activities across the CLRC. This position plays a key role in facilitating collaboration, mentoring team members, and promoting consistent, high-quality service. This incumbent requires excellent problem-solving, time management, organizational, leadership, and mentoring skills. The position may involve constant interruptions, changing priorities, strict deadlines, multiple competing demands, addressing complex issues, moderate to heavy physical labour, and extended periods of standing. Evening and occasional weekend work may be required. Typical Duties: Coordinate the operational aspects of course implementation, including clinic room setup with identified supplies and equipment, lab support during turnover times, equipment management, and cleanup following all events. Coordinate workflow, delegate project-related tasks, and collaborate with Simulation Technicians and other CLRC team members to ensure operational requirements are met and courses run smoothly. Work closely with faculty during sessions to ensure course delivery requirements are met. Manage equipment and consumable inventories for each course offered, updating lists as necessary, ensuring supplies are current, and assisting with ordering. Maintain CLRC facilities, including clinic rooms and other CLRC and USask spaces, as clean and organized learning environments; maintain checklists of required supplies and equipment; and conduct daily room restocking as needed. Provide guidance, mentorship, and knowledge sharing to less experienced team members to support skill development and consistent service delivery. Assist with problem-solving by identifying and recommending solutions related to scheduling, space management, equipment, and supplies, and coordinate resolutions independently or in collaboration with the CLRC Manager. Provide excellent customer service to faculty, instructors, students, clients, and visitors. Maintain confidentiality regarding job assignments, courses, student activities, and other sensitive matters. Specialized duties related to the Clinical Assessment Area: Coordinate the day-to-day operational activities of the Clinical Assessment Area. Coordinate learner practice sessions and support learner Special Interest Groups. Coordinate equipment loan programs and related processes. Qualifications Education: Undergraduate degree; related health care degree, diploma or certificate preferred. Experience: 1-2 years of experience leading projects, coordinating workflows, or providing functional guidance to team members is required; 1-2 years of healthcare experience is required; proficiency with Microsoft Office applications is required. Demonstrated administrative experience, including scheduling, event planning, and managing multiple concurrent projects is essential; experience in customer service or other roles requiring strong interpersonal and communication skills is highly preferred. Skills: Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills; strong organizational and time management abilities; demonstrated ability to multitask while maintaining a high level of accuracy and attention to detail; ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a team environment; demonstrated tact, professionalism, and diplomacy; proven ability to manage multiple projects with competing priorities and deadlines; proficiency with video-based applications such as Panopto and Zoom, as well as Microsoft Office applications including Word, Excel, Outlook, and Teams; strong initiative, adaptability, and willingness to learn. Department: Academic Health Sciences Status: Term 1 year with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: CUPE 1975 Shift: Monday-Friday, 7:30-16:00 or 9:00-17:30 Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $30.25 - 38.38 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 5 Posted Date: 10/21/2026 Closing Date: 11/4/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15743?c=usask": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Assistant, College of Nursing",
      "description": "Graduate Research Assistant, College of Nursing | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Graduate Research Assistant, College of Nursing Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15743 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Graduate Research Assistant, College of Nursing In the appointment of Graduate Research Assistant, your general duties and responsibilities will be: Organising, supporting and analysing focus group discussions and interviews supporting meetings, knowledge sharing events and preparing reports. Student research assistants will be allowed the opportunity to participate in authorship on related publications. Dr. Maina is seeking a casual research assistant to contribute to a project centered on sexual health and supporting immigrant and racialized communities. We are looking for a candidate that has demonstrated experiences in sexual health research, and preferably, a background working with immigrant and racialized communities. The incumbent is expected to organize community consultation meetings, conduct scoping reviews, collect and analyze qualitative data (when necessary). The incumbent should have the ability to work independently and keen attention to detail is necessary for this position. Your general location of work will be remotely from your own home/office, and you will report to Dr. Geoffrey Maina and Stéphanie Caron-Roy (Research Manager) as your supervisors. Required Number of Hours: 30-60 hours per academic term Duration of Employment: November 1, 2026 – October 30, 2027. Qualifications (Skills and Abilities) Applicants are required to be enrolled in a graduate program at the University of Saskatchewan. The ideal candidate should be a PhD candidate nearing completion of their program, particularly those with training in public health, sociology, psychology, nursing, or related fields. Must have proficiency using Microsoft 360, and strong organization and communication skills. Experience conducting scoping reviews, focus groups, interviews, and data analysis are considered assets. People with lived experience of being a Migrant and ethnic minority are encouraged to apply. Connection to, or knowledge of migrant communities in Saskatoon is an asset. You must be registered in a graduate program. To Apply : Interested applicants must include the following in their application: cover letter and curriculum vitae. References will be requested for applicants short-listed for an interview. Inquiries for this position can be directed to Dr. Geoffrey Maina at geoffrey.maina@usask.ca , and Stéphanie Caron-Roy at stephanie.caron-roy@usask.ca . Where a graduate student is appointed to this position, it will be in scope of PSAC. Department: College of Nursing Status: Casual Employment Group: Graduate Students - PSAC Rate of Pay: Current rates of pay Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 11/9/2026 Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: May be eligible for hybrid work under the terms of USask's Alternative Workspace Guidelines Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15742?c=usask": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant, Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Teaching Assistant, Mechanical Engineering | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Teaching Assistant, Mechanical Engineering Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15742 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Teaching Assistant, Mechanical Engineering In the appointment of Teaching Assistant, you will report directly to the Instructor(s) responsible for the course.  Specific duties will be assigned by the Instructor(s) and may include one or more of the following: Marking assignments; Marking laboratory logbooks and/or reports; Marking quizzes and/or exams; Holding tutorials and/or staffing a Help Desk for students; Assisting with general administration of the course such as email communications with students regarding assignments, and recording grades Demonstrating and/or assisting with the demonstration of laboratories; Videorecording laboratory demonstrations Editing videos and preparing online laboratory materials Monitoring safety in the laboratory in cooperation with the Instructor(s); Invigilating examinations; Collecting information for continuous program improvement; Other duties related to the course deemed appropriate by the Instructor(s). These positions will be in-person. Successful candidates are expected to be on campus for the duration of the appointment. Your general location of work will be in the College of Engineering and you will report to the respective course instructor. Required Number of Hours : 1 to 12 hours per week (course dependent) Duration of Employment : 2026-2027 Regular Session Course Name, Abbreviation and Number: see list Start and End Dates: January 2 to April 30, 2027 Courses requiring Teaching Assistants: GE 213 – Mechanics of Materials GEOE 380 – Mine Ventilation ME 215 – Fluid Mechanics I ME 229 – Introduction to Mechanical Engineering Design ME 314 – Machine Design ME 323 – Mechanics of Materials II ME 329 – Collaborative Design and Manufacturing ME 335 – Fluid Mechanics II ME 352 – Engineering Analysis III ME 450 – Finite Element Analysis ME 460 – Automation and Robotics in Manufacturing ME 476 – Nuclear Reactor Thermal Hydraulics ME 490 – Design of Fluid Power Circuits ME 491 – Thermal Systems Design ME 492 – Materials in Engineering Design ME 495 – Industrial Design Project ME 328 Lab – Review of Electrical Concepts ME 328 Lab – Metallurgy of Ferrous Alloys ME 328 Lab – Hardenability of Steels ME 328 Lab – Dynamic Measurement (Wheel Balancing) ME 328 Lab – Heat Exchangers and Temperature Measurements ME 328 Lab – Thermal Conductivity ME 328 Lab – Pipe Flow Networks ME 328 Lab – Bolt Preloading and Load Cells ME 328 Lab – Strain Gauges and Strain Fields ME 328 Lab – Wheatstone Bridges and Selective Load Sensing ME 328 Lab – Project You will be asked to specify which courses and/or labs you are applying for during the application process. Qualifications (Skills and Abilities) You must be an active registered graduate student at the University of Saskatchewan. Minimum Qualifications: Graduate student in good standing at the University of Saskatchewan Demonstrated experience (e.g., undergraduate or graduate coursework, research or industrial experience, experience with specific software packages) that is relevant to the specific class or laboratory Good English language oral and written communication skills Completion of the University of Saskatchewan Laboratory Safety and WHMIS training courses Ideal Qualifications: MSc or PhD student in good standing in Mechanical Engineering with an undergraduate degree in Mechanical Engineering Excellent English language oral and written communication skills Previous experience as an instructor or teaching assistant Familiarity with specific equipment used in the laboratory (if applicable) To Apply : Interested applicants must apply only through THIS LINK . Inquiries regarding this position can be directed to engr.ta.recruitment@usask.ca Where a graduate student is appointed to this position, it will be in-scope of PSAC. Department: Mechanical Engineering Status: Term Employment Group: Graduate Students - PSAC Rate of Pay: Current rates of pay Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 11/9/2026 Number of Openings: 50 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15741?c=usask": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant, Chemical and Biological Engineering",
      "description": "Teaching Assistant, Chemical and Biological Engineering | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Teaching Assistant, Chemical and Biological Engineering Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15741 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Teaching Assistant, Chemical and Biological Engineering In the appointment of Teaching Assistant, your general duties and responsibilities will be: Preparing, delivering and/or attending lectures; demonstrating, marking, student consulting, invigilating, holding office hours, setting up experiments, supervising field trips and conferring with the supervisor in charge as required by the assignment. These positions will be in-person. Successful candidates are expected to be on campus for the duration of the appointment. Your general location of work will be in the College of Engineering and you will report to the respective course instructor. Required Number of Hours : 1-12 hours per week (course dependent) Duration of Employment : 2026-2027 Regular session Course Name, Abbreviation and Number: see list below. Start and End Dates: January 2 to April 30, 2027 CHE 210 – Fluid Mechanics I CHE 223 – Chemical Thermodynamics CHE 315 – Equilibrium Stage Operations CHE 322 – Mathematical Modeling CHE 324 – Heat Transfer CHE 326 – Plant Design Project CHE 333 – Chemical Engineering Laboratory I CHE 369 – Fundamentals of Mineral Processing CHE 424 – Chemical Engineering Laboratories III CHE 460 – Oil and Natural Gas Upgrading CHE 461 – Biochemical Engineering Qualifications (Skills and Abilities) MSc or PhD student in good standing enrolled in the College of Engineering, preferably in Chemical and/or Biological Engineering with an undergraduate degree in Chemical Engineering. Strong English language, oral and written communication skills required. Familiarity with specific equipment used in laboratory (if applicable). Completion of the University of Saskatchewan Laboratory Safety and WHMIS training courses is required. Prior experience at USask as an instructor or teaching assistant along with coursework, research, or industrial experience that is relevant to a specific class or laboratory may be taken into consideration. To Apply : Interested applicants must complete the online application and submit their current CV/resume to be considered: Online Application: https://www.surveymonkey.ca/r/CBE_TA_202701 Email a PDF copy of your resume to engr.ta.recruitment@usask.ca ; please include CBE TA Application 2027-01 in the subject line. Must be a current, registered student to be considered. Where a graduate student is appointed to this position, it will be in scope of PSAC. Department: Chemical and Biological Engineering Status: Term Employment Group: Graduate Students - PSAC Rate of Pay: Current rates of pay Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 11/9/2026 Number of Openings: 15 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous m