    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15737?c=usask": {
      "title": "Government Relations Officer",
      "description": "Government Relations Officer | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Government Relations Officer Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15737 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Government Relations Officer Summary: The government relations officer supports the university's government relations strategy by coordinating stakeholder engagement activities, preparing briefing materials, monitoring government developments, and assisting with the planning and execution of events involving elected officials, public servants, and external partners. Working closely with senior government relations staff, the officer provides research, logistical coordination, and communications support to advance institutional priorities and strengthen relationships with municipal, provincial, federal, Indigenous, and sector stakeholders. Key responsibilities for this position include event planning, preparation of scenario notes, and development of briefing materials for university leadership. Duties and Responsibilities: Leads logistical planning and execution of meetings, roundtables, government visits, announcements, tours, receptions, and special events in line with government relations strategy and the university’s priorities. Develops scenario notes and issue-management materials for senior leadership. Prepares briefing notes, speaking notes, key messages, stakeholder profiles, biographies, and meeting packages. Prepares detailed event briefing books and coordination materials for university leadership. Conducts research and environmental scans and prepares background materials on government initiatives, public policy issues, and key stakeholders. Supports the implementation of government relations priorities and engagement plans. Supports relationship-building activities with government, Indigenous, community, and sector partners. Coordinates the collection of information from internal subject matter experts to support the preparation of briefing materials. Coordinates invitations, attendance tracking, event logistics, run-of-show documents, and stakeholder communications. Supports protocol considerations and stakeholder management before, during, and after events. Supports the development of communications products related to government engagement activities, in collaboration with communications partners. Prepares summaries and reports highlighting engagement outcomes, issues, and next steps. Monitors and tracks government announcements, legislative developments, policy discussions, and stakeholder activities relevant to the university. Maintains consistency of messaging across briefing materials and stakeholder communications. Coordinates and tracks follow-up actions and reporting arising from government relations meetings, events, and engagements. Liaises with internal and external stakeholders regarding event logistics and participation. Assists with scheduling and coordination of government-relations-related visits and stakeholder meetings. Assists in maintaining stakeholder databases and engagement records. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in political studies, public administration, communications, public policy, business, Indigenous governance, or a related field. Minimum of three years of related experience in government relations, public affairs, communications, stakeholder relations, event management, or public policy, preferably within a post-secondary, government, public-sector, or not-for-profit environment. Experience planning events or stakeholder engagement activities. Experience preparing briefing materials for executives, elected officials, or senior leaders is an asset. Strong written communication abilities. Ability to synthesize complex information into concise and actionable materials. Excellent organizational and project coordination skills. Strong event planning and stakeholder management capabilities. Knowledge of governmental decision-making processes and public policy environments. Strong interpersonal and relationship-building skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. High degree of discretion, judgment, and professionalism. Proficiency with Microsoft 365 applications and project coordination tools. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Department: Government Relations Office Status: Term 2 years with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $69,035.00 - 107,868.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 2 Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 11/9/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15734?c=usask": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant, Administrative Network",
      "description": "Executive Assistant, Administrative Network | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Executive Assistant, Administrative Network Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15734 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Executive Assistant, Administrative Network Summary: The executive assistant provides a wide range of confidential support to one or more senior leaders. Key responsibilities include coordinating complex calendars, providing comprehensive meeting support, and handling confidential information with discretion. Acting as the primary contact for the senior leader and various stakeholders, this position drafts, reviews, and sends communications on behalf of the leader. The executive assistant also plays a crucial role in initiative tracking, preparing presentations and briefing documents, and conducting research to aid decision-making. This position provides strategic monitoring of the senior leader’s initiatives and priorities and plays a crucial role in ensuring the efficiency and effectiveness of the office. Duties and Responsibilities: Provides strategic calendar management by coordinating complex schedules, establishing standards that balance meeting and office time, reflect priorities, and allow for flexibility. Provides advanced email management by monitoring, triaging, organizing, prioritizing and responding to emails on the leader’s behalf or as a delegate, ensuring timely and appropriate communication. Provides comprehensive meeting preparation with briefings, updates, materials, and information, while tracking deadlines, commitments, and emerging issues. Tracks initiatives and priorities, ensuring they go through the right channels, are included in relevant meetings, and are added to the appropriate agendas. Gathers information, creates, drafts, edits, and files a variety of documents, presentations, reports, and correspondence, ensuring confidential and sensitive information is handled appropriately. Provides meeting and committee support by coordinating meeting logistics, preparing the committee chair, creating and distributing agendas and documentation, taking minutes, tracking and following up on action items, setting up video conferencing tools, and troubleshooting technical issues for smooth communication during meetings. Coordinates assigned projects and tasks, developing collective knowledge and delegating work, and engaging colleagues. Contributes to continuous improvement initiatives by identifying issues and participating in process evaluation and re-design. Serves as the primary point of contact for the office and leaders, utilizing clear and effective communication, addressing inquiries, and providing direction in alignment with organizational values and priorities. Organizes complex travel arrangements, itineraries, submits, and approves travel and other expense requests. Coordinates and oversees all event logistics, including budget management, catering, and invitations. Builds and maintains positive relationships with key internal and external stakeholders, working collaboratively with administrative network colleagues. Processes financial transactions and reconciliations. Assists with human resource processes such as submitting job postings, scheduling interviews, submitting letters of offer, assisting with onboarding and offboarding processes, and processing changes to employment records. Provides general office administrative support for efficient and effective operations. Greets and assists visitors, providing a welcoming and professional environment. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all community members, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion, and responds to different perspectives with curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: An office administration diploma; a bachelor’s degree is preferred. Minimum of five years of senior-level administrative experience, preferably supporting executive members in a large, complex organization. Proven ability to manage executive level calendars, communications, and confidential information with a high degree of discretion and professionalism. Exceptional communication skills, including the ability to draft and edit high level correspondence and reports. Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to build and foster effective relationships with diverse internal and external stakeholder groups. Ability to act with a high degree of professionalism, discretion, and navigate sensitive matters with tact and diplomacy. Advanced organizational and time management skills with the ability to anticipate needs, prioritize tasks and manage multiple demands independently. Demonstrates resilience and adaptability in high-pressure environments through a proactive, self-directed approach to learning and problem-solving; committed to continuous improvement and aligning actions with evolving best practices and strategic priorities. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and the ability to quickly learn and adapt to new technologies and systems. Experience with university systems is preferred. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Department: Administration Network Status: Permanent Employment Group: Exempt Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $54,520.00 - 95,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Phase/Band: Salary Band 2 Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 11/9/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15730?c=usask": {
      "title": "Human Resources Strategic Business Advisor",
      "description": "Human Resources Strategic Business Advisor | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Human Resources Strategic Business Advisor Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15730 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Human Resources Strategic Business Advisor PRIMARY\nPURPOSE As Human Resources Strategic Business Advisors (HR SBA), we partner\nwith colleges and units across campus to support strategic people initiatives focused\non in the University Plan 2025 in being the university\nthe world needs (nīkānītān manācihitowinihk; ni manachīhitoonaan). NATURE\nOF WORK Our Human Resources Strategic Business Advisor (SBA) group is part\nof the Human Resources portfolio. Within our team structure, HR SBAs and HR\nCoordinators report directly to one of our five Senior HR SBAs, while the team\ncollectively reports to the Manager, HR Advisory services. HR SBAs work closely\nwith leadership, faculty and staff in our assigned colleges or units. Our success depends on our ability to demonstrate a combination of\nstrong university acumen, HR functional knowledge and a strategic mindset to\nparticipate in the development and implementation of people strategies for the\ncolleges and units. SBAs are assigned to support specific colleges and units. Within\nthese assigned areas, we partner with college and unit leaders by understanding\ntheir values, culture, challenges and operational needs. We work\ncollaboratively with our specialist groups in the Human Resources portfolio,\nwhich includes employee & labour relations, total rewards and talent\nmanagement, to help achieve people strategies and outcomes both institutionally\nand across the different colleges and units. Relationships are at the heart of what we do and its critical that\nour SBAs are comfortable and effective in working across multiple, diverse and,\nat times, challenging stakeholder groups.  From academics to administration, research to operations, we work with\nall groups in identifying, planning and implementing people practices to\nadvance the mission of the university. As SBAs, we regularly address complexities in the delivery of\nservices and are required to manage emerging and unpredictable events in a sensitive\nand timely manner. We regularly work with leaders when implementing difficult and\nchallenging decisions, often during periods of change. As a result, we must have\nwell developed organizational and project management skills and may be required\nto work outside of the university’s regular operating hours, which we balance\nwith professional flexibility. The HR SBA role comes with opportunities to\ncollaborate with leadership, faculty, staff and colleagues across a variety of\nplatforms, work in support of operational and academic initiatives and further our\ncareers through professional development and continuing education. ACCOUNTABILITIES College/Unit\nSupport We support the implementation and delivery of integrated human resource strategies that assist colleges and units in achieving their goals, priorities, and operational objectives; We support leaders by providing strategic guidance, and creative solutions which assist in the development of initiatives in areas such as talent acquisition, career engagement, problem solving, labour relations, organizational development and change management, to assist the college and unit in meeting its strategic goals; We work with leaders to identify areas of specific focus in which to provide specific HR support and development. Functional\nExpertise & Collaboration We establish and maintain strong working relationships with a variety of constituents across campus, including college staff, central office colleagues, and service centre partners to ensure success in meeting the college’s and university’s strategic and operational goals; We contribute to the development of the Human Resources portfolio through leading and/or participating in projects to advance the tools and supports delivered by HR to colleges and units across campus; We partner with our colleagues in functional specialist groups to develop a depth of university specific skill and knowledge in areas such as labour relations, talent management, career engagement, wellness, organizational design and diversity and inclusion; We ensure that exposure to risk and liability is mitigated by ensuring compliance with and providing strategic guidance related to collective agreements, labour legislation, employment law, and university and college/unit policies and procedures. QUALIFICATIONS A relevant university degree, preferably in a human resources or related field, along with a minimum of three (3) years’ experience working in human resources; A Chartered Professional in Human Resources (CPHR) designation is considered an asset; Experience working in a unionized environment is preferred. SKILLS A strong understanding and knowledge of several human resource disciplines through progressively responsible positions including labour relations, performance management, diversity and talent acquisition; The ability to influence outcomes and provide guidance to operational decisions unique to a college or unit; Strong facilitation, conflict resolution, problem-solving and communication skills are required to provide strategic supports and guidance across portfolios and functional areas of HR; Knowledge and understanding of issues (including cultural, socio-economic and other factors) affecting Indigenous peoples, in support of Indigenous engagement as a strategic priority at the university; Understanding of equity, diversity and inclusion principles and the ability to identify where greater EDI and anti-racism efforts are required, connecting with institutional EDI strategies and supports; The ability to work collaboratively, build relationships with internal and external partners, and manage multiple demands to meet deadlines; Proficient computer skills, including Microsoft Office applications.Experience and skills in working within SharePoint and OneNote is considered an asset. Department: SBA Human Resources Status: Term 12 months with the possibility of extension Employment Group: Exempt Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $72,511.00 - 119,943.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Phase/Band: Salary Band 3 Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 11/9/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15728?c=usask": {
      "title": "Career Education and Co-op Program Coordinator, College of Engineering",
      "description": "Career Education and Co-op Program Coordinator, College of Engineering | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Career Education and Co-op Program Coordinator, College of Engineering Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15728 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Career Education and Co-op Program Coordinator, College of Engineering Summary: The career education and co-op programming coordinator oversees the centre and leads the development, evaluation, revision, and delivery of co-operative program courses and career-related materials. This role coordinates co-operative education and experiential learning programs while supporting the creation and accreditation of new initiatives, including a master’s co-op program. It establishes and delivers career advising services and opportunities for undergraduate and graduate engineering students. This position collaborates with academic leaders, faculty, industry partners, and external stakeholders to align programming with strategic priorities and labour market demands. It contributes to student outreach, engagement, and success within a high-volume, student-centered environment. This role also monitors program outcomes and adapts programming to meet evolving institutional and industry needs. Duties and Responsibilities: Develops and submits proposals for new co-operative education programs, including policies, procedures, and resource plans. Secures accreditation for co-operative education programs through relevant governing bodies. Designs and leads the creation, revision, and delivery of co-operative courses and career programming. Integrates equity, diversity, and inclusion principles into program design and delivery. Oversees and operates the centre, including programming, budgeting, and reporting. Establishes and coordinates experiential learning and work-integrated learning initiatives. Identifies labour market trends and incorporates industry needs into program improvements. Builds partnerships with faculty, industry, and external organizations for program delivery and engagement. Supervises teaching assistants, including hiring, training, assigning duties, and evaluating performance. Develops and delivers workshops, sessions, and instructional materials for students. Coordinates course delivery activities, including content preparation, grading systems, and learning platforms. Provides one-on-one career advising and supports students in developing career plans and opportunities. Conducts program evaluations and analyzes data to inform continuous improvement. Liaises with academic teams and stakeholders to support program integration and collaboration. Monitors budgets and allocates resources to support programming and operations. Maintains and updates co-operative program content and career-related resources. Supports accreditation and reporting requirements related to professional engineering organizations. Oversees outreach, engagement, and student-focused initiatives within the college. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in engineering or a related field. Registration or eligibility to register as a Professional Engineer (P.Eng.) with the Association of Professional Engineers and Geoscientists of Saskatchewan (APEGS) is preferred. Minimum of five years of experience in teaching, instructional support, or engineering education. Experience supervising and training teaching assistants and working with students in academic or career-focused settings. Experience with e-learning technologies and online course delivery, as well as managing work-integrated learning or experiential programs are assets. Familiarity with engineering-related technologies and learning systems. Familiarity with engineering academic programs and university environments is an asset. Ability to support engineering students with career development and employment opportunities. Strong interpersonal and communication skills. Ability to plan, maintain, and prioritize multiple projects and budgets. Effective judgment, professionalism, discretion, and confidentiality practices. Ability to conduct research, analyze data, and interpret labour market trends. Strong organizational skills with high attention to detail. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a team. Proficient computer skills, including Microsoft Office applications and media tools such as Adobe Premiere Pro. Department: College of Engineering Status: Term 1 year with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $69,035.00 - 107,868.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Instructional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 2 Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 11/9/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15727?c=usask": {
      "title": "Research Coordinator, Health Equity and Social Accountability",
      "description": "Research Coordinator, Health Equity and Social Accountability | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Coordinator, Health Equity and Social Accountability Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15727 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Research Coordinator, Health Equity and Social Accountability Description: Primary Purpose: The Research Coordinator role is to work under the direction of the Vice-Dean of Research, to support the initiation and growth of health equity, health services, and population health research studies with an initial focus on community mapping of engagement, equity and opportunity determinants in Saskatchewan. This individual is expected to assist with the support and implementation of research activities related to specific health equity, health policy, and social accountability. This person will assist with facilitating partnership connections and communications, conducting environmental scans, baseline data creation and collection, project grants, administrative support duties, meetings, writing, community engagement and the creation and implementation of work plans. Nature of Work: As a valued research coordinator, this individual will build and maintain professional relationships to work collaboratively with community and university stakeholders, research staff and faculty as well as independently manage the smooth, effective and efficient research milestones. The work is diverse and innovative. It will involve working on the initiation and launch of ideas into action, practice, partnership and policies. It will also involve working across populations, communities, partners, researchers and disciplines. The work will involve a mix of administrative and research tasks. The Research Coordinator will have a breadth of knowledge and experience in health-related research that could include but is not limited to population health, public health, community health, improvement science, clinical and health services. The successful candidate must have the ability to build relationships, navigate uncertainty, work well independently, is results oriented and adept at balancing priorities and multiple deliverables simultaneously and in a timely manner. The individual must exercise sound judgment and have advanced project management skills. Accountabilities: Provide seamless research support for the Research Manager, including communicating with team members and stakeholders, as well as organizing meetings. Track, support and coordinate research related activities, progress and outcomes Provide research support for the research portfolio including a structured workplan of deliverables with key milestones. Assist with developing, preparing and finalizing a broad range of written materials, including strategic documents, reports, literature reviews, funding proposals, both independently and in collaboration with the research team and key stakeholders and partners. Ensure the high quality of studies as per regulations stipulated by the Tri-Council and other granting agencies, Research Ethics Boards, and other oversight and regulatory bodies, and uplift health equity and social accountability research philosophies and methodologies. Generate data (mapping practices, policies and programs), conduct interviews, and analyze documents, literature to inform research program design and implementation Develop strong, collaborative relationships within the communities in Saskatchewan as well as nationally and internationally, to meet, grow and scale research activities and project goals. Participate i n relevant meetings and committees at the organizational and regional levels related to programs of research. Qualifications Education: A level of education equivalent to a bachelor’s degree in a related health, social sciences, or related discipline from a recognized program. Experience: A minimum of two years of related experience in research and/or community engagement and health equity projects/initiatives. Skills: Ability to work both independently and collaboratively Prioritization, problem-solving and presentation skills Ability to lead, adapt, and innovate Experience setting-up accountable process and organizing systems/structures Excellent communication skills, both written and verbal Strong analytical and interviewing skills Proven ability to effectively work with decision-makers, across systems, communities and sectors to achieve solutions to unique and complex problems Ability to navigate change and ambiguity. Ability to apply design-thinking and generate solutions Demonstrated ability to exercise judgment, tact, diplomacy and confidentiality Highly proficient computer skills with excel and mapping software programs Department: College of Medicine Status: Term 1 year with the possibility of extension Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 0.5 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $53,091.00 - 82,954.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 1 Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 11/9/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15726?c=usask": {
      "title": "Well-Being Coordinator, College of Law",
      "description": "Well-Being Coordinator, College of Law | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Well-Being Coordinator, College of Law Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15726 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Well-Being Coordinator, College of Law Overview : The College of Law recognizes that low well-being impedes student academic success. Additionally, the legal profession has increasingly recognized the value of professional development relating to well-being. Self-awareness, self-management and resilience enable law students to be successful and are considered essential competencies in legal professionals. This position offers an opportunity for a motivated and compassionate professional to make a positive impact on the lives of Law students. Primary Purpose: The well-being coordinator designs and delivers programming that supports the personal well-being and professional development of law students including both JD and graduate students. This role focuses on fostering self-awareness, resilience, and healthy practices that contribute to academic success and readiness for the legal profession. The coordinator collaborates with students, faculty, leadership, and campus and community partners to identify emerging needs and to create responsive, relevant initiatives. This role also serves as a point of connection between students and appropriate wellness-related resources. Nature of Work: Reporting operationally to the Director of Administration and working closely with the Associate Dean Academic, the Coordinator will identify the needs of the law students by working with various groups such as the college and student leadership, faculty, students and administrative staff to collect data and gather information. There is considerable judgement and discretionary decision-making as well as the individual must maintain and conduct themselves with a high level of confidentially. As the position is .5FTE, the incumbent will need to prioritize the work, pivot their workflow with little to no notice and manage expectations by all. Accountabilities: Identifies law student well-being needs through consultation, data collection, and collaboration with college leadership, faculty, staff, students, and legal professionals. Develops and coordinates comprehensive well-being programs, initiatives, and educational activities related to mental health, resilience, stress management, and law-specific wellness topics. Integrates well-being initiatives with academic and co-curricular programming within the college. Connects students with appropriate campus and community resources in collaboration with internal and external partners. Advises college leadership on emerging trends, ongoing initiatives, and program outcomes Maintains current knowledge of trends and emerging practices in student well-being and professional wellness. Participates on wellness-related committees. Maintains confidential records related to student interactions and program delivery. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in psychology, education, social work, or related field; a master’s degree is preferred. Mental Health First Aid certification is preferred. Minimum of five years of related experience. Experience in post-secondary teaching, counselling, or the development of wellness-related curriculum or programs are assets. Skills: Strong interpersonal and relationship-building abilities. Knowledge of mental health, well-being, and young adult development. Ability to communicate clearly and effectively. Strong research, planning, and analytical skills. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a team. Sound judgement and discretion when handling sensitive and confidential matters. Strong organizational, problem-solving, and creative thinking skills. Proficient computer skills, including Microsoft Office applications. Department: College of Law Status: Term 24 months with the possibility of extension or becoming permanent Employment Group: ASPA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 0.5 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $69,035.00 - 107,868.00 per annum. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Specialist Professional Salary Phase/Band: Phase 2 Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 11/2/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/14707?c=usask": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Oral Radiology, College of Dentistry",
      "description": "Tenure Track Oral Radiology, College of Dentistry | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Tenure Track Oral Radiology, College of Dentistry Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req14707 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Tenure Track Oral Radiology, College of Dentistry The College of Dentistry invites applications from qualified individuals for a full-time tenure-track faculty position at the academic rank of Assistant Professor in Oral Radiology . Responsibilities include delivering didactic, pre-clinical, and clinical teaching, supervising student research projects; initiating, leading, and participating in innovative research programs; competing successfully for internal and external funding; and undertaking relevant administrative activities. Applicants must have a DDS or DMD (or equivalent) degree and specialty training in Oral Radiology. A strong preference will be given to candidates who hold a PhD or MSc degree with a record of research and/or academic accomplishments. Postdoctoral training is also considered an asset. Desirable attributes include effective classroom teaching skills, strong mentoring abilities, and excellent interpersonal communication skills. Salary information for this position for the 2026-2027 academic year is as follows: Assistant Professor: $110,489 to $131,909. Independent of rank, a starting salary above the standard salary bands is usually common and possible in the College of Dentistry This position includes a comprehensive benefits package which includes a dental, health and extended vision care plan; pension plan, life insurance (compulsory and voluntary), academic long-term disability, sick leave, travel insurance, death benefits, an employee assistance program, a professional expense allowance, and a flexible health and wellness spending program. This tenure-track position is supported by start-up funds designed to enable early success in research and scholarship. Start-up support may include resources for establishing research infrastructure, initiating pilot studies, and advancing scholarly activities aligned with the College’s strategic research priorities. The level and structure of start-up funding will be commensurate with the candidate’s experience, research program, and anticipated funding trajectory. Interested candidates must submit, via email, 1) a cover letter; 2) a detailed curriculum vitae; 3) three letters of reference 4) a detailed 5-year research plan focusing on grant applications, including research ambitions, research outcomes and pitfalls (maximum 8 pages), and 5) a teaching philosophy statement to: Dr. Walter Siqueira DDS MBA PhD FCAHS Dean and Professor College of Dentistry, 107 Wiggins Road University of Saskatchewan Saskatoon, Saskatchewan   S7N 5E5 Telephone: (306) 966-5121         Fax: (306) 966-5132        Email: dentistrysearch@usask.ca Review of applications will begin immediately; however, applications will be accepted and evaluated until the position is filled. The anticipated start date is December 1, 2026. The University of Saskatchewan’s main campus is situated on Treaty 6 Territory and the Homeland of the Métis. The University of Saskatchewan is located in Saskatoon, Saskatchewan, a city with a diverse and thriving economic base, a vibrant arts community and a full range of leisure opportunities. The University has a reputation for excellence in teaching, research and scholarly activities and offers a full range of undergraduate, graduate, and professional programs. Due to federal immigration requirements, we also ask candidates to indicate whether they are Canadian citizens, permanent residents, or are otherwise already authorized to work at this position for the duration of the appointment, with an explanation if this last category is indicated. Status: Tenure Track Employment Group: USFA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 4/26/2027 The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact Human Resources for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/14705?c=usask": {
      "title": "Dentistry, Tenure Track Comprehensive Care, College of Dentistry",
      "description": "Dentistry, Tenure Track Comprehensive Care, College of Dentistry | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Dentistry, Tenure Track Comprehensive Care, College of Dentistry Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req14705 Apply Now Share Save Job Back to Search Dentistry, Tenure Track Comprehensive Care, College of Dentistry The College of Dentistry invites applications from qualified individuals for a full-time tenure-track faculty position at the academic rank of Assistant Professor in Comprehensive Care . Responsibilities include delivering didactic, pre-clinical, and clinical teaching, supervising student research projects; initiating, leading, and participating in innovative research programs; competing successfully for internal and external funding; and undertaking relevant administrative activities. Applicants must have a DDS or DMD (or equivalent). Research expertise in oral microbiology and oral biology is preferred for this position. Postdoctoral training is also considered an asset. Desirable attributes include effective classroom teaching skills, strong mentoring abilities, and excellent interpersonal communication skills. Salary information for this position for the 2026-2027 academic year is as follows: Assistant Professor: $110,489 to $131,909. Independent of rank, a starting salary above the standard salary bands is usually common and possible in the College of Dentistry. This position includes a comprehensive benefits package which includes a dental, health and extended vision care plan; pension plan, life insurance (compulsory and voluntary), academic long-term disability, sick leave, travel insurance, death benefits, an employee assistance program, a professional expense allowance, and a flexible health and wellness spending program. This tenure-track position is supported by start-up funds designed to enable early success in research and scholarship. Start-up support may include resources for establishing research infrastructure, initiating pilot studies, and advancing scholarly activities aligned with the College’s strategic research priorities. The level and structure of start-up funding will be commensurate with the candidate’s experience, research program, and anticipated funding trajectory. Interested candidates must submit, via email, 1) a cover letter; 2) a detailed curriculum vitae; 3) three letters of reference 4) a detailed 5-year research plan focusing on grant applications, including research ambitions, research outcomes and pitfalls (maximum 8 pages), and 5) a teaching philosophy statement to: Dr. Walter Siqueira DDS MBA PhD FCAHS Dean and Professor College of Dentistry, 107 Wiggins Road University of Saskatchewan Saskatoon, Saskatchewan   S7N 5E5 Telephone: (306) 966-5121         Fax: (306) 966-5132        Email: dentistrysearch@usask.ca Review of applications will begin immediately; however, applications will be accepted and evaluated until the position is filled. The University of Saskatchewan’s main campus is situated on Treaty 6 Territory and the Homeland of the Métis. The University of Saskatchewan is located in Saskatoon, Saskatchewan, a city with a diverse and thriving economic base, a vibrant arts community and a full range of leisure opportunities. The University has a reputation for excellence in teaching, research and scholarly activities and offers a full range of undergraduate, graduate, and professional programs. Due to federal immigration requirements, we also ask candidates to indicate whether they are Canadian citizens, permanent residents, or are otherwise already authorized to work at this position for the duration of the appointment, with an explanation if this last category is indicated. Status: Tenure Track Employment Group: USFA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 4/26/2027 The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact Human Resources for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/14703?c=usask": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Faculty Member, Pediatric Dentistry",
      "description": "Tenure Track Faculty Member, Pediatric Dentistry | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Tenure Track Faculty Member, Pediatric Dentistry Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req14703 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/26/2026 Back to Search Tenure Track Faculty Member, Pediatric Dentistry The College of Dentistry invites applications from qualified individuals for a full-time tenure-track faculty position at the academic rank of Assistant Professor in Pediatric Dentistry. Responsibilities include delivering didactic, pre-clinical, and clinical teaching, supervising student research projects; initiating, leading, and participating in innovative research programs; competing successfully for internal and external funding; and undertaking relevant administrative activities. Applicants must have a DDS or DMD (or equivalent) degree and specialty training in Pediatric Dentistry. A strong preference will be given to candidates who hold a PhD or MSc degree with a solid emerging record of research and/or academic accomplishments. Research expertise in oral microbiology and oral biology is preferred for this position. Postdoctoral training is also considered an asset. Desirable attributes include effective classroom teaching skills, strong mentoring abilities, and excellent interpersonal communication skills. Salary information for this position for the 2026-2027 academic year is as follows: Assistant Professor: $110,489 to $131,909. Independent of rank, a starting salary above the standard salary bands is usually common and possible in the College of Dentistry. This position includes a comprehensive benefits package which includes a dental, health and extended vision care plan; pension plan, life insurance (compulsory and voluntary), academic long-term disability, sick leave, travel insurance, death benefits, an employee assistance program, a professional expense allowance, and a flexible health and wellness spending program. This tenure-track position is supported by start-up funds designed to enable early success in research and scholarship. Start-up support may include resources for establishing research infrastructure, initiating pilot studies, and advancing scholarly activities aligned with the College’s strategic research priorities. The level and structure of start-up funding will be commensurate with the candidate’s experience, research program, and anticipated funding trajectory. Interested candidates must submit, via email, 1) a cover letter; 2) a detailed curriculum vitae; 3) three letters of reference 4) a detailed 5-year research plan focusing on grant applications, including research ambitions, research outcomes and pitfalls (maximum 8 pages), and 5) a teaching philosophy statement to: Dr. Walter Siqueira DDS MBA PhD FCAHS Dean and Professor College of Dentistry, 107 Wiggins Road University of Saskatchewan Saskatoon, Saskatchewan   S7N 5E5 Telephone: (306) 966-5121 Fax: (306) 966-5132 Email: dentistrysearch@usask.ca Review of applications will begin immediately; however, applications will be accepted and evaluated until the position is filled. The University of Saskatchewan’s main campus is situated on Treaty 6 Territory and the Homeland of the Métis. The University of Saskatchewan is located in Saskatoon, Saskatchewan, a city with a diverse and thriving economic base, a vibrant arts community and a full range of leisure opportunities. The University has a reputation for excellence in teaching, research and scholarly activities and offers a full range of undergraduate, graduate, and professional programs. Due to federal immigration requirements, we also ask candidates to indicate whether they are Canadian citizens, permanent residents, or are otherwise already authorized to work at this position for the duration of the appointment, with an explanation if this last category is indicated. Status: Tenure Track Employment Group: USFA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Posted Date: 10/26/2026 Closing Date: 4/26/2027 The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact Human Resources for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
