    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15695?c=usask": {
      "title": "Sessional Lecturer, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy",
      "description": "Sessional Lecturer, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Sessional Lecturer, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15695 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Sessional Lecturer, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy The Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy invites applications for Sessional Lecturers to teach the following courses during the 2026-27 Regular Session, Winter Term: Course Number, Section, and Name: JSGS 802.3 Public Finance Term and Course Dates: January 6 to April 8, 2027 CRN: 31683 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Wednesday, 5:00-8:00 PM Expected Enrollment Limit: 25-30 Location: Saskatoon Qualifications: Must have at least a Master’s degree but preferably a PhD in public administration, public policy, political science, or a related field. Experience teaching in a post-secondary setting, ideally at the graduate level. Working experience in the public service will be considered a strong asset. Course Number, Section, and Name: JSGS 806.3 Public Policy Analysis Term and Course Dates: January 6 to April 8, 2027. CRN: 31687 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Thursdays, 5:00-8:00 PM Expected Enrollment Limit: 25-30 Location: Saskatoon Qualifications: Minimum of a Master’s degree but PhD preferred in public administration, public policy, political science, or a related discipline. Experience in a senior leadership position is required. Previous teaching experience, particularly at the graduate level, would be considered an asset. Course Number, Section, and Name: JSGS 808.3 Ethical Leadership and Democracy Term and Course Dates: January 6 to April 8, 2027. CRN: 31690 Delivery Mode: Lecture Course Schedule: Monday, 5:00-8:00 PM Expected Enrollment Limit: 25-30 Location: Saskatoon Qualifications: Minimum of a Master’s degree but preferably a PhD in economics, public policy, public administration, or a related discipline is required. Experience teaching in a post-secondary setting, ideally at the graduate level. Working experience in the public service will be considered a strong asset. The appointment period includes additional days in recognition of preparation and marking time required. Courses may be cancelled due to low enrolment. Application Process: Complete the CUPE 3287 Employment Application Form . Submit as a single PDF file the application form along with your curriculum vitae, a cover letter, your teaching philosophy, and evidence of teaching effectiveness no later than November 3, 2026, to jsgs‑hr@usask.ca . The subject line must indicate the course name for which you are applying. If you are currently employed by the University of Saskatchewan in a full-time non-teaching position, discuss your application with your people leader to ensure the appointment is suitable relative to your current position. Department: School of Public Policy Status: Term Employment Group: CUPE 3287 Academic Calendar: Course Dates and Academic Calendar Stipend: CUPE 3287 Stipends Posted Date: 10/21/2026 Closing Date: 11/3/2026 Number of Openings: 3 Work Location: Saskatoon #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15604?c=usask": {
      "title": "Exam Office Administrator, Access and Equity Services",
      "description": "Exam Office Administrator, Access and Equity Services | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Exam Office Administrator, Access and Equity Services Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15604 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Exam Office Administrator, Access and Equity Services Summary: The exam office administrator provides front-line administrative support and oversees the daily operations of the department’s general office. This role coordinates front-office functions, supports students, staff, and instructors, and contributes to the delivery of exam accommodations. This position supervises and guides front-office staff, helping maintain consistent service delivery and adherence to university and department policies and procedures. Additionally, the administrator coordinates responses to inquiries, supports exam logistics, and completes operational processes related to payroll, human resources, and technology. The role also addresses complex and unexpected administrative situations using knowledge of disability-related needs, human rights legislation, and institutional policies. Duties and Responsibilities: Provides daily supervision and coordination of front-office staff activities. Oversees front-office operations and adjusts workflow based on program demands and student needs. Provides guidance and mentorship to clerical staff in task prioritization and issue resolution. Responds to and triages inquiries via multiple channels. Completes exam administration processes using the accommodation system, including data entry and reporting. Coordinates delivery and pickup of completed exams and supports exam logistics during peak periods. Supports exam program staff by providing guidance and reinforcing adherence to policies. Facilitates payroll and human resources processes, such as reviewing submissions and approving timesheets. Troubleshoots electronic exam issues and provides basic information technology support and training. Monitors facility and space-related concerns and submits maintenance or service requests. Performs general office administration tasks, such as filing, printing, sorting, and secure document handling. Assists invigilators with academic integrity concerns in accordance with university policies. Responds to emergency situations in alignment with established protocols and documents incidents. Communicates and acts in a respectful and professional manner, collaborates effectively, abides by policy, and contributes to a positive and productive work and learning environment. Engages in continuous learning by participating in activities, training, and events related to Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Demonstrates respect and dignity for all members of the community, actively contributes to an environment of acceptance and inclusion of others, and responds to different perspectives from a place of curiosity, respect, and non-judgement. Undertakes related duties, as assigned, consistent with the nature of the position. Qualifications: An office administration diploma. Minimum of one year of related experience working with individuals with disabilities, preferably in a post-secondary environment. Experience providing front-line guidance and mentorship. Strong knowledge of human rights legislation and protected grounds. Tact and discretion in working in sensitive situations involving a diverse range of individuals. Strong problem-solving, critical thinking, and decision-making skills. Effective organizational, prioritization, and multitasking abilities. High level of integrity in handling confidential information. Ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively in a team. Familiarity with university academic structures, policies, and systems is preferred. Knowledge of university exam-related policies and processes is an asset. Proficient computer skills, including Microsoft Office applications, with the ability to learn new technologies; experience with university systems is preferred. The University of Saskatchewan recognizes alternate career paths; therefore, a combination of education and experience may be considered. Department: Access and Equity Services Status: Permanent Employment Group: CUPE 1975 Shift: Mon-Fri, 8:00-4:00pm Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Salary: The salary range, based on 1.0 FTE, is $24.74 - 31.37 per hour. The starting salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Salary Family (if applicable): Operational Services Salary Phase/Band: Phase 4 Posted Date: 10/21/2026 Closing Date: 11/4/2026 at 6:00 pm CST Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15715?c=usask": {
      "title": "Term Professorship, Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics",
      "description": "Term Professorship, Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Term Professorship, Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15715 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/20/2026 Back to Search Term Professorship, Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics Formal applications are invited from qualified individuals for the Hadley Van Vliet Term Professorship in Agricultural Economics in the Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics at the University of Saskatchewan in Canada . The position has an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027, or soon thereafter. This is a term position with a mutually agreed upon duration of between 1 to 5 years. The position includes generous research support to help the successful candidate develop and advance their research program. The successful candidate will have a Ph.D. in Agricultural Economics or Economics or closely related field and demonstrated potential for research excellence in areas of importance for Saskatchewan agriculture. The specific research area is open, but candidates who most closely match the objectives of the Hadley Van Vliet Professorship are preferred. These objectives are: To analyze and address pressing issues in agriculture, particularly those relevant to Saskatchewan agriculture; To engage with students and colleagues through the ideas, understanding and analysis that can be applied to major issues in agricultural economics; To engage and communicate research findings to agriculture stakeholders; and To work with public institutions and their agencies in seeking solutions to agricultural and rural issues. The successful candidate will: Develop an innovative research program in agricultural and/or international economics. Teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students, strengthening their analytical skills and understanding of the agricultural economy. Communicate effectively with a wide range of industry stakeholders. Rigorously analyze agricultural issues and communicate findings to diverse audiences. Along with high-quality research, the successful candidate will deliver up to 2 courses per year in either undergraduate or graduate instruction in areas that align with candidate qualifications and department needs (the specific courses will be determined at the time of appointment). The successful candidate will provide leadership and mentorship to both graduate and undergraduate students and is also expected to engage with relevant agricultural industry stakeholders. We welcome applications from scholars at all career stages. Past holders of the Hadley Van Vliet Professorship have included both recent PhD graduates and established academics. The Department is committed to supporting the professional and scholarly development of the successful candidate, including providing strong mentorship to early-career scholars. Past early-career holders of the Professorship have gone on to successful tenure-track academic careers. The Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics has a long history of research excellence in agricultural economics, policy and agribusiness. The department offers both a Ph.D. and M.Sc. in agricultural economics, as well as undergraduate programs in agribusiness, agricultural economics, and renewable resource management. The department is situated within the College of Agriculture and Bioresources, recognized as an international leader in applied research and scholarship with expertise in, and integrated across, four primary themes: Food, Feed and Bioproducts; Environment and Ecology; Business and Applied Economics; Plants and Animals. The college has a century-long reputation for teaching, research, and outreach and has been a consistent strength both for the university and the region. The college continues to play a pivotal role in the development of agricultural and food industries in Saskatchewan, building on a legacy of public engagement and cooperative partnerships. Our departmental faculty are committed to providing exceptional learning experiences and preparing graduates for leadership roles in the global bio-economy. The University of Saskatchewan is situated on Treaty 6 Territory and the Homeland of the Métis. The main campus is in Saskatoon, Saskatchewan, a fast-growing metropolitan area with a diverse and thriving economic base, supporting a vibrant arts community and a full range of leisure opportunities. Overall, the University has a long-standing reputation for excellence in teaching, research and scholarly activities, offering a full range of undergraduate, graduate, and professional programs to a diverse student population of over 25,000. Salary bands for this position for the 2026 – 2027 academic year are as follows: Assistant Professor: $113,527 to $135,535; Associate Professor: $135,535 to $157,543; Full Professor: $157,543 to $183,219. Additional research funding for the Professorship is included to assist the appointee in developing and continuing a productive research program. This position is supported by a comprehensive benefits package which includes a dental, health and extended vision care plan; pension plan, life insurance (compulsory and voluntary), academic long-term disability, sick leave, travel insurance, death benefits, an employee assistance program, a professional expense allowance, and a flexible health and wellness spending program. APPLICATION PROCEDURE Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026. A letter of application that outlines how your research aligns with the objectives of the Hadley Van Vliet Professorship, CV (including a list of publications and exposition of teaching experience), a job market paper or sample of recent research, and letters from three external referees should be submitted electronically through the Econ Job Market site: https://econjobmarket.org/positions/12865 *Due to federal immigration requirements, we also ask candidates to indicate whether they are Canadian citizens, permanent residents, or are otherwise already authorized to work in this position for the duration of the appointment, with an explanation if this last category is indicated. The University is committed to employment equity. Members of Designated Groups (women, indigenous people, people with disabilities and visible minorities) are encouraged to self-identify on their applications. Enquiries about the position should be directed to: Dr. Eric Micheels, Associate Professor and Department Head, Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics, University of Saskatchewan, Canada; eric.micheels@usask.ca. Relevant websites: Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics University of Saskatchewan City of Saskatoon Status: Term Employment Group: USFA Full Time Equivalent (FTE): 1.0 Posted Date: 10/20/2026 Closing Date: 12/1/2026 The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
    },
    "https://usask.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/14/home/requisition/15713?c=usask": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy",
      "description": "Research Assistant, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy | Job Details University of Saskatchewan Careers P A WS Current Opportunities Benefits Relocating Tips for Applying Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Assistant, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy Saskatoon, SK, Canada | req15713 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/20/2026 Back to Search Research Assistant, Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy The Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy (JSGS) is recruiting a Graduate Research Assistant to work with and report to Dr. Daniel Dickson, Assistant Professor at JSGS. The typical duties for the Research Assistant will be: conducting literature review on disability housing policy in Canada; assisting with archival research; analyzing basic descriptive data (no stats training required); working in partnership with disability advocacy organizations and self-advocates; attending/co-facilitating regular research meetings; and preparing research notes. The general location of work will be the Diefenbaker Building where JSGS is located. Required Number of Hours: 10 hours per week Duration of Employment: November 30, 2026 to March 26, 2026 (15 weeks) Qualifications (Skills and Abilities) Minimum of a bachelor’s degree or equivalent in a public policy-relevant social science discipline (i.e., political science, sociology, social work, disability studies, etc.). Preference will be given to applicants currently registered in a graduate program at the University of Saskatchewan. Exceptional oral and written communications skills Excellent critical thinking skills Excellent research skills Ability to work well with others Ability to work independently, engage in planning, follow timelines, and meet deadlines To Apply Applicants must include the following in a single PDF file : cover letter, CV/resume, and two references. In the cover letter, please make explicit mention of any research projects in which you have been involved. Applications must be sent via email to jsgs-hr@usask.ca . The subject line should read: “Application – RA for Professor Dickson”. Where a graduate student from the University of Saskatchewan is appointed to this position, it will be in scope of PSAC. Department: School of Public Policy Status: Term Employment Group: Graduate Students - PSAC Rate of Pay: Current rates of pay Posted Date: 10/20/2026 Closing Date: 11/10/2026 Number of Openings: 1 Work Location: On Campus #LI-DNI The University of Saskatchewan aspires to be what the world needs and embraces equity, diversity and inclusion as foundational to excellence and innovation. We actively seek to create a welcoming environment where all individuals feel empowered to thrive, contribute, and grow. Applications from equity-deserving groups are encouraged as part of our ongoing efforts to reflect the diversity of the communities we serve: EDI Framework for Action . We continue to grow our partnerships with Indigenous communities across the province, nationally, and internationally and value the unique perspective that Indigenous employees provide to strengthen these relationships. Verification of Indigenous Membership/Citizenship at the University of Saskatchewan is led and determined by the deybwewin | taapwaywin | tapwewin: Indigenous Truth policy and the Standing Committee in accordance with the processes developed to enact the policy. Successful candidates that assert Indigenous membership/citizenship will be asked to complete the verification process of Indigenous membership/citizenship with documentation. The University of Saskatchewan provides an accessible and inclusive workplace. Should you require support through any stage of the recruitment process, please contact us for assistance. Apply Now Share Save Job Faculty/Staff Login Follow on LinkedIn University of Saskatchewan © University of Saskatchewan Disclaimer | Privacy"
